hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"6601","895 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated in what the reciters differed in their reciting of His saying: ""It is not for a prophet to embezzle"" or ""to be embezzled from,"" and in the cause in which it came down. 5601 - Abu Umayyah Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Amr al-Azdi narrated to us, Abu Ishaq al-Fazari narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Said, from Khusayf, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas who said: They missed a red velvet cloth of what was taken from the polytheists on the day of Badr, so they said: Perhaps the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took it. Then came down: ""It is not for a prophet to embezzle, and whoever embezzles shall bring what he embezzled on the Day of Resurrection."" Khusayf said: I said to Ikrimah: Said recites ""to be embezzled from."" He said: Yes, and to be killed."
"6602","5602 - And Ismail ibn Ishaq ibn Sahl al-Kufi narrated to us, Abu Nuaym al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us, from Khusayf who said: Miqsam informed me, from Ibn Abbas, then he mentioned the like of it, except that he did not mention in it: I said to him: Said recites ""to be embezzled from,"" to the end of the hadith. Sufyan and Abd al-Salam differed over who is between Khusayf and Ibn Abbas in the chain of this hadith; Sufyan mentioned that it is Ikrimah, and Abd al-Salam mentioned that it is Miqsam. In both their narrations is ""embezzle,"" not ""be embezzled from."""
"6603","And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Qays ibn Sad, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas who said: He used to recite ""It is not for a prophet to embezzle."""
"6604","And Ahmad ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, Khalaf ibn Hisham narrated to us, from al-Khaffaf, from Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, and from Hanzalah, from Shahr, from Ibn Abbas: ""to be embezzled from."" Of those to whom Ibn Abbas's this reading returned, of the reciters after him around whom recitation revolved, of Asim ibn Abi al-Najud and Abu Amr ibn al-Ala, we know no reciter who recited it likewise but them. As for others than them of them, al-Amash."
"6605","As Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, Khalaf narrated to us, he recited it ""to be embezzled from"" with raising the ya; Hamzah likewise, and Nafi likewise. Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz related to us, from Abu Ubayd in the Quran entirely, likewise; he added, in one who recited ""be embezzled from,"" saying: Likewise recited Abu Jafar, Shaybah and al-Kisai. Then he said: Abu Ubayd said, of the first reading, reciting ""embezzle,"" because of what was narrated in it from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, of his saying: How can he not be embezzled from when he may be killed? And because the Arabs also say to a man when he does what doing is not: it was not for him to do; and when there is done to him what should not be done: it was not for them to do that. He said: This is the import of the speech; the other too is permissible, not impossible. A speaker said: In this hadith, in the cause of the velvet cloth mentioned in it, is what contradicts what you narrated in the chapter before this chapter, in it of what Quraysh mentioned the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with, of trustworthiness and truthfulness of speech. Our answer to him regarding that is that what we mentioned before this chapter, Quraysh mentioned the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with trustworthiness and truthfulness of speech, as we mentioned in it. What we mentioned in this chapter is only of what was said of him in Medina, in which the surah containing this verse, namely Al Imran, came down in Medina. The sayers of this saying were those who were hypocritical to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying of him the like of this saying and its likes. Fighting had not come down in Mecca; it only came down in Medina, and with it was hypocrisy. Those whose sayings about the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) are proof against them are the People of the Book in Mecca; his truthfulness was with them, despite their opposing him with that. As for those with him in Medina of those who pledged him while concealing to him other than what they showed him, they are not of those whose sayings about him are adduced, for they are neither of his townspeople nor of the people of experience of him. And Allah is the Granter of success."
"6606","896 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his saying to his wives after the Farewell Pilgrimage: ""This is the pilgrimage, then staying upon the mats."" 5603 - al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from Salih, freedman of al-Tawamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said to his wives: ""This is the pilgrimage, then upon you is staying upon the mats."" They used to perform pilgrimage except Zaynab bint Jahsh and Sawdah bint Zamah, saying: No mount shall move us after we heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6607","5604 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud and Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us; they said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Waqid ibn Abi Waqid al-Laythi, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his wives in the Farewell Pilgrimage: ""This is the pilgrimage of Islam, then staying upon the mats."" A speaker said: In these two hadiths is of the Messenger of Allah's saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to his wives after they performed the Farewell Pilgrimage with him, yet they, except Zaynab and Sawdah, performed pilgrimage with his Rightly-Guided caliphs and his Companions besides them, with no denial of them of that upon them, and no preventing of them of them likewise, except Zaynab and Sawdah, for they held back from that, arguing against those contrary to them of them by what was of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Our answer to him regarding that is that what is in these two hadiths was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in them, and it is what was binding upon them, abandoning going out of it to other than it, until there was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying to Aishah when she asked him to strive with him, meaning herself and others than her of his wives and of others than them."
"6608","5605 - As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Raja al-Ghudani narrated to us, Ubaydah narrated to us, from Muawiyah ibn Ishaq, from his paternal aunt Aishah bint Talhah, from her maternal aunt Aishah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The striving of women is pilgrimage of this House."" Some people of knowledge claimed the Ubaydah mentioned in this hadith's chain is Ubaydah ibn Humayd; others than them of them claim he is Ubaydah ibn Abi Raitah."
"6609","5606 - And as al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Zayd ibn Abi al-Zarqa narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Muawiyah ibn Ishaq, from Aishah bint Talhah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: We asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about striving, and sought his permission in that. He said: ""Your striving, or your confinement, is pilgrimage."""
"6610","5607 - And as Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, Sawwar narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Muawiyah ibn Ishaq, from Aishah bint Talhah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: We asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about striving. He said: ""Your confinement is pilgrimage,"" or ""your striving."""
"6611","5608 - And as Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Amrah, from Aishah bint Talhah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: I said: O Prophet of Allah, shall we not go out striving with you, for I see no deed in the Quran better than it? He said: No, but the best and most complete striving is pilgrimage of the House, an accepted pilgrimage."
"6612","5609 - And as al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Yazid ibn Ata narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Amrah, from Aishah daughter of Talhah, from Aishah, Mother of the Believers, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Shall we not go out striving with you? He said: No, your striving is the accepted pilgrimage; it is striving for you. The Messenger of Allah's answer (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in her seeking his permission for her and others than her to go out with him in striving was what he mentioned of his answering her in this hadith. That was proof that their striving does not cease, as the striving of men does not cease. It is possible that was after his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to her and to the rest of his wives besides her what he said to them in the first two hadiths. She stood upon that, she and others than her of his wives, upon that, to the exclusion of one who did not stand upon it. Neither Zaynab nor Sawdah stood upon that, so they adhered to what is in the first two hadiths. All of them, Allah's pleasure upon them all, are praiseworthy upon what is mentioned of that. The caliphs of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Allah's pleasure upon his Companions, and the rest of the Companions, in their leaving disagreement upon them in that and their permitting it to them, are praiseworthy, by their knowledge of what they knew of that. The interpretation of these hadiths must be borne only upon what we bore them upon, for in that is safety and good opinion of the caliphs of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his wives and his Companions. In other than it is the opposite of that, of what we seek refuge in Allah from. A claimant claimed that Aishah's abandoning shortening prayer in her journeys after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was only because of his saying to them in the first two hadiths. He attached to something in that narrated in it by Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad."
"6613","5610 - As Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Jabalah ibn Abi Rawwad, from his uncle, who said to al-Qasim ibn Muhammad: What is it with Aishah that she used to complete in travel? He said: Because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This, then staying upon the mats. This interpretation with us is corrupt, since Aishah was more knowledgeable of Allah Almighty and His rulings than to do this act, meaning travel, in opposition of her to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then abandon for its sake shortening prayer in her travels. She did not consider shortening obligatory upon anyone, so she did not shorten for that. As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, Sharik and Ali ibn Mushir informed us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her: that she used not to shorten in travel, nor consider it obligatory upon anyone. Her abandoning shortening in travel was for that, not for other than it, and Allah knows best."
"6614","897 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his saying to his wives: ""Which of you is the owner of the shaggy camel, going out at whom the dogs of al-Hawb bark, many slain killed on her right and left, then she escapes after she almost perished."" And of his saying to Ali: ""There shall be between you and Aishah something; when that is, convey her to her safety."" 5611 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Isam ibn Qudamah narrated to us, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his wives: ""Which of you is the owner of the shaggy camel, going out at whom the dogs of al-Hawb bark, many slain killed on her right and left, then she escapes after she almost perished."" Abu Jafar said: Thus the people of hadith say in this cited place of the dogs' barking: al-Hawb with dammah; as for the people of Arabic, they all say with fathah, reciting in that."
"6615","5612 - And he mentioned what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, al-Muqaddami narrated to us, al-Fudayl ibn Sulayman al-Numayri narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Yahya narrated to us, from Abu Asma, from Abu Jafar, from Abu Rafi: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ali: ""There shall be between you and Aishah something."" He said: I, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Yes. He said: I, of all my companions?! He said: Yes. He said: Then I am the most wretched of them, O Messenger of Allah. He said: No; when that is, convey her to her safety. Thus Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us this hadith, saying in it: from Abu Asma, from Abu Jafar, from Abu Rafi."
"6616","5613 - And likewise Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, al-Husayn ibn Muhammad al-Marwazi narrated to us, al-Fudayl ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sulayman ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Abu Asma, freedman of Abu Jafar, from Abu Rafi: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ali: ""There shall be between you and Aishah a matter."" Then he mentioned the rest of the hadith. He said: In this hadith is that the one of whom that shall be, upon the tongue of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), is Aishah. This is severe contradiction. Our answer to him regarding that is that there is no contradiction in that as he imagined. With us, and Allah knows best, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his wives what Ibn Abbas narrated from him of what we mentioned, after Allah Almighty informed him that of his wives is one of whom that shall be, without His informing him which of them she is. Then He informed him which of them she is after that, so he addressed Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, with what he addressed him of that in Abu Rafi's hadith. It has become clear, by Allah's praise and aid, that there is no contradiction in anything of what we mentioned in this chapter of these two matters. And success is from Allah."
"6617","898 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from Ibn Masud, of his saying when the tashahhud was obligated, meaning the tashahhud in prayer. 5614 - Said ibn Abd al-Rahman informed us, from Abu Ubaydullah al-Makhzumi, Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Amash and Mansur, from Shaqiq ibn Salamah, from Ibn Masud who said: We used to say before the tashahhud was obligated: Peace upon Jibril and Mikail. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Do not say so, for Allah is Peace. Rather say: Greetings to Allah, prayers and good words. Peace upon you, O Prophet, and Allah's mercy and blessings. Peace upon us and upon Allah's righteous servants. I testify there is no god but Allah, and I testify Muhammad is His servant and Messenger."" We know no one who narrated this hadith mentioning in it ""when the tashahhud was obligated"" but Ibn Uyaynah. Others than him narrated it, all not mentioning this word in it. A questioner asked of the meaning of obligation in this: is it like the obligation of prayer, whose denier is a disbeliever? Our answer to him regarding that is that obligation may be upon the meaning he mentioned, of obligation of things binding, obligating those obligated to exit them, like prayers and their likes. Of it is Allah Almighty's saying: ""Alms are only for the poor, the needy, those employed upon them,"" then He mentioned its people, who they are, then followed that with His saying: ""An obligation from Allah, and Allah is Knowing, Wise."" It may be upon other than that, of informing people of things obligated upon them in what is mentioned thereby of lawful and unlawful."
"6618","As Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, al-Firyabi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: ""A surah We sent down and obligated it,"" he said: Command of lawful, and prohibition of unlawful."
"6619","And as Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, al-Firyabi narrated to us, Warqa narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, the like of it."
"6620","And as Wallad al-Nahwi narrated to us, he said: al-Masadiri narrated to us, from Abu Ubaydah: ""A surah We sent down and obligated it,"" he said: We sent down in it different obligations, and things We obligated upon you and upon those after you to the Day of Resurrection. He said: Emphasis, meaning in ""obligated it,"" in this is better. Abu Jafar said: The obligation of this description may be an obligation of choice, as was narrated from Ibn Umar: The Messenger of Allah obligated Zakat al-Fitr, mentioning in that what he mentioned in it. That obligation was not like the obligation of circumambulation, nor like the obligation of zakats, for one who denies what is in this hadith is not a disbeliever, as one who denies those things is a disbeliever. Like the obligation we mentioned, obligation: a thing may be mentioned with an obligation which may not be left, which if one denies it, by his denying it he is a disbeliever. Like the obligation we mentioned, it may be mentioned upon obligation of choice. Of it is what was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: ""Friday washing is obligatory upon every pubescent one."" That was upon obligation of choice. Obligation may be upon giving, not upon other than it. Of it is Allah Exalted's saying: ""Indeed He who obligated the Quran upon you shall surely return you to the return."" The obligation in this is giving."
"6621","As Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, from Isa ibn Maymun, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid: ""Indeed He who obligated the Quran upon you shall surely return you to the return,"" he said: Indeed He who gave you, meaning the Quran, shall surely return you to the return, meaning to Mecca. The meaning of obligation in this is giving. It is possible the obligation of the tashahhud is the giving from Allah Almighty to them of the tashahhud, in which is their testifying to Him Almighty of unity, then to His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of messengership, to establish them upon what He wills to establish them upon. There is not established in Ibn Masud's hadith an obligation of duty going beyond what the people of knowledge say in that. It was in prayer, though in it besides the Quran, like the opening of it, glorification in its bowing and prostration. Since those things, though complete in themselves, are not obligated, the tashahhud is like them."
"6622","899 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his leaving rebuke upon one addressing him: ""May Allah make me your ransom."" 5615 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, Uthman al-Shahham narrated to us, Muslim ibn Abi Bakrah narrated to us, from Abu Bakrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: ""There shall be tribulations, then tribulation. The walking in it is better than the running to it; the sitting in it is better than the standing; the lying in it is better than the sitting. When it descends, whoever has sheep, let him join his sheep; whoever has land, let him join his land; whoever has camels, let him join his camels."" A man of the people said: O Prophet of Allah, may Allah make me your ransom, what of one who has neither sheep, camels nor land, what does he do? He said: ""Let him take his sword, head with it to a rock, strike its edge with a stone, then escape if he can escape. O Allah, have I conveyed?"" A man said: O Prophet of Allah, may Allah make me your ransom, what if I am taken by my hand coerced till I am brought to one of the two rows, or one of the two factions, Uthman doubts, and a man throws me with his sword and kills me, what shall be of my affair? He said: ""He bears your sin and his sin, and shall be of the people of the Fire."""
"6623","5616 - And Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, Abu Qazaah informed me, that Abu Nadrah and Hasan informed him: that Abu Said al-Khudri informed them: that the delegation of Abd al-Qays when they came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Prophet of Allah, may Allah make us your ransom, what is wholesome for us of drinks? He said: ""Do not drink in naqir."" They said: O Prophet of Allah, may Allah make us your ransom, do you know what naqir is? He said: ""Yes, the trunk hollowed in its middle,"" nor in dubba, nor in hantam."
"6624","5617 - And Bakkar ibn Qutaybah and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us; they said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Hammad ibn Salamah who said: Yala ibn Ata informed me, from Abdullah ibn Yasar, surnamed Abu Hammam, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Fihri who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while in a tent, and said: Peace upon you, O Messenger of Allah, and Allah's mercy; departure time has come, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Yes. Then he said: O Bilal. Bilal sprang from under an acacia like his shade was a bird's shade, and said: At your service, at your happiness, and I am your ransom. He said: Saddle my horse for me. Then he mentioned the rest of the hadith. A speaker said: How do you accept this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while it is, as he said it, beyond ability, not answered to, as he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Umm Habibah when she said: O Allah, grant me enjoyment of my husband, the Messenger of Allah, my father Abu Sufyan, and my brother Muawiyah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""You asked for fixed terms, apportioned provisions, and reached traces; nothing of it hastened which He deferred, nor deferred of it anything He hastened."" We mentioned this chapter with its chains earlier in this book of ours, namely what was narrated of lizards, that Allah Almighty did not destroy a people making for them progeny or offspring, and that the transformed was before that. Our answer to him regarding that is that a questioner asking this of his Lord asked Him something he knows he shall not be answered to. The addressed with that also knows of that the like of what his addresser knows of it. But he said a saying he wished to be as he said. That, though of what nothing reaches him being a cause for the love of the منزل for him, its sayer for him, for he said to him: Had it reached him, and passed upon him, he would be given that. That was not disliked of its sayer, nor did he rebuke him for that, nor was it blameworthy upon him. The one said to stood thereby of his sayer upon his love for him and his place in his heart. He, upon him be peace, commanded Muslims to be brothers; of their brotherhood is love of one another. That saying confirms brotherhood among them and love of one another. Like it is what we found them supplicating one for another, of remaining, increase in age and extension in term, for this meaning in it, of casting love in the hearts of one another, and other than that. Likewise said Muhammad ibn Sirin."
"6625","As Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, Muadh ibn Muadh al-Anbari narrated to us, from Ibn Sirin who said: Muslims know they have no supplication in the term. And success is from Allah."
"6626","900 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of the Messenger of Allah's act (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for one for whom he ransomed his father and mother. 5618 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, from Shubah, from Sad ibn Ibrahim who said: I heard Abdullah ibn Shaddad ibn al-Had saying: I heard Ali saying: I never heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) joining for anyone the ransom of his parents but Sad ibn Malik. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Uhud kept saying to him: ""Shoot, may my father and mother be your ransom."""
"6627","5619 - And Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala and Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us; they said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, that he said to his father: O father, I saw you while you charge upon your blond horse. He said: Ho, did you see me, my son? He said: Yes. He said: The Messenger of Allah at that time joined for your father his parents, saying: Charge, may my father and mother be your ransom."
"6628","5620 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to us, from his father, from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr who said: I and Umar ibn Abi Salamah were on the day of the Trench in the fortress. He would stoop for me and I would stoop for him, and we would look at the fighting. I saw my father in those days roaming in the salt-flat, charging upon these once and upon these once. I said: I saw you roaming in the salt-flat, charging upon these once and upon these once. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) today joined for me his parents."
"6629","5621 - And Yunus narrated to us, Anas ibn Iyad informed us, from Yahya ibn Said who said: I heard Said ibn al-Musayyib saying: I heard Ibn Abi Waqqas saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) joined for me his parents on the day of Uhud."
"6630","5622 - And Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Said informed us, Ubaydullah ibn Musa informed us, from Ali ibn Salih, from Asim, from Zirr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like, meaning of the hadith which Zirr did not go beyond. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was prostrating, and al-Hasan and al-Husayn began riding upon his back. He said: When he finished, he said: ""By my father, you two, and my mother; whoever loves me, let him love these two."" What we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter indicates what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended for one who said to him these mentioned sayings in these reports. It is: had I been able to make my father and mother ransom for one for whom I made them ransom, I would have done. That reaches of his heart the utmost what the like of him reaches of him. One who said that to him knows of him that he is of his heart in the utmost what is of the like of him of the heart of his like. And Allah is the Granter of success."
"6631","901 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his saying to people after prayer was established: ""Straighten your rows and press together, for I surely see you from behind my back."" 5623 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, Humayd al-Tawil narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik who said: The Messenger of Allah came to us after prayer was established, before he said takbir, and said: ""Establish your rows and press together, for I surely see you from behind my back."" A speaker said: How do you accept the like of this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when you narrated from him in Abu Bakrah's hadith his saying: When he finished his prayer, while Abu Bakrah had come running while he was in it, breathless, bowing before the row: ""Which of you bowed before the row?"" Abu Bakrah said: I said, I. He said: ""May Allah increase you in eagerness, and do not repeat."" This indicated he had behind him what he did not see until he inquired of it from another. You also narrated, mentioning."
"6632","5624 - What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Bakr narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood to prayer, then a man came after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood to prayer, hastening his walk, reaching the people breathless. When he reached the row he said: Praise be to Allah, abundant, good, blessed praise. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) completed his prayer, he said: ""Who is the speaker, or sayer of the words?"" The people fell silent, so he said the like of it. He said: ""Who is he? For he said no harm,"" or he said: ""but good."" The man said: I came, O Messenger of Allah, hastening my walk, out of breath, or breathless, and said what I said. He said: ""I saw twelve angels racing to it, which of them raises it."" Then he said: ""When one of you comes to prayer, let him walk with calm, pray what he catches, and make up what he was preceded in."" He said: In this too is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not know who the sayer of these words was until he inquired of it from another. This is severe contradiction. Our answer to him regarding that is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not say in the hadith we began mentioning in this chapter: I see you from behind my back with my eye. Seeing may be with the eye, and may be with knowledge. Of that is His saying Exalted: ""You used to wish for death before you met it; you have seen it while you look on,"" meaning: you knew it, though you did not witness it with your eyes. Of that is what He related of His servant and Prophet Shuayb, upon him be peace, of his saying to his people: ""I see you in good,"" while Shuayb was blind. That was for him a seeing of knowledge. That indicates seeing may be with the eye, and seeing may be a seeing of knowledge. His saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): ""I see you from behind my back,"" means: for what Allah casts into his heart of what they are upon in their prayers, of humility in them and other than it of what they are upon in them behind him. It has become clear, by Allah's praise, that there is no contradiction in anything of what this imaginer imagined as contradiction in the reports of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6633","902 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from Bilal, may Allah be pleased with him, of his stipulating upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) not to precede him with ""Amen."" 5625 - al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb al-Azdi narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Sulayman al-Dari narrated to us, from Asim, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Bilal who said: I stipulated upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) not to precede me with ""Amen."""
"6634","5626 - And al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb narrated to us, Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Asim, from Abu Uthman: that Bilal said: I stipulated upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) not to precede me with ""Amen."" What is in this hadith indicated that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite in his prayer after entering it a portion of the Opening of the Book before Bilal finished his iqamah. This indicates what Abu Hanifah used to hold regarding the imam: that he says takbir for prayer when the caller says ""prayer has been established,"" before he finishes his iqamah. This school was narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him."
"6635","As Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, Yahya ibn Yahya al-Naysaburi informed us, he said: I recited to Sharik, from Imran ibn Muslim, from Suwayd who said: Umar used to say takbir when the caller said: Prayer has been established. The like of that was also narrated from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, despite the many he met of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6636","As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Isa ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim narrated to us, Ismail ibn Abi Khalid narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn Abi Hazim, when the caller said: Prayer has been established, would say takbir and recite the Opening of the Book. Most people of knowledge besides Abu Hanifah and his companions, and others than them, held that the imam does not say takbir until the caller finishes his iqamah. They argue in that with Anas's hadith which we narrated in the chapter. With us Anas's that hadith was not contrary to this hadith of Bilal, for what is in Anas's hadith is only the acts of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions after prayer was established. His saying that saying may mean he intended them to do what he commanded them, while what he is upon before that and after it is upon what is in Bilal's hadith. They said: It was narrated from Anas and from al-Bara what indicates that what he was upon in his prayer was leaving takbir until the caller finishes his iqamah. They mentioned."
"6637","5627 - What Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Qasim al-Asadi narrated to us, from Abu Janab al-Kalbi, from Talhah ibn Musarrif, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awsajah, from al-Bara ibn Azib who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when prayer was established, would wipe our chests and say: ""Align shoulders with shoulders, feet with feet, for Allah Exalted loves in prayer what He loves in fighting, as if a firmly joined structure."""
"6638","5628 - And what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, from Musab ibn Thabit who said: We sought the knowledge of this wood in the imam's station in the Messenger of Allah's mosque (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and could find no one to tell us anything of it. Musab said: Muhammad ibn Muslim ibn al-Saib ibn Khabbab, keeper of the enclosure, informed me, he said: Anas ibn Malik sat to me one day and said: Do you know why this wood was made? We had not asked him of it. I said: No, by Allah. Anas said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would place his right, then turn to us and say: ""Straighten and righten your rows."" They said: This school was also narrated from Uthman. They mentioned:"
"6639","What Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from his uncle Abu Suhayl ibn Malik, from his father who said: I was with Uthman ibn Affan, then prayer was established while I spoke to him about allotting for me. I kept speaking to him while he leveled pebbles with his sandals, until men came to him he had entrusted with leveling rows, informing that rows were leveled. He said to me: Stand straight in the row, then said takbir. They said: In this is what indicated Uthman would not say takbir for prayer but after the caller's finishing the iqamah for it. This is of what nothing greater comes in it than what we brought in it, since the like of it is not reached to its reality. Since that is so, the better is that the matter be wide in it, and that what is known of him is other than one acting upon leaving its contrary. And success is from Allah."
"6640","903 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of Allah Almighty's saying: ""We wrote in the Zabur after the Reminder that the earth, My righteous servants shall inherit it,"" and of what is narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in it. Abu Jafar said: Allah Almighty said: ""We wrote in the Zabur after the Reminder that the earth, My righteous servants shall inherit it."" We reflected on the Reminder intended in this verse, finding more than one of the Followers said differing sayings in that. Of them is what was narrated from Said ibn Jubayr in that. As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath al-Nakhai narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from al-Amash who said: I asked Said ibn Jubayr of this verse: ""We wrote in the Zabur after the Reminder,"" he said: The Torah, Gospel and Criterion, after the Reminder in heaven: that the earth, the earth of Paradise, ""My righteous servants shall inherit it."""
"6641","And as Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, Shubah informed us, Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Said ibn Jubayr: ""We wrote in the Zabur after the Reminder,"" he said: The Torah, Gospel and Criterion, after the Reminder, the origin from which these books were copied: that the earth, the earth of Paradise, ""My righteous servants shall inherit it."" In this hadith was that the Reminder intended in this verse is the Reminder in heaven, and the Zabur mentioned in it is the Torah, Gospel and Criterion."
"6642","And as Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, Mansur narrated to us, from Said ibn Jubayr: ""We wrote in the Zabur after the Reminder,"" he said: The Zabur, Criterion and Reminder is the Torah; the earth is the earth of Paradise. This is what we found in this verse's interpretation from Said ibn Jubayr."
"6643","And of it is what was narrated from al-Shabi. As Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Abd al-Wahhab, meaning al-Thaqafi, narrated to us, Dawud, meaning Ibn Abi Hind, narrated to us, from Amir: ""We wrote in the Zabur after the Reminder,"" he said: The Zabur of Dawud, after the Reminder, he said: Musa's reminder, the Torah. This contradicts what we narrated in its interpretation."
"6644","As Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, al-Firyabi narrated to us, Warqa narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid: ""We wrote in the Zabur after the Reminder,"" he said: The Zabur is the Book with Allah, that the earth, meaning the earth of Paradise, ""My righteous servants shall inherit it."" When there occurred in this the disagreement occurring in it of what we mentioned, we sought the meaning in it of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6645","5629 - We found Muhammad ibn Sulayman ibn Hisham narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah al-Darir narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Jami ibn Shaddad, from Safwan ibn Muhriz, from Imran ibn al-Husayn who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Accept glad tidings, O Banu Tamim."" They said: You gave us tidings, so give us. He said: ""Accept glad tidings, O people of Yemen."" He said: We said: We accepted; inform us of the first of this affair, how was it? He said: ""Allah was before everything, His Throne was upon water, and He wrote in the Tablet the mention of everything."" A comer came to me and said to me: O Imran, your she-camel loosened from its hobble. I went out, and there was mirage between me and her. I went out after her, and know not what was after me. In this hadith was that Allah Exalted wrote in the Tablet the mention of everything."
"6646","5630 - And we found Jafar ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Abu Marwan Abd al-Malik ibn Habib narrated to us, Abu Ishaq al-Fazari narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Jami ibn Shaddad, from Safwan ibn Muhriz, from Imran ibn al-Husayn who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a party of Yemen's people came to him and said: We came to you, O Messenger of Allah, to learn religion, and ask you of the first of this affair, how was it? He said: ""Allah was, and nothing but Him was. His Throne was upon water. Then He wrote in the Reminder everything. Then He created the heavens and earth."" What is in this hadith was like what is in the first hadith, with increase upon it, namely his saying: Then He created the heavens and earth."
"6647","5631 - And we found Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud, author of al-Tayalisah, narrated to us, al-Masudi narrated to us, from Jami ibn Shaddad, from Buraydah ibn al-Husayb. Thus I found it in my book from Bakkar."
"6648","5632 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated it to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, al-Masudi narrated to us, from Jami ibn Shaddad, from Safwan ibn Muhriz, from Ibn Husayb: that a people entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he began giving them tidings while they said: Give us. They went out from him, and another people entered upon him and said: We came to you to learn religion, and ask of the beginning of this affair. He said: ""Accept glad tidings, since those did not accept it."" He said: ""Allah, Glorified, with nothing but Him; His Throne was upon water, and He wrote in the Reminder everything."" al-Amash differed in to whom this hadith returned, of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); al-Amash mentioned that it is Imran ibn al-Husayn, and al-Masudi mentioned that it is Buraydah ibn al-Husayb. The sound with us is what al-Amash said in it. Indicating that is that al-Thawri narrated it from Jami ibn Shaddad, agreeing with al-Amash in it and disagreeing with al-Masudi, though he fell short of part of its text in their narration."
"6649","5633 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Muammal ibn Ismail narrated to us, Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Jami ibn Shaddad, from Safwan ibn Muhriz, from Imran ibn Husayn: that the delegation of Banu Tamim came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he said: ""Receive glad tidings, O Banu Tamim."" They said: You gave us tidings, so give us. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) changed. Then the delegation of Yemen's people came to him and he said: ""Receive glad tidings, O people of Yemen; accept glad tidings, since Banu Tamim did not accept it."" They said: We accepted, O Messenger of Allah. Then he narrated, and a man said to me: Your camel has gone. Would it had gone and I had not stood. In this hadith narrated by Safwan, from whom he narrated it, from Imran, of one intending Allah's Book in the Reminder everything before His creating heavens and earth, it was understood by what is in this hadith that the Reminder intended in His saying Exalted: ""We wrote in the Zabur after the Reminder,"" is that that Reminder is what was written before creating heavens and earth, and the things mentioned after it are what besides it, of Torah, Gospel and Quran. As for linguists, they held the Reminder intended in this verse is the Criterion, arguing in that with His saying: ""Sad, by the Quran full of Reminder,"" His saying Almighty: ""Ask the people of Reminder,"" His saying Exalted: ""We sent down the Reminder, and We preserve it,"" and His saying Exalted: ""It is but Reminder and clear Quran."" In these verses was what indicated the Reminder mentioned in them is the Quran. They used to say in that that they found prepositional letters alternating one another, addressed in them with ""after"" for what is intended ""before,"" and with ""before"" for what is intended ""after."" That exists in Arabs' speech. What what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of what we mentioned, indicated is worthier for this verse's interpretation than what they said, since what they said was not called to by a necessity obligating bearing the matter upon what they bore it upon. And success is from Allah."
"6650","904 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his saying regarding the imam: ""When he prays sitting, pray sitting, all."" Is that ruling remaining upon its state, or abrogated by other than it after the Messenger of Allah's death (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 5634 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in his house while ill, praying sitting, with people behind him standing. He gestured to them to sit. When he finished he said: ""The imam was made only to be followed. When he bows, bow; when he rises, rise; and when he prays sitting, pray sitting."""
"6651","5635 - And al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with this hadith too."
"6652","5636 - And Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, Yahya ibn Yahya al-Naysaburi narrated to us; and Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us; they said: Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Humayd narrated to us, from his father, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Zuhr with us, with Abu Bakr behind him; when the Messenger of Allah said takbir, Abu Bakr said takbir making us hear. He saw us standing, and said: Sit; he gestured that to them. When he completed prayer he said: ""You almost did the deed of Persia and Rome with their chiefs. Follow your imams: if they pray standing, pray standing; if they pray sitting, pray sitting."""
"6653","5637 - And Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Ibn Yazid, Malik and Ibn Saman informed me: that Ibn Shihab informed them, he said: Anas ibn Malik informed me: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rode a horse and it threw him, scraping his right side. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with us one of the prayers sitting, and we prayed behind him sitting. When he finished he said: ""The imam was made only to be followed, so do not differ against him. When he bows, bow; when he rises, rise; when he says: Allah hears one who praises Him, say: Our Lord, to You is praise; when he prostrates, prostrate; and when he prays sitting, pray sitting."""
"6654","5638 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Umar ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rode a horse in Medina and it threw him at a palm trunk. His horse bolted. We came visiting him and found him in Aishah's storeroom glorifying sitting. We stood behind him, and he was silent toward us. Then we came to him another time visiting him, finding him praying the prescribed prayer sitting. We stood behind him, and he gestured to us, so we sat. When we completed prayer he said: ""When the imam prays sitting, pray sitting; when the imam prays standing, pray standing. Do not do as Persia's people do with their chiefs."""
"6655","5639 - And al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us. And Muhammad ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, my father and Shuayb ibn al-Layth informed us, then they joined, saying: al-Layth narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) complained, and we prayed with him sitting, Abu Bakr saying takbir making people hear. He turned to us, seeing us standing, gestured to us, and we sat. When he gave salam he said: ""Persia and Rome stand over their kings while they sit; do not do so. Follow your imams: if the imam prays standing, pray standing; if he prays sitting, pray sitting."""
"6656","5640 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Surayj ibn al-Numan narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, Umar ibn Abi Salamah informed us, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The imam was made only to be followed. When he says takbir, say takbir; when he bows, bow; when he says: Allah hears one who praises Him, say: Our Lord, to You is praise. If he prays standing, pray standing; if he prays sitting, pray sitting, all."""
"6657","5641 - And Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Musab ibn Muhammad al-Qurashi, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The imam is only to be followed: when he prays sitting, pray sitting, all."""
"6658","5642 - And Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Said ibn Amir al-Dubai narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqamah narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6659","5643 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Dawud narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Yala ibn Ata who said: I heard Abu Alqamah narrating, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever obeys me has obeyed Allah; whoever disobeys me has disobeyed Allah. Whoever obeys the commander has obeyed me; whoever disobeys the commander has disobeyed me. When he prays standing, pray standing; if he prays sitting, pray sitting."""
"6660","5644 - And Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Humran narrated to us; and Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Raja narrated to us; they said: Uqbah ibn Abi al-Sahba al-Bahili narrated to us, he said: I heard Salim saying: Abdullah ibn Umar narrated to me: that he was one day with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among a group of his Companions, and he said: ""Do you not know I am Allah's Messenger?"" They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah, we testify you are Allah's Messenger. He said: ""Do you not know Allah Exalted sent down in His Book that whoever obeys me has obeyed Allah?"" They said: Yes, we testify whoever obeys you has obeyed Allah. He said: ""Of my obedience is that you obey your imams: if they pray sitting, pray sitting."" A speaker said: These reports came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with successive coming, by sound, accepted chains. After him more than one of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) acted with it, among them Usayd ibn Hudayr."
"6661","As Yunus narrated to us, Anas ibn Iyad informed us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Bushayr ibn Yasar: that Usayd ibn Hudayr used to lead his people, Banu Abd al-Ashhal. He went out to them after his complaint, and they commanded him to advance and pray with them. He said: I cannot pray standing. He prayed sitting and they prayed sitting. And among them: Jabir ibn Abdullah."
"6662","As Abdullah ibn Raja narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Shafii narrated to us, Dawud ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Attar narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said who said: Abu al-Zubayr al-Makki informed me, from Jabir ibn Abdullah: that he complained at Mecca, then went out after, praying sitting, and we prayed behind him sitting. Our answer to him regarding that is that we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as he mentioned. Except that there came from him that he applied after them contrary to what he applied in them, in his illness in which he died."
"6663","5645 - As Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us. 5646 - And as al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us; they said: Israil ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Arqam ibn Shurahbil who said: I traveled with Ibn Abbas from Medina to Syria. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he fell ill with his illness in which he died, was in Aishah's house. He said: ""Call Ali for me."" She said: Shall we not call Abu Bakr for you? He said: Call him. Hafsah said: Shall we not call Umar for you? He said: Call him. Umm al-Fadl said: Shall we not call al-Abbas for you? He said: Call him. When they attended, he raised his head and said: ""Let Abu Bakr pray with people."" Abu Bakr advanced praying with people. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) felt relief and went out supported between two men. When Abu Bakr sensed him, they glorified; Abu Bakr went to step back, and the Prophet, upon him be peace, gestured to him: Your place. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) continued from where Abu Bakr ended of the Quran, with Abu Bakr standing and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sitting. Abu Bakr followed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) completed prayer until he became heavy, he went out supported between two men, his feet trailing on the ground. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died after a day. In this hadith was that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with people sitting, with Abu Bakr standing, and people likewise."
"6664","5647 - And as Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, Zaidah ibn Qudamah narrated to us, Musa ibn Abi Aishah narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, meaning Ibn Utbah, who said: I entered upon Aishah and said: Will you not narrate to me of the Messenger of Allah's illness (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? She said: Yes. People were gathered in the mosque awaiting the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for the last evening prayer. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to Abu Bakr to pray with people, and he prayed with them those days. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) felt relief in himself and went out supported between two men for Zuhr prayer, with Abu Bakr praying with people. When Abu Bakr saw him, he went to step back, and he gestured to him not to step back, saying to them: Seat me beside him. They seated him beside Abu Bakr. Abu Bakr began praying standing with the Messenger of Allah's prayer (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and people praying with Abu Bakr's prayer. In these reports was what we mentioned, of the Prophet's praying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sitting with people standing. That indicated the abrogation of what was of him before that in the first reports. A speaker said: What was in these reports we began mentioning was of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that prayer while a follower, not an imam. He mentioned in that."
"6665","5648 - What Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Shabbah ibn Sawwar informed us, Shubah narrated to us, from Nuaym ibn Abi Hind, from Abu Wail, from Masruq, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in his illness in which he died behind Abu Bakr, sitting."
"6666","5649 - And what Muhammad ibn Humayd ibn Hisham al-Ruayni narrated to us, Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, Humayd narrated to me, Thabit al-Bunani narrated to me, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed behind Abu Bakr in a single garment, a cloak differing between its ends. It was the last prayer he prayed. He said: In these two hadiths of Aishah and Anas was that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that prayer was a follower, not an imam. Our answer to him regarding that is that the worthier for us in reports, when there occurs in them the like of what occurred in this, is to bear them upon agreement, turning their aspects to what they bear turning to, and not bear them upon contradiction and divergence, while we find the way to that. In Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah's hadith, from Ibn Abbas and from Aishah, was that Abu Bakr had been praying with people those days in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was kept back from prayer by his illness cutting him off from that. It is possible what was of him in the two hadiths of Ibn Abbas, al-Aswad and Ubaydullah, from Aishah, is upon a prayer in which what was of him was while he was the imam, with Abu Bakr a follower. What is in the two hadiths of Anas and Masruq, from Aishah, is in another prayer than that prayer in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed behind Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. Since this meaning applies in these reports, we understood thereby that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed for people sitting while they behind him were standing. That is confirmed by what is in al-Arqam's hadith, from Ibn Abbas, of his taking (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from where Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, ended. That is not permissible but while he is the imam in that prayer, namely upon what Abu Bakr ended of recitation in it. It is established thereby that he was in it an imam, not a follower, for the follower does not recite behind the imam in what is recited aloud in, except that a party said: he recites the Mother of the Quran specifically. In al-Aswad's hadith, from Aishah, is that his sitting was to Abu Bakr's left. That was the sitting of the imam, not the sitting of the follower, for Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, returned with him to his right, namely the follower's station, not the imam's station. It was understood by his sitting to Abu Bakr's left, not behind him, that he intended thereby imamate in that prayer, not following in it. Had he intended following another, he would have sat behind him, as he did on the day of Banu Amr ibn Awf when he went to reconcile between them, and Abu Bakr came praying with people."
"6667","5650 - As Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Hisham al-Tammar informed us, Hammad ibn Zayd informed us, Ubaydullah ibn Umar ibn Hafs narrated to us, from Abu Hazim who said: He narrated to me with what I denied, meaning Abu Hazim, he said: Sahl ibn Sad al-Saidi narrated to me, he said: There was fighting, and it reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He prayed Zuhr, then came to them to reconcile between them, saying: O Bilal, if prayer comes and I have not come, command Abu Bakr to pray with people. When Asr came and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had not come, Bilal called adhan, then iqamah, then said: O Abu Bakr, advance. Abu Bakr advanced. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came, and people began clapping. Abu Bakr, when he entered prayer, would not turn. When he saw clapping not stopping, he turned, seeing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah gestured to him to remain, but Abu Bakr stepped back. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw that, he advanced and prayed with the people. When he completed his prayer he said: O Abu Bakr, what prevented you standing firm when I gestured to you? He said: It was not for Ibn Abi Quhafah to lead the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: He said to the people: When something befalls you in your prayer, glorification is for men, clapping for women."
"6668","5651 - And as Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us: that Malik informed him, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sad: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went to Banu Amr ibn Awf to reconcile between them. Prayer came, and the caller came to Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: Will you pray with people, so I establish? He said: Yes. Abu Bakr prayed. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came while people were in prayer, making his way till he stood in the row. People clapped. Abu Bakr would not turn in his prayer. When people increased clapping, he turned, seeing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah gestured to him to remain in your place. Abu Bakr raised his hands, praising Allah for what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him of that. Then Abu Bakr stepped back till he stood level with the row, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) advanced. When he finished, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Abu Bakr, what prevented you standing firm when I commanded you? Abu Bakr said: It was not for Ibn Abi Quhafah to pray before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Why did I see you increasing clapping? Whoever is befallen by something in his prayer, let him glorify; when he glorifies, he is turned to. Clapping is only for women."
"6669","5652 - And as al-Muzani narrated to us, al-Shafii narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, Abu Hazim narrated to us, he said: I heard Sahl ibn Sad saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out reconciling between Banu Amr ibn Awf. Then he mentioned the like of it, except that he said: Clapping is only for women, glorification for men. Whoever is befallen by something in his prayer, let him say: Glory be to Allah. Abu Jafar said: Do you not see that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this hadith, when he intended to be a follower in that prayer, stood the follower's station. That indicates that in his prayer in his illness, when he commanded them to seat him beside Abu Bakr, that was for his intending to be the imam in that prayer, not a follower in it. Likewise was of him when Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf prayed with people."
"6670","5653 - As al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, Ibn Awn informed us, from Urwah ibn al-Mughirah ibn Shubah, from his father. And Ibn Awn, from Ibn Sirin, raising it to al-Mughirah ibn Shubah who said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey. At predawn the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made his camel kneel, then descended, withdrawing from me the distance a man fulfills his need. Then he came and said to me: ""Have you water?"" I said: Yes, a waterskin or flat vessel with water in it. I poured upon him. He washed his face with a Syrian cloak upon him. He went to take his hand out of it, but the cloak's sleeves were narrow, so he took his hands out from under the cloak, perhaps casting the cloak off his hands. He washed his hands, wiped his turban, rubbed the forelock with something, wiped over his leather socks, then we rode. He caught the dawn prayer, with Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf leading them, having prayed a rakah. I went to notify him, but he forbade me. We prayed behind him a rakah, and made up the rakah we were preceded in."
"6671","5654 - And as Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Azhar ibn Sa'd al-Samman narrated to us, from Ibn 'Awn, from al-Sha'bi, 'Urwah ibn al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah narrated to me, from his father, who said: We were on a journey, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) tapped my back with a stick he had with him, so I went with him; he turned aside and I turned aside with him, and we went on until we came to a mountain pass, where he dismounted and went off until he disappeared from me, then he came and said: Do you have water? He said: And I had a small water-skin, so I poured from it onto his hands and he washed them and washed his face, then he mentioned the rest of the hadith: and we caught up with 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf, who had led the people in prayer and prayed one rak'ah, so I went to inform him but he prevented me, and we prayed what we caught and made up what we had missed. Do you not see that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this prayer, when he intended to be a follower in it, stood in the place of the follower and did not move to the side of the imam, so that indicates that his sitting beside Abu Bakr in the prayer in which Abu Bakr was leading the people, he intended thereby to be the imam in it. And what is in the hadith of Masruq from 'A'ishah, and what is in the hadith of Anas about Abu Bakr's prayer during his illness, that in our view - and Allah knows best - concerns another prayer, because in the hadith of Ibn 'Abbas and 'A'ishah, Abu Bakr had been leading the people during those days, which indicates he prayed several prayers with them, so it is possible he prayed some of them behind Abu Bakr and some with Abu Bakr and the people, so that the narrated reports agree and none contradicts another. And in what we have explained of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) leading seated while the people stood, Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, Zufar, and Muhammad ibn Idris al-Shafi'i, may Allah have mercy on them, held the permissibility of the imamate of one sitting who bows and prostrates for those standing who bow and prostrate, because sitting which includes bowing and prostration, since it is a substitute for standing, the substitute is like what it substitutes for, and the doer of the substitute is like the doer of the original, so it is permissible for him to be imam for them; this is the analogy in this chapter. And Malik ibn Anas and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan held that a sitting person does not lead a standing person after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), holding that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did in that prayer was specific to him and not for anyone of his ummah besides him, and no one may declare something done by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as specific except by what requires it through a directive from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the people about it. And with Allah is success."
"6672","905 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his cursing the ra'ish or the rashi along with his cursing the briber and the bribed. 5655 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad ibn Nuh narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Mansur al-Saluli narrated to us, Huraym - meaning Ibn Sufyan - narrated to us, from Layth, from Abu Zur'ah, from Abu Idris, from Thawban who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cursed the briber, the bribed, and the ra'ish."
"6673","5656 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, Yazid ibn Khalid ibn Mawhab and Sahl ibn Muhammad al-'Askari narrated to us, they said: Ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, Layth narrated to us, from Abu al-Khattab, from Abu Idris, from Thawban who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah cursed the briber, the bribed, and the ra'ish, and he is the one who goes between them. Ibn Abi Za'idah and Huraym differed from Layth in the chain of this hadith as we mentioned their difference from him. A questioner asked about the ra'ish and the rashi mentioned in this hadith, what is it? Our answer to him was that it is the one who strives in that affair until it is completed thereby; thus say the scholars of language about it, saying it is taken from the feathers from which arrows are made and placed in them, without which the arrows do not stand, so likewise the intermediary without whose action it would not come together is likened to it. As for what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his cursing the briber and the bribed with no mention of anyone else with them."
"6674","5657 - Among what Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us, Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to me, from al-Harith ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The curse of Allah is upon the briber and the bribed."
"6675","5658 - And among what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu 'Amir al-'Aqadi narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. And what is in this hadith of joining the briber and the bribed in the curse indicates they are equal in it, and that each of them did what is not lawful for him: the briber did what is not lawful to bribe with, and the bribed did what is not lawful to take as bribe, and that is clarified by what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in hadiths other than this hadith."
"6676","5659 - Among them is what Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Duhaym ibn al-Yatim narrated to us, Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ya'qub al-Zam'i, from his aunt Quraybah daughter of 'Abdullah ibn Wahb, from her father who said: My mother Umm Salamah informed me, of some anxiety in it: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cursed the briber and the bribed in judgment. That indicates that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) joining them in the curse in this hadith does not make their cases equal in it. And among what was narrated in which they were joined without knowing what it was, except that it is understood it was from them over what is forbidden to them."
"6677","5660 - Among what Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn 'Umar narrated to me, from Abu Harzah - meaning Ibn Mujahid - from al-Hasan, from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from the Prophet, upon him be peace, who said: The curse of the eater and the giver is equal in bribery. In our view - and Allah knows best - one who was denied a right and so bribed to reach his right is not included in that, for he is not included in the blame because he sought to reach his right, while the taker of the bribe from him, without whose taking he would not have reached his right due to his withholding it, is included in the curse mentioned in these hadiths. And like that is what was narrated from Jabir ibn Zayd on this meaning."
"6678","As Ahmad ibn Abi 'Imran narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il or Ishaq ibn Isma'il narrated to us - Abu Ja'far doubts - Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir ibn Zayd who said: We found in the days of Ziyad or Ibn Ziyad nothing more beneficial than bribes, meaning they did that to ward off evil from themselves. And among what was narrated in this chapter also of what is meant by briber and bribed: that it was concerning what is unlawful for both the briber and the bribed together."
"6679","5661 - Among what 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Ziyad al-Madani narrated to us, 'Abbas ibn al-Walid al-Narsi narrated to us. 5662 - And among what Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, they said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from 'Umar ibn Abi Salamah, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cursed the briber and the bribed in judgment. And Allah is the Granter of success."
"6680","906 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) indicating that a man may be attributed to a place whose people he was not from by becoming one of its people. 5663 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Abu Dawud narrated to us, Hisham narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas who said: A blackening from the Fire will afflict some people as punishment for sins they committed, then Allah the Exalted will admit them into Paradise by His mercy and the intercession of the intercessors, and they will be called: the Jahannamis."
"6681","5664 - And 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Hisham narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Some people will be struck by a blackening from the Fire for sins they committed, then they will come out, and the people of Paradise will call them the Jahannamis. In this hadith is that these people were called Jahannamis for being among the people of Hell, though they were not born in it, and in that is evidence for what is preferable in what scholars differed over regarding to which lands a man is attributed: some held a man is from the people of the place where he was born, not from other places he moves to and settles in, among them Abu Hanifah; and some said whoever settles in a place and takes it as home may be said to be from its people even if born elsewhere, among them Abu Yusuf. And Abu Hanifah had agreed with Abu Yusuf."
"6682","In what Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn al-'Abbas al-Razi mentioned to us, from Musa ibn Nasr, from Hisham ibn 'Ubaydullah al-Razi: that Abu Yusuf mentioned to them this view from Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, and that Abu Hanifah argued with him saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) moved to Medina, yet that did not remove him from being among the people of Makkah. Abu Yusuf said: So I said to him: And he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) became, by moving to Medina and settling it, among the people of Medina, though his birth was elsewhere. He said: Then he said, leave me alone, and said nothing about it. In that, in our view, is what indicates he saw it as binding upon him. The Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, is Allah's proof over His creation, and he informed us about those admitted to Hell, though not born in it, that they were called Jahannamis, and in that is evidence it is permissible to say of a man after moving from a place whose people he had become by settling it that he is from the people of the first place where he was and moved from, just as one who settled in Egypt from the people of Medina is said to be Madani, and one who settled in it from Kufah is said to be Kufi, just as the Jahannamis were called by that name after becoming among the people of Paradise and being brought out from Hell to it. In what we mentioned of this is sufficiency from the rest of what is argued therewith in our view in this chapter. And with Allah is success."
"6683","5665 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from 'Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from 'Amr ibn Maymun, from Ibn Mas'ud: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There will be a people in the Fire as long as Allah wills them to be, then Allah the Exalted will have mercy on them and bring them out of it, and they will be in the lowest Paradise in a river called al-Hayawan; if the people of this world were to host them they would feed them and give them drink and clothe them. 'Ata said: And I think he said: And they would marry them off. We have mentioned earlier in this book of ours, in the chapter explaining the problem of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: The place of a whip in Paradise is better than the world and what is in it, concerning this chapter from Ibn Mas'ud from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what makes us needless to repeat it."
"6684","And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, 'Arim Abu al-Nu'man narrated to us, Abu Hilal al-Rasibi narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik concerning this verse: {But as for those who were wretched, they will be in the Fire}: He said: A people will come out of the Fire, and we do not deny it as the people of Harura' denied it."
"6685","And as Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated to us, Abu Hilal narrated to us, Qatadah narrated to us concerning this verse: {But as for those who were wretched, they will be in the Fire} up to His saying: what He wills. He said: At this point is the hadith of Anas ibn Malik, who said: A people will come out of the Fire. Qatadah said: We do not say as the people of Harura' say."
"6686","5666 - And as 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us, Hisham ibn Yusuf narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from Qatadah and Thabit, from Anas that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - or that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - said: Indeed some people will come out of the Fire."
"6687","5667 - And as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Thabit al-Bunani and Abu 'Imran narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - Abu 'Imran said -: Four will come out of the Fire - and Thabit said: two men - and they will be presented before Allah, Mighty and Majestic, then they will be ordered back to the Fire, then one of them will turn and say: I had hoped, when You brought me out of it, that You would not return me to it. So Allah the Exalted will save him from it. We have mentioned from Anas ibn Malik also in the chapter before this chapter of this meaning what has made us needless to repeat it here."
"6688","5668 - And as Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud and Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated to us, and the wording is Abu Dawud's. 5669 - And Bakkar ibn Qutaybah and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abu Dawud narrated to us. 5670 - And Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahim al-Harawi narrated to us, Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us, they said: Al-Qasim ibn al-Fadl narrated to us. 5671 - And Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Al-Qasim ibn al-Fadl al-Huddani narrated to us, Sa'id ibn al-Muhallab al-Jahdami narrated to me, from Talq ibn Habib who said: I met Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, and I was the most vehement of people in denying intercession, so I recited to him every verse in the Qur'an in which Allah promised its people eternity in the Fire, and he said to me: O Tulayq, do you think you know Allah's Book and the Sunnah of His Prophet better than I?! I said: No. He said: Then let these two fall deaf - and he pointed with his hands to his ears - if I did not hear Muhammad say: They will come out of the Fire, while we recite what you recite, and those you recite about are the polytheists, they are its people. I said: And who are these people? He said: A people who committed sins, so they were punished for their sins, then they were brought out."
"6689","5672 - And as Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, 'Amr ibn 'Awn al-Wasiti narrated to us, Khalid ibn 'Abdullah narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Yahya, from his father, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the people of Paradise enter Paradise and the people of the Fire enter the Fire, Allah will say by His mercy: Bring out whoever had in his heart the weight of a mustard seed of faith. He said: So they will come out having become charred, and they will be cast into a river called the River of Life, and they will grow thereby just as foam grows on the bank of a torrent; have you not seen that it comes out yellow and curled? In these reports is that some people come out of the Fire after being in it, and in Allah's Book is what indicates that, and it is His saying, Mighty and Majestic, informing about the people of the Fire: {So the intercession of the intercessors will not benefit them}, meaning that others will be benefited by the intercession of the intercessors, and among that is His saying informing about them: {So we have no intercessors}, and like things of this kind. And what is more indicative than this in the Qur'an is His saying: {Who is it that can intercede with Him except by His permission}, and His saying: {And they cannot intercede except for one He approves}. So the most fitting of these things for interpreters is to refer the exception in the verse we recited to this meaning. As for the linguists, among them al-Farra', he held that the meaning of: {Abiding therein as long as the heavens and the earth endure, except what your Lord wills} is upon two meanings: one is to make it an exception, like one's saying: By Allah I will beat you unless I see otherwise, while his resolve is to beat him; so likewise: {Abiding therein as long as the heavens and the earth endure, except what your Lord wills}, and He will not will it. And the other: then he mentioned the interpretation we mentioned of excepting the many from the few. And there is nothing in this chapter more deserving in our view than what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about those who come out of the Fire after being punished in it, so that is what is excepted by His saying: {except what your Lord wills}. And with Allah is success."
"6690","908 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Ajwah is from Paradise. 5673 - Al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb narrated to us, Mahdi ibn Ja'far narrated to us, 'Abd al-Majid ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz - meaning Ibn Abi Rawwad - narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Uthman ibn Khuthaym, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ajwah is from Paradise, and in it is cure for poison, and truffles are from manna and in them - or their water - is cure for the eye, and in the Arab ram is cure for sciatica, its meat is eaten and its broth is drunk."
"6691","5674 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, 'Ali ibn Yahya narrated to us, Jarir - and he is Ibn 'Abd al-Hamid - narrated to us, al-A'mash narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Iyas, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, Ja'far said: And Abu Nadrah narrated to me, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri and Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out with a truffle in his hand and said: This is from manna, and its water is cure for the eye, and ajwah is cure from poison."
"6692","5675 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq and Abu Umayyah together narrated to us, they said: Sa'id ibn 'Amir al-Duba'i narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ajwah is from Paradise, and in it is cure from poison, and truffles are from manna, and their water is cure for the eye."
"6693","5676 - And 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, Aswad ibn 'Amir narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Wasil ibn Hayyan, from 'Abdullah ibn Buraydah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Truffles are cure for the eye, and ajwah is from the fruit of Paradise. An objector said: How do you accept the like of this while you narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates its opposite? And he mentioned."
"6694","5677 - Among what Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Dawud and Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, they said: Ahmad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, Wasil ibn Hayyan narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn Buraydah narrated to me, from his father: that he was among the forty-two companions who prayed behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the Maqam, and when he finished his prayer he reached with his hands between himself and the Ka'bah as if wanting to take something with his hand, then he turned to us and said: Did you see me when I finished my prayer reaching with my hand toward the Ka'bah as if I wanted to take something? They said: Yes, O Prophet of Allah. He said: Paradise was shown to me, and I saw in it wonders of beauty, and a bunch of grapes passed before me and pleased me, so I reached with my hand to take it but it eluded me; had I taken it I would have planted it among you so you would eat from the fruit of Paradise. In that is evidence they did not eat from the fruit of Paradise, and likewise in what you narrated before it in this chapter, and in this is severe contradiction, because in this hadith and in what you narrated before it in this chapter is that ajwah is from the fruit of Paradise, yet they had been eating it before that. Our answer to him was that there is no contradiction in any of that, because it may be that what is meant by ajwah being from the fruit of Paradise is that Allah, Majestic is His majesty, singled out some of His allies among the people of this world, either from His prophets or others, by gifting him something of the ajwah of Paradise, which the gifted one ate, then planted a stone in this world, from which came the palm trees from which is the ajwah existing today, and what was planted in the earth transfers it to the state of the rest of its plantings in deficiency from other plantings, and the like of this exists in worldly fruits, for we see palms grown from the stone of dates brought from the Hijaz, Kufah and Basrah in land whose other palms are different, so likewise the ajwah the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned may be so, and the shortcoming in it from the ajwah of Paradise is due to the earth it was planted in turning it to the state of its other fruits. What strengthens this possibility is what we mentioned in the hadith of Buraydah we narrated from Muhammad ibn 'Ali and Fahd, the Messenger of Allah's saying about the bunch mentioned in it: Had I taken it I would have planted it so you would eat from the fruits of Paradise. And a bunch is not planted; rather the seeds of its grains are planted, from which come trees whose fruit takes the ruling of the land it is planted in. And it may be that the Prophet's saying: so you would eat from the fruit of Paradise means the grapes in that bunch, not other than it, and what came from the seeds of those grapes in its fruit returns to the like of what the mentioned ajwah returned to in what we narrated about it in this chapter."
"6695","909 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in these reports about ajwah, whether it concerns ajwah from all palm trees in all lands or from a specific kind. 5678 - Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Shayzari narrated to us, 'Isa ibn Sulayman al-Shayzari narrated to us, Marwan narrated to us, from Hashim - meaning Ibn Hashim ibn 'Utbah ibn Abi Waqqas - from 'Amir ibn Sa'd, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever eats seven of the ajwah of al-'Aliyah every morning, neither poison nor magic will harm him that day."
"6696","5679 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ja'far - meaning Ibn Abi Kathir - informed us, 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman - meaning Abu Tuwalah - informed me, from 'Amir ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, from his father: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever takes seven ajwah dates in the morning between the two lava fields of Medina, nothing will harm him that day until evening."
"6697","5680 - And al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Umarah, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'Amir ibn Sa'd, from his father: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No man takes seven ajwah dates in the morning between its two lava fields but nothing will harm him that day until night. We understood thereby that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended by ajwah the ajwah mentioned in these reports which is between the two lava fields of Medina, not other kinds, then we examined the hadith of 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman in its chain and found it had entered it what necessitates the corruption of its chain."
"6698","5681 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Abu 'Amir al-'Aqadi narrated to us, Abu Mus'ab narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman who said: Some people came out from 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz and reported that 'Amir ibn Sa'd said: I heard my father say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever eats seven dates from between the two lava fields of Medina, no poison will harm him that day until night. This hadith is thereby corrupted, and what remained of the sound hadiths on it is what came from Hashim ibn Hashim of what we narrated in this chapter. And Allah is the Granter of success."
"6699","910 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about truffles, and the reason for which he said to the people that they are from manna. 5682 - 'Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, al-Firyabi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, from 'Amr ibn Hurayth, from Sa'id ibn Zayd, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Truffles are from manna, and their water is cure for the eye."
"6700","5683 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, from 'Amr ibn Hurayth, from Sa'id ibn Zayd, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"6701","5684 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Abu Hafs narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, from 'Amr ibn Hurayth, from Sa'id ibn Zayd, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. In this hadith is the Messenger of Allah's informing that truffles are from manna, then we looked into the reason for which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed the people of that about truffles."
"6702","5685 - We found Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Muhammad al-Mu'addib narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Isa al-'Abdi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir narrated to us, from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah who said: Truffles became abundant in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and some of the Prophet's Companions said: Truffles are from the smallpox of the earth, so they refrained from eating them. That reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he came out and ascended the pulpit and said: How is it that some people claim truffles are from the smallpox of the earth; indeed they are not from the smallpox of the earth; indeed truffles are from manna, and their water is cure for the eye; and indeed ajwah is from Paradise, and it is cure from poison. In this hadith is the clarification of the reason for which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed them what he informed them about truffles. Then we examined Muhammad ibn 'Isa, the narrator of this hadith, and found him acceptable among his people, a man from Basrah from whom Yunus ibn Muhammad and Yahya ibn Hammad narrate. And Allah is the Granter of success."
"6703","5686 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, 'Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, 'Asim ibn Kulayb al-Jarmi narrated to us, from his father who said: 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas said to me: 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, when he invited the elders among the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), would invite me with them and say: Do not speak until they speak. One day - or one night - he invited us and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said about the Night of Decree what you know: Seek it in the last ten, on the odd nights; in which odd night do you see it? A man said: The ninth, seventh, fifth, third. He said to me: Why do you not speak? I said: If you wish I will speak. He said: I invited you only to speak. I said: I only speak by my opinion. He said: I ask you about your opinion. I said: I heard Allah the Exalted say - He mentioned seven - He mentioned seven heavens, seven earths, and what the earth grows as seven. Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: Then We split the earth open, and grew therein grain, grapes, herbage, olives, palms, dense gardens, fruit and fodder. Gardens are every dense garden, and fodder is what the earth grows of what people eat. 'Umar said: Are you unable to say the like of what he said?! They said: In this hadith 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas mentioned what the earth grows as seven, while in the verse there are eight; when we reflect we understand grapes are from fruit with no disagreement among scholars, so grapes enter into fruit though mentioned separately in this verse, leaving seven in the verse, not more, so we understood thereby that palms from which come fresh dates are not fruit, for if we referred them to fruit what is in the verse would become six, which indicates fresh dates are not fruit. That was from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas in the presence of 'Umar ibn al-Khattab and others among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of Muhajirun and Ansar, and they did not repel him nor oppose him, indicating their agreement with him, so this view left to itself would be preferable to what is said in this chapter, except that when we found from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about ajwah that it is from the fruit of Paradise of what we narrated in it in this chapter before this chapter, and he is the one besides whom none is reported, for it is the speech of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who is the proof over all people, it became necessary to take fresh dates as included in fruit, and that the rest of fruit after fresh dates and grapes completes the number so the mentioned in Ibn 'Abbas's hadith is as he intended, with fruit being as those who said fresh dates are from it said, not as their opponents said. Another hadith was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter, and it is:"
"6704","5687 - Among what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us, Husayn ibn 'Umar al-Ahmasi narrated to us, Mukhariq narrated to us, from Tariq, from 'Umar who said: Some Jews came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Muhammad, is there fruit in Paradise?! He said: In it is fruit, palms and pomegranates. He said: And do they eat as they eat in this world! He said: Yes, and multiples of that. He said: Then do they relieve themselves? He said: No, but they sweat and perspire, and Allah removes what is in their bellies of harm. In this hadith is that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: Is there fruit in Paradise? He said: In it is fruit, palms and pomegranates, and it is impossible he answered one who asked about fruit by mentioning what is not fruit; rather he answered by mentioning fruit, and in that is evidence palms and pomegranates are among fruits. And with Allah is success."
"6705","912 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying after leading the people in the eclipse prayer: I saw Paradise - or I was shown Paradise - and I reached for a bunch from it, and had I taken it you would have eaten from it as long as the world remains. 5688 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Zayd ibn Aslam. 5689 - And al-Muzani narrated to us, al-Shafi'i narrated to us, Malik informed us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from 'Ata ibn Yasar, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas who said: The sun eclipsed, and he mentioned the Messenger of Allah's prayer with the people and his saying to them when they said to him: We saw you step back. He said: I saw Paradise - or I was shown Paradise - and I reached for a bunch from it, and had I taken it you would have eaten from it as long as the world remains. We reflected on this hadith and found the best of what came in it is that the meaning of his saying: you would have eaten from it as long as the world remains is upon the meaning we mentioned about ajwah and in the hadith of Buraydah in which it was mentioned, and mentioned with it what he would have planted had he taken it so they would eat from the fruit of Paradise: that what is meant here is likewise, and the permanence mentioned in it is upon what grows in this world from the seeds of those grapes, so it is in its meaning like the ajwah whose meaning we mentioned."
"6706","913 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the Dajjal: that with him are mountains of bread. Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Sufyan al-Jahdari narrated to us, Ibn 'Awn narrated to us, from Mujahid who said: We were at sea in year sixty, under Junadah ibn Abi Umayyah, and one day he addressed us and said: We came to a man among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one day and he said: I warn you of the False Messiah, I warn you of the False Messiah; he is a man with one eye effaced - he said: I think he said: the left - he remains on earth forty mornings, with him are mountains of bread and rivers of water, his authority reaches every watering place, he does not come to four mosques: the Sacred Mosque, al-Aqsa Mosque, the mosque of Tur and the Mosque of the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), except that whatever of that is, know that Allah is not one-eyed; he said it three times."
"6707","5690 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Khalaf ibn Hisham al-Bazzar narrated to us, Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Nasr ibn 'Asim, from Subay' ibn Khalid who said: I heard Hudhayfah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Then the Dajjal will come out with a river of cold water; whoever falls into his river, his sin is confirmed and his reward is removed, and whoever falls into his fire, his reward is confirmed and his sin is removed."
"6708","5691 - And Fahd narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn Raja' narrated to us, Shayban informed us, from Mansur, from Rib'i, from Hudhayfah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I know better what is with the Dajjal than he does; with him is a burning fire and a river of cold water, so whoever among you meets him, let him not perish: let him close his eyes and fall into what he sees as fire, for it is cold water."
"6709","5692 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, my father informed us, he said: I heard Qays narrating from Mujahid, from Junadah ibn Abi Umayyah, from a man among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: We said to him: Narrate to us about the Dajjal a hadith you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for there has been disagreement over it for us. He said: I will narrate to you only what my ears heard: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood among us and said: I warn you of the False Messiah, he said it three times; indeed there was no prophet before me but he warned his nation of him and feared him for them; indeed he is among you, O nation; indeed he is short, curly-haired, with his left eye effaced; indeed with him are a garden and a fire; indeed his garden is fire and his fire is garden; and with him is a mountain of bread and a river of water; indeed he rains yet the earth does not grow; indeed he is given power over a soul so he kills it then brings it to life, then he is given no power over another; indeed he remains among you forty mornings. Then he mentioned the rest of the hadith of Yazid from Sa'id ibn Sufyan al-Jahdari. Abu Ja'far said: We reflected on these reports regarding what is with the Dajjal of bread and water, whether that is literal or otherwise?"
"6710","5693 - We found Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ishaq ibn Abi 'Abbad narrated to us, and we found al-Qasim ibn 'Abdullah ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Abu Mus'ab narrated to us, they said: 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, Yusuf said in his hadith: that he heard al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah, and al-Qasim said in his hadith, from al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah who said: No one asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the Dajjal more than I asked him, and he said: It will not harm you that he does you no harm. I said: They claim with him are food and rivers. He said: He is too insignificant to Allah for that. So the verification of this hadith of al-Mughirah and what we narrated before it is that what we narrated before it is what the Dajjal deludes people with his magic that it is water and bread, so they see it as such by his magic which is with him, of what he is able over them until they see in reality as they see with their eyes in their supposition, though it is not so; rather it is like what Allah informed about what Pharaoh's magicians did in His saying: {It was made to appear to him by their magic that they were moving}. An objector said: You narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter what contradicts what you mentioned, and he mentioned."
"6711","5694 - Among what Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sabiq narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Dajjal will come out amid a lull in religion and turning away from knowledge, and he has forty nights roaming the earth: a day of them like a year, a day of them like a month, a day of them like a Friday, then the rest of his days like these days of yours. He has a donkey he rides, the width between its ears is forty cubits, and he says to people: I am your lord, while he is one-eyed and your Lord is not one-eyed; written between his eyes is: disbeliever, read by every believer, literate or illiterate. He comes to every water and watering place except Medina and Makkah, which Allah has forbidden him, with angels standing at their gates. With him are mountains of bread and greenery traveling with him among people, while people are in hardship except those who follow him. With him are two rivers, I know them better than he: a river he calls Paradise and a river he calls Fire; whoever is admitted to what he calls Paradise, it is Fire, and whoever is admitted to what he calls Fire, it is Paradise. Devils are sent with him speaking to people. With him is great tribulation: he commands the sky and it rains as people see, and he kills a soul then brings it to life as people see, and says to people: Does any but the Lord do this? The Muslims flee to the mountain of fire in Sham, and he comes to them and besieges them, and their siege intensifies and afflicts them severely, then 'Isa descends and calls at dawn saying: O people, what prevents you from going out to the wicked liar? They say: This is a jinn man. Then they look and behold 'Isa son of Maryam, blessings of Allah upon him, and prayer is established and it is said: Come forward, O Spirit of Allah. He says: Let your imam come forward and pray with you. When he prays the dawn prayer they go out to him, and when the liar sees him he melts as salt melts in water, and he walks to him and kills him and those with him upon Judaism, until trees and stones call out. Then he cut the hadith. This objector said: In this hadith is confirmation these things will be from the Dajjal. Our answer to him was that in this hadith is what indicates other than what he thought, for in it is: then he commands the sky and it rains as people see, and he kills a soul then brings it to life as people see, and in that is confirmation of what we said: these things are from him only by way of magic which makes those reached by that magic imagine they are realities though they are not realities. In this chapter also are many reports of this kind, some of which we left for fear of lengthening the book, whose meanings return to the meanings we mentioned, and that all of that is upon magic not reality. We seek refuge with Allah from that."
"6712","914 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the first sent among Allah's prophets, who is he?! 5695 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Abi Suwayd al-Dhari' narrated to us, Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The first prophet sent was Nuh, blessings of Allah upon him. In this hadith is that the first of Allah's prophets sent was Nuh, and an objector rejected that and said: How can that be so when Allah the Exalted informed about His prophet Idris, and he is Ilyas."
"6713","As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Isra'il ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Rabi'ah ibn 'Abdullah who said: Idris is Ilyas, and Ya'qub is Isra'il, blessings of Allah upon them. Allah, Mighty and Majestic, informed about him - meaning Ilyas - that he is among the messengers by His saying: {And indeed Ilyas is among the messengers}, and he is the great-grandfather of Nuh, for Nuh is the son of Lamk son of Mattushalakh son of Akhnukh, and he is Idris."
"6714","As Ahmad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Barqi narrated to us, 'Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn 'Abdullah al-Bakka'i, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq who said: Akhnukh is Idris the Prophet as they claim - and Allah knows best - and he was the first of Adam's children given prophethood and to write with the pen."
"6715","And as Abu al-Rawwad 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Salam narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sulayman al-Tammar narrated to us, 'Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham narrated to us, then he mentioned the like of it with its chain and said: Allah said in His Book what we recited of affirming his messengership to him, and that was before Nuh existed, so precedence in messengership from Allah became due to him, and he is among Allah's prophets whom He mentioned in His Book by His saying: {And mention in the Book Idris; indeed he was truthful, a prophet}. And in what we mentioned is what negated what you narrated that Nuh was the first of Allah's prophets sent. Our answer to him was that nothing of what this deluded denier mentioned its negation is negated thereby, for the Qur'an came down in the tongue of the Arabs, so they were addressed with what they know, and they understood thereby Allah's intent, He made them understand it by what He sent down upon His Prophet sent to them in their tongue. Idris was a messenger from Allah to his people apart from others, evidenced by Allah's informing about him in His saying: {And indeed Ilyas is among the messengers, when he said to his people: Will you not fear Allah? Do you call upon Ba'l and leave the best of creators?} And it is impossible he intended by this address but his people apart from others, so one sent to them is like one sent to his people whom he addressed, while Nuh was sent to all who were on earth in his time, evidenced by Allah's punishment of them when they defied what he conveyed to them by drowning the whole earth, and that would not be except that all who were in it were among those who deserved that punishment, and since it was so we understood thereby Idris was sent to his people specifically apart from others on earth, and Nuh, blessings of Allah upon him, was sent to all on earth in his time, and none before him was sent with the like of that, so he was the first prophet sent to all on earth in his time. We understood thereby that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed to people informing them about Nuh is what we mentioned of what Allah had not given any of His prophets, blessings of Allah upon them, the like of what He gave His prophet Nuh. What Allah addressed about Idris and Nuh of what Allah undertook, since it was not contradictory, as Allah said: {Had it been from other than Allah} - meaning the Qur'an He sent down upon His Prophet - {they would have found therein much contradiction}. We understood thereby that what He caused upon His Prophet's tongue was likewise of this kind, by His saying: {Nor does he speak from desire; it is but revelation revealed; taught to him by one mighty in power}."
"6716","915 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his command in milking the she-camel to leave the milk-callers. 5696 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Kathir al-'Abdi narrated to us, Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from 'Abdullah ibn Sinan, from Dirar ibn al-Azwar who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by me - or by a man milking, as if meaning a she-camel - and said: Leave the milk-callers."
"6717","5697 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from al-A'mash, likewise the same. As for others besides him who narrated it from al-A'mash, they narrate it from him."
"6718","5698 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, 'Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, al-A'mash narrated to us, from Ya'qub ibn Buhayr, from Dirar ibn al-Azwar that he heard him say: We gifted the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a milch she-camel, then he mentioned the like of it the same."
"6719","5699 - And as Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Habban ibn Hilal informed us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to me, Sulayman al-A'mash narrated to us, from Ya'qub ibn Buhayr, from Dirar ibn al-Azwar who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a milch camel from my family, and he said to me: Milk it. I went to exert it. He said: Do not exert it; leave the milk-callers."
"6720","5700 - And as 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, Ya'la ibn 'Ubayd al-Tanafisi narrated to us, al-A'mash narrated to us, from Ya'qub ibn Buhayr, from Dirar ibn al-Azwar who said: We gifted the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a milch she-camel, and he ordered me to milk it, so I milked it and exerted it in milking, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave the milk-callers. This meaning was also narrated in the command to leave the milk-callers from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from other than Dirar, and he is Nuqadah ibn Ma'n al-Asadi."
"6721","5701 - As Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Isma'il al-Talqani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Nadlah ibn al-Sakan narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from his grandfather on his mother's side Nuqadah ibn Si'r al-Asadi who said: My family sent me with a milch camel to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Milk. I went to milk, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave the milk-callers. We reflected on the meaning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended by his command to leave the milk-callers in milking the she-camel, what is it? What we found in that is that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was an Arab who loved Arab character and adhering to it unless commanded otherwise, and it was among Arab character when they sought to milk a she-camel to leave in its udder something of the milk in it, so when they later needed its milk, either for a guest alighting or for their own need, they milked from what they had left in its udder something, however little, then mixed it with cold water, then struck its udder therewith and brought near its calf if they had it, or the skin of a calf if they had slaughtered it before, which they stuffed as they stuffed it, so it licks it and yields milk from its udder upon it, and they spend it in what they need to spend of it for a guest and themselves. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded whom he commanded to leave the milk-callers for this meaning, and no better meaning for it is possible. And with Allah is success."
"6722","916 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his coming to the mosque of Quba' and his praying in it. 5702 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan narrated to us, 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Umar narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to come to Quba' riding and walking."
"6723","5703 - And Yazid narrated to us, al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"6724","5704 - And al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb narrated to us, Yahya ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, from Ibn 'Ajlan, from Nafi' the freedman of Ibn 'Umar: that Ibn 'Umar used to come to the mosque of Quba' and ride; he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) come to it riding and walking."
"6725","5705 - And Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Ahmad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Ajlan, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar who said: The Prophet, upon him be peace, did not come to any of those mosques except the mosque of Quba'. He said: And Ibn 'Umar used to do it."
"6726","5706 - And Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, Ma'n ibn 'Isa al-Madani narrated to us, from Malik ibn Anas, from 'Abdullah ibn Dinar, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to come to Quba' walking and riding."
"6727","5707 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ja'far informed us, Ibn Dinar informed me that he heard Ibn 'Umar say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to come to the mosque of Quba' walking and riding."
"6728","5708 - And Yazid narrated to us, Habban ibn Hilal and Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated to us, they said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muslim narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Ibn 'Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. In these reports is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to come to the mosque of Quba', and in them is what indicates that was among his habits, for in these reports is that he used to come to it, not that he came to it once. An objector said: You narrated from al-Ma'rur ibn Suwayd what you already narrated in this book, that they were with 'Umar on the road to Makkah, and he saw people going aside and said: Where are these going? They said: They come to a mosque in which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed, and that he said: Those before you perished only by the likes of that, following the traces of their prophets until they took them as churches and synagogues; whoever is overtaken by prayer in one of these mosques in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed, let him pray in it, otherwise let him not intend it. He said: In what you narrated of the Messenger of Allah's intending to come to the mosque of Quba' is what indicates his urging his Companions to the like of their coming to it; indeed it was narrated of their coming to it, adhering to it, and praying in it."
"6729","Among what Yunus narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, Ibn Jurayj informed me, that Nafi' informed him, that 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar said: Salim the freedman of Abu Hudhayfah used to lead the first Muhajirun and the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the mosque of Quba', among them Abu Bakr, 'Umar, Abu Salamah, Zayd, and 'Amir ibn Rabi'ah, because he was the most versed of them in Qur'an. He said: In this is what contradicts what al-Ma'rur narrated from 'Umar, especially since in this hadith 'Umar is among those who prayed in it with others mentioned in the hadith of Ibn Jurayj from Nafi' among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also prayed in it."
"6730","5709 - He mentioned among what Yunus narrated to us, from Ibn Wahb, Hisham ibn Sa'd informed me, from Nafi' who said: I heard 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar say, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out to Quba', and the Ansar heard of him and came greeting him. I said to Bilal - or Suhayb: How did you see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) return their greeting while praying? He said: He gestured with his hand."
"6731","5710 - And among what Yunus and Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah narrated to us, they said: 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Nafi' informed us, from Hisham ibn Sa'd, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Quba' and prayed, and the Ansar heard of him and came and greeted him, and he gestured to them with his hand spreading his palm. Yunus did not say: spreading his palm."
"6732","5711 - And among what 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, Abu Nuh 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghazwan narrated to us, Hisham ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out to Quba' to pray, and we went out with him, and the Ansar heard of him and came greeting him. He said: I said: O Bilal, how did he return to them? He said: He used to gesture to them with his hand. He said: That indicates the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in it, and in this is severe confusion with what you narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying also: The best prayer of a man is in his house except the obligatory, which you mentioned earlier in this book. Is it permissible the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left his prayer in his house and went out to the mosque of Quba' to pray in it, while that prayer is voluntary, so he leaves the better along with avoiding the distance, and goes to what is lesser with undertaking the journey to it despite the distance? This is not hidden. Our answer to him was that there is no disagreement in any of these reports in what was narrated therein from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) nor from any of his Companions mentioned therein, for the hadith of al-Ma'rur from 'Umar concerns only their intending places in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not pray for merit therein over others; rather prayer overtook him so he prayed in the place he prayed in, not for merit of that place over others, so 'Umar disliked them giving it merit over others lest they return thereby to the like of what those before them were upon of following the traces of their prophets until they took them as churches and synagogues. The mosque of Quba' has a merit for which it is visited, namely that Allah commanded regarding another built when He mentioned those who built it that they built it to be like it, and Allah revealed in His Book its exposure and what they built it for of intending division among believers, while He left the mosque of Quba' and affirmed it upon what it was, and that was only for merit in it and Allah's pleasure with what its people built it for, then He praised them for that in His Book by His saying: {In it are men who love to purify themselves, and Allah loves the purified}. As for prayer in it, it may be that was due to what was incumbent upon him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) not to sit in it until there was from him therein the prayer he commanded people to do when entering mosques before sitting in them."
"6733","5712 - As Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik narrated to him, from 'Amir ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from 'Amr ibn Sulaym al-Zuraqi, from Abu Qatadah al-Sulami: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you enters the mosque, let him bow two rak'ahs before sitting."
"6734","5713 - And as Yunus narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman: that he heard 'Amir ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from 'Amr ibn Sulaym al-Zuraqi, from Abu Qatadah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"6735","5714 - And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Hind narrated to us, from 'Amir ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from 'Amr ibn Sulaym - and he was a man of bearing - that he heard Abu Qatadah al-Ansari say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the like of it."
"6736","5715 - And as Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, Abu Salamah Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us, Hammam narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Ajlan and Ibn Jurayj, from 'Amir ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from 'Amr ibn Sulaym, from Abu Qatadah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. He said: And Ibn Jurayj added: And let him not sit until he prays."
"6737","5716 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Khalid ibn Abi Yazid narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Yahya ibn 'Umarah, Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban narrated to me, from 'Amr ibn Sulaym al-Zuraqi, from Abu Qatadah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you comes to the mosque, let him not sit until he prays two rak'ahs."
"6738","5717 - And as Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Sabbah narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, from Suhayl, from 'Amir ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from 'Amr ibn Sulaym, from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"6739","5718 - And as Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Sa'd ibn 'Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far al-Ansari narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Yazid ibn Qudayd informed us, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you enters the mosque, let him not sit until he bows two rak'ahs, for Allah will make for him from his rak'ahs in his house good. So what the Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, prayed in the mosque of Quba' was for his entering it, the entering by which he intended sitting in it, so he prayed the prayer he prayed in it likewise, not for other than it. He said: In the hadith of Abu Nuh which you mentioned from 'Ali ibn Ma'bad is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to come to the mosque of Quba' to pray in it, which indicates he intended prayer in it. Our answer to him was that we did not find this hadith in the hadith of anyone who narrated it from Hisham ibn Sa'd except Abu Nuh, and it may be an error from him, for the group is more entitled to preservation than one, and it may be that what is in the hadith, to pray in it, if established, is from the speech of one of its narrators, not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), upon the understanding he took the matter to be that he did not come to sit in it except he prayed in it before sitting. As for his praying voluntary prayer in his house, it is better than prayer in the mosque of Quba', for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he preferred it over prayer in his mosque, said to people when they gathered to him in Ramadan to pray with them in it: O people, pray in your houses, for the best prayer of a man is in his house except the obligatory. And his mosque (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in merit is above the mosque of Quba', so if voluntary prayer in houses is better than prayer in his mosque (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), it is more deserving to be in houses better than in the mosque of Quba'. It has become clear, by Allah's praise, that there is no contradiction in any of these reports we narrated in this chapter. And with Allah is success."
"6740","917 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir about his ordering him to sacrifice an 'atud. 5719 - Al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, Ibn Abi Habib narrated to me, from Abu al-Khayr, from 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave him sheep to divide among his Companions as sacrifices, and an 'atud remained, so he mentioned it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Sacrifice it yourself. An objector said: How do you accept this when the 'atud is only among the young of goats, and the Muslims have agreed one may not sacrifice its like? Our answer to him was that was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a concession from him to 'Uqbah by making that for him, not for others, just as he made for Abu Burdah ibn Niyar to sacrifice a jadha' of goats upon that being for him specifically and not sufficing anyone after him. We mentioned this hadith of Abu Burdah earlier in this book. An objector said: This hadith was narrated from 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir contrary to what is in this hadith you mentioned in this chapter, and he mentioned."
"6741","5720 - Among what Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, 'Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to us, that Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj narrated to him: that Mu'adh ibn 'Abdullah al-Juhani narrated to him, from 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir that he said: We sacrificed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) young sheep. Our answer to him was that what 'Uqbah reported in this hadith of what they sacrificed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) may mean what the group with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) then besides him sacrificed of what 'Uqbah had divided among them by the Messenger's command, then he singled him out with the concession in what he ordered him to sacrifice of the 'atud, though we examined this hadith and found its chain defective, falling short from 'Uqbah."
"6742","5721 - As Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Usamah ibn Zayd informed me, Mu'adh ibn 'Abdullah ibn Habib al-Juhani narrated to me, he said: I asked Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib about the jadha' of sheep, and he said: The practice of the jadha' of sheep was only among you; 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the jadha' of sheep, and he said: Sacrifice it. This hadith returns to Mu'adh ibn 'Abdullah ibn Habib al-Juhani from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib mentioning what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did about sacrificing the jadha' of sheep, so it returns disconnected, and the connected hadith from 'Uqbah returns to the hadith we began with. Since the jadha' is permissible only from sheep specifically in sacrifice, permitting sacrifice with it from other than sheep indicates specificity thereby for whom it was permitted. If an objector says: Do you find a sound hadith about the Messenger of Allah's command to sacrifice the jadha' of sheep? It is said to him: Yes, we found in that a sound hadith, and it is."
"6743","5722 - Among what 'Imran ibn Musa al-Ta'i and 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh al-Basri narrated to us, they said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Do not slaughter but a musinnah, except if it is difficult for you, then slaughter in its place a jadha'ah of sheep. If an objector says: This is evidence sacrifice with the jadha'ah of sheep is permissible only when the musinnah is unavailable, so whence did you permit sacrifice with it when the musinnah exists? Our answer in that was:"
"6744","5723 - That Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Anas ibn 'Iyad informed me, from Muhammad ibn Abi Yahya al-Aslami, from his mother, he said: Umm Bilal al-Aslamiyyah informed me, from her father: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The jadha' of sheep suffices as sacrifice if he has sheep. In this is permission of sacrifice with the jadha' of sheep in all cases. An objector said: It was narrated from 'Uqbah that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to sacrifice was a jadha', not other, and he mentioned."
"6745","5724 - Among what Yahya ibn 'Uthman narrated to us, Nu'aym narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Hisham - the author of al-Dastawa'i - informed us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ba'jah ibn 'Abdullah, from 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divided sacrifices among his Companions, and 'Uqbah received a jadha'ah, so I said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I sacrifice it? He said: Yes. In this hadith is mention of the jadha'ah unrestricted without mention of sheep. We said: This hadith is not connected to 'Uqbah, for Ba'jah ibn 'Abdullah never met 'Uqbah, so the connected hadith from 'Uqbah returns to what Abu al-Khayr narrated from him, and the jadha'ah in this hadith and in Abu al-Khayr's hadith is from goats, upon the concession from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) thereby to 'Uqbah, not upon other than that, and 'Uqbah in that is like Abu Burdah in what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted him to sacrifice of what we mentioned, which he did not permit others."
"6746","918 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of Allah's saying: {If you fear you will not deal justly with the orphans}, the verse, of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions about it. 5725 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Yunus ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me: that he asked 'A'ishah about Allah's saying: {If you fear you will not deal justly with the orphans, then marry what pleases you of women}. She said: O son of my sister, she is the orphan girl in her guardian's care sharing his wealth, whose wealth and beauty please him, and her guardian wants to marry her without being just in her dowry, giving her like what others give her, so they were forbidden to marry them unless they are just to them and reach with them their highest Sunnah of dowry, and they were commanded to marry other women. 'Urwah said: 'A'ishah said: Then people asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for verdict after this verse, so Allah sent down: {They ask you for verdict about women; say Allah gives you verdict about them}, the verse, to His saying: {and you desire to marry them}. She said: What Allah mentioned as recited to you in the Book is the first verse in which is: {If you fear you will not deal justly with the orphans, then marry what pleases you of women}. 'A'ishah said: And Allah's saying in the other verse: {and you desire to marry them} is the desire of one of you away from his orphan in his care when she is of little wealth and beauty, so they were forbidden to marry those they desired for wealth and beauty among female orphans except with justice, because of their turning away from them."
"6747","5726 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Abdullah al-Uwaysi narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sa'd al-Zuhri narrated to us, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Ibn Shihab, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah the like of it, with addition at its end: when they are of little wealth. This hadith was narrated from 'A'ishah via al-Zuhri as we mentioned. It was also narrated from her via Hisham ibn 'Urwah without that."
"6748","As Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Kufi narrated to us, Waki' ibn al-Jarrah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from 'A'ishah: {And what is recited to you in the Book about orphan women whom you do not give what is decreed for them and you desire to marry them}. 'A'ishah said: This is about the orphan girl with a man who knows she would be his partner in his wealth, and he is most entitled to it, so he turns away from her for her wealth so another marries her, lest he share his wealth with her. It was also narrated from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas in its interpretation the like of what was narrated from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her."
"6749","As Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, and as Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, 'Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, they said: 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Amr narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, about His saying: {If you fear you will not deal justly with the orphans, then marry what pleases you of women, two, three and four; but if you fear you will not be just, then one, or what your right hands possess; that is nearer that you not oppress}. Ibn 'Abbas said: Just as you feared you would not be just with orphans, so fear with women when gathered with you that you not be just."
"6750","And as Rawh narrated to us, Hamid ibn Yahya narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, Abu Sa'd narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abi Musa, from Ibn 'Abbas about His saying: {If you fear you will not deal justly with the orphans, then marry what pleases you of women}. He said: If you fear fornication for them, then marry them. In what we narrated from 'A'ishah and Ibn 'Abbas is evidence of the permissibility of marrying orphans, who are those without father, and this confirms what Abu Hanifah and his companions held of permitting guardians of orphans to marry them before puberty, from themselves or others. An objector said: These orphans mentioned in the two verses you recited are orphans who had already reached puberty, named so for their nearness to orphanhood, and they argued with what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6751","5727 - As 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Amr al-Dimashqi Abu Zur'ah narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, from Abu Burdah ibn Abi Musa, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The orphan girl is consulted about herself; if she is silent she has permitted, and if she denies she is not compelled."
"6752","5728 - And as Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, 'Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from Muhammad ibn 'Amr - meaning Ibn 'Alqamah - from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The orphan girl is consulted; if she is pleased, hers is her pleasure, and if she denies there is no compulsion upon her."
"6753","5729 - And as Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Amr, Abu Salamah narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. They said: Seeking permission and consultation is only for one who reached puberty, and in that is evidence the name orphan may be applied to one who reached puberty before that; likewise those called orphan for their nearness to it in the reports you narrated in this chapter are also upon those who reached puberty among those who were orphans before, called orphan for their nearness to it. Our answer to him was that the matter is not as he mentioned, and in the two verses we recited in these reports we narrated is what indicates orphans not yet pubescent were meant, for in them their guardians were forbidden to marry them unless they reach with them the highest of their women in dowry; had they been pubescent, their matter in dowry would return to what they accept, little or much, not to other, for Allah said: {Give women their dowries graciously; but if they willingly remit to you part of it, consume it wholesomely}, and if they may remit it to their husbands after its obligation, they may do so before its obligation by marriage contracted upon what they accepted, even more so. Allah's preventing them from that in the two verses indicates they are orphans without consent, namely not pubescent. That is confirmed by what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) earlier in this book in the chapter of no divorce except after marriage, of his saying: There is no orphanhood after puberty, thereby negating orphanhood after puberty. This objector said: What then is the meaning of the two hadiths of Abu Musa and Abu Hurayrah you mentioned after negating puberty from the orphans therein, with confirmation of seeking permission and consultation? Our answer was that it may mean orphans who understand and from whom is known what their hearts incline to of their welfare or turn away from of what has no welfare, though not pubescent; perhaps some of them, though not pubescent, have better choice than others who reached puberty, yet Allah's command in His creation is not exceeded in them or others; it behooves their guardians when marrying them to consider what is with them of inclination or aversion, for they know of themselves what others do not, so what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded in these two hadiths about them is upon this meaning, not other. When that is negated, the permissibility of guardians marrying orphans before puberty stands, as those scholars we mentioned held. What was narrated from 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, in his view on marrying orphans before puberty also indicates this meaning, indicating his interpretation of the two verses was as 'A'ishah's and Ibn 'Abbas's."
"6754","As Yahya ibn 'Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abi al-Haytham informed us, Abu Dawud Yazid al-Awdi informed us, he said: I was with 'Ali after 'Asr when a man was brought and they said: We found him in the ruin of Murad with a girl whose shirt was stained with blood. He said to him: Woe to you, what is this you did? He said: May Allah set right the Commander of the Believers, she was my cousin, an orphan in my care, rich in wealth, and I am an old man without wealth, so I feared if she attained what women attain she would turn away from me, so I married her. He said: And she was weeping. He said: Did you marry him? One among the people with him said to her: Say yes, and another said: Say no. She said: Yes, I married him. He said: Take your wife by the hand. What 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, did in this hadith indicates his interpretation of the two verses we recited was like that of 'A'ishah and Ibn 'Abbas we mentioned, and in that is evidence of the permissibility of a man marrying from himself one he is guardian of, as Abu Hanifah, Malik and their companions say, contrary to one who says a man cannot marry from himself just as he cannot sell to himself. In 'Ali's hadith we mentioned is evidence that the word of one to whom a marriage contract belongs who reports: I contracted it, is accepted, as those scholars say, among them Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, contrary to what Abu Hanifah said therein, that it is not accepted from him except with proof established thereupon."
"6755","919 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the intent of Allah's saying: {That is nearer that you not oppress}. 5730 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ibrahim - meaning Duhaym - narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Shu'ayb ibn Shabur narrated to us, from 'Umar ibn Muhammad, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about His saying: {That is nearer that you not oppress}. He said: That you not act unjustly. We know no one who narrated this hadith except from this route, and it is a praiseworthy route, for 'Umar ibn Muhammad around whom it revolves has distinction evident to scholars; Malik and more than one of his likes narrated from him, and Ibn Wahb narrated from no Medinan more eminent than him, and he is 'Umar ibn Muhammad ibn Zayd ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar ibn al-Khattab. We narrated from Ibn 'Abbas in its interpretation also this very view in the chapter before this chapter, and this is not said by opinion but only by directive. It was narrated from Ibn 'Abbas from another route."
"6756","As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Abu 'Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, Khalid ibn 'Abdullah narrated to us, from 'Ata - meaning Ibn al-Sa'ib - from 'Amir, from Ibn 'Abbas: {That is nearer that you not oppress}. He said: That you not incline. We know it was not narrated from any Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in its interpretation other than this view. It was also narrated from more than one Follower in its interpretation the like thereof."
"6757","As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from al-Zubayr ibn al-Khirrit, from 'Ikrimah about this verse: {That is nearer that you not oppress}. He said: That you not incline. He said: And he recited us a verse of poetry he claimed Abu Talib said."
"6758","And as Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, Mughirah informed us, from Ibrahim: {That is nearer that you not oppress}. He said: That you not incline."
"6759","And as Yusuf narrated to us, Sa'id narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, Husayn informed us, from Abu Malik, the like of it. We know no Follower from whom other than this interpretation was narrated in its interpretation except Zayd ibn Aslam, for it was narrated from him in its interpretation that it concerns that their dependents not multiply, and this is a view the linguists claim is corrupt, for had it been as Zayd said it would be: that you not become dependent. And with Allah is success."
"6760","920 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: The previously married woman is more entitled to herself than her guardian, and the virgin is consulted, and her permission is her silence. 5731 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik informed him. 5732 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq and Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, they said: 'Abdullah ibn Maslamah al-Qa'nabi narrated to us. 5733 - And Muhammad ibn al-'Abbas ibn al-Rabi' al-Lu'lu'i narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Maslamah al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, they said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Fadl, from Nafi' ibn Jubayr ibn Mut'im, from Ibn 'Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The previously married woman is more entitled to herself than her guardian, and the virgin is consulted about herself, and her permission is her silence. Thus Malik narrated this hadith from 'Abdullah ibn al-Fadl, and Ziyad ibn Sa'd narrated it from 'Abdullah ibn al-Fadl but fell short of some wordings Malik narrated from him."
"6761","5734 - As al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Sa'd, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Fadl: he heard Nafi' ibn Jubayr narrating from Ibn 'Abbas: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The previously married woman is more entitled to herself than her guardian, and the virgin is consulted. Salih ibn Kaysan also narrated it from Nafi' ibn Jubayr with addition over what 'Abdullah ibn al-Fadl narrated from him."
"6762","5735 - As Farwah ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Nafi' ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The father has no authority with the previously married woman, and the virgin is consulted, and her permission is her silence. Salih added over 'Abdullah ibn al-Fadl in this hadith of his: The father has no authority with the previously married woman. Ibn Mawhab also narrated this hadith from Nafi' ibn Jubayr."
"6763","5736 - As al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: Ibn Mawhab narrated to us. 5737 - And as al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad narrated to us, 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, al-Husayn said in his hadith: from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abdullah ibn Mawhab, then they agreed, and said: from Nafi' ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then they mentioned the like of Malik's hadith from 'Abdullah ibn al-Fadl. We reflected on this hadith to ascertain what is meant thereby, and the apparent meaning of what is in the hadith of Ziyad, Malik and Ibn Mawhab is that the previously married woman is more entitled to herself than her guardian, with no authority for her guardian over her, including her father and other guardians. What is in Salih ibn Kaysan's hadith confirmed the inclusion of her father therein. In that is evidence the virgin's case is likewise, and her father is among those commanded not to marry her off until he seeks her permission, just as he is commanded with the previously married woman not to marry her off until she is consulted. In that is evidence that if a virgin's father marries her off before seeking her permission, abandoning what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him therein, that marriage is not binding upon her until she approves it, as those who say it say, among Abu Hanifah, Sufyan and their companions. We found this meaning likewise in other than Ibn 'Abbas's hadith."
"6764","5738 - As 'Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj who said: I heard Ibn Abi Mulaykah say: Dhakwan the freedman of 'A'ishah said: I heard 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, say: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the girl her family marries off: Is she consulted or not? He said: Yes, she is consulted. I said: She is shy and silent. He said: That is her permission when she is silent."
"6765","5739 - And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. In this hadith is the Messenger of Allah's command to seek the virgin's permission as in it is his command to consult the previously married woman; since the father was commanded to consult the previously married woman as others of her guardians consult her, he is likewise with the virgin in what he was commanded of seeking her permission like others of her guardians."
"6766","5740 - And as Abu Bakrah Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Abu Dawud narrated to us, Hisham al-Dastawa'i narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The previously married woman is not married until she is consulted, nor the virgin until she is permitted. They said: And how is her permission, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Silence."
"6767","5741 - And as Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Maymun al-Baghdadi narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us. 5742 - And as Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj al-Hadrami and al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, they said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, they said: Al-Awza'i informed us, Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"6768","5743 - And as Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Husayn, from 'Adi ibn 'Adi al-Kindi, from his father 'Adi, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The previously married woman expresses herself, and the virgin's consent is her silence."
"6769","5744 - And as Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, from Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth, from al-Layth, then with his chain the like of it."
"6770","5745 - And as Yahya ibn 'Uthman narrated to us, 'Amr ibn al-Rabi' ibn Tariq narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'Adi ibn 'Adi, from his father, from al-'Urs - and he is Ibn 'Umayrah - who was among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. In these reports is what necessitates that the father in marrying his virgin pubescent daughter is like others of her guardians, and he may not contract marriage over her before her consent. Jarir ibn Hazim narrated in this meaning."
"6771","5746 - Among what Abu Umayyah and Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, they said: Al-Husayn ibn Muhammad al-Marwazi narrated to us, Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, from Ayyub, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas: that a man married off his daughter while she was a virgin and unwilling, so she came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he gave her choice. In this hadith is evidence the virgin's father has no contracting over her private part without her consent. An objector said: Sufyan narrated this hadith from Ayyub and differed from Jarir therein."
"6772","5747 - He mentioned among what Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, Waki' narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, from 'Ikrimah: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) separated a man and his wife whom her father married off while she was unwilling, and she was previously married. In that is what necessitates the corruption of this hadith in chain and text: as for chain, its disconnection and falling short of Ibn 'Abbas; as for text, its mentioning she was previously married while Jarir's hadith says she was virgin. Our answer was that when we find narrations, what necessitates their verification and what necessitates their contradiction, they are to be taken upon verification not contradiction; Jarir's hadith is upon her being virgin and Sufyan's upon her being previously married, so each may be upon a different case so they do not contradict. Some who hold the father's marrying the virgin to the view we mentioned in this chapter also argue for his view."
"6773","5748 - With what Ahmad ibn Abi 'Imran, Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud and 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, they said: Abu Salih al-Hakam ibn Musa informed us, Shu'ayb ibn Ishaq al-Dimashqi informed us, from al-Awza'i, from 'Ata, from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah: that a man married off his daughter while virgin without her command, so she came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he separated them. This hadith was not among what may be argued with, since its root is."
"6774","5749 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, 'Amr ibn Abi Salamah narrated to us, al-Awza'i narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Murrah, from 'Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with that, so this hadith is corrupted by Ibrahim ibn Murrah entering between al-Awza'i and 'Ata, and it also confirms its agreement upon 'Ata not exceeding to Jabir. Since the matter is as we mentioned in the reports, we found reflection also necessitates what we mentioned of the father's authority over the virgin ceasing in contracting her private part without her command: since he has no contracting over her wealth after puberty as he had before puberty, likewise in contracting her private part he has no such after puberty, so his ruling therein after puberty differs from before puberty. We found Allah's Book indicates that by His saying: {But if they willingly remit to you part of it, consume it wholesomely}, so she may by this verse remit to her husband what she wills of her dowry, with no objection from her father therein, which indicates he has no objection either in her private part in contracting marriage without her permission. In Allah's Book also is what indicates that, His saying: {And for you is half of what your wives leave if they have no child}, then He said: {But if they have a child, for you is a fourth of what they leave, after a bequest they bequeath or debt}. Since they are in their bequests in their wealth like men in their bequests, they are like men in their wealth and in its permissibility and the removal of hands from them therein, which indicates their removal from them in their private parts. An objector said: You narrated from Ibn 'Abbas from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about virgin and previously married what you narrated in this chapter in which is that the previously married woman is more entitled to herself than her guardian, and in that is negation of her guardian having right in her private part, and you mentioned that in what you narrated in Ma'mar's hadith from Salih ibn Kaysan: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The father has no authority with the previously married woman in her private part. And it was narrated from Ibn 'Abbas in his view after the Prophet, upon him be peace, what contradicts that,"
"6775","He mentioned among what Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Ta'ifi informed us, 'Abdullah ibn 'Uthman ibn Khuthaym narrated to me, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr who said: I said to 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas: Is a woman married without her guardian's permission? Ibn 'Abbas said: A woman is not married except with a guardian's permission or the ruler's. He said: I said: She has no patron, or her patron perished. He said: Then the ruler. He said: I repeated that to him until he became angry. In this is evidence that Ibn 'Abbas's hadith you narrated from him from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), if sound, was abrogated by what is in this hadith, for Ibn 'Abbas would not oppose what he took from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except with what is worthier thereof taken from him. Our answer was that the matter is not as he imagined; rather what belongs to the woman in the hadith of Nafi' ibn Jubayr ibn Mut'im from Ibn 'Abbas is that it is to her as she wishes to do therein, of what was made to her to delegate to other men in charge of her so one she delegates contracts her upon her command from one she approves, so that contracting from him upon her by her command is a contracting from her thereof upon herself, for contracts of agents are attributed to those who command them, as a man says: I did such, for what was done by his command. It is thus cleared that nothing we mentioned from Ibn 'Abbas from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and what we mentioned from him after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), contains contradiction, and the guardian's right in what was narrated from Ibn 'Abbas in what he said after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is what the woman gave him of what was made for her to give him, with no objection for him upon her in a contract without her command."
"6776","921 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his accepting from al-'Abbas his marrying him to Maymunah. 5750 - Abu Ja'far said: Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata, from Ibn 'Abbas that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Maymunah while in ihram; she gave her affair to al-'Abbas, so he married her to him. An objector said in this hadith: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) accepted al-'Abbas's marrying him to Maymunah though he is not her guardian, while in the hadith of Nafi' ibn Jubayr from him which you interpreted in the first chapter as you interpreted it, and which you verified, and the hadith of Sa'id ibn Jubayr from him thereupon, this contradicts that of the woman's sole contracting marriage upon herself without her guardian's command. Our answer was that Maymunah may have had none of her guardians present, with no guardian then among her people due to their religions differing from hers, so her affair returned to one to whom her private part's guardianship belongs, namely the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); she may have initiated by giving it to al-'Abbas, so al-'Abbas contracted it upon her and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) accepted it from him, which was an enforcement from him of what she had given to al-'Abbas. In that is evidence of the permissibility of contracts for things belonging to another than the contractor upon the approval of whom they belong to, as those scholars say, among them Abu Hanifah, Malik, al-Thawri and their companions."
"6777","922 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying to Umm Salamah when he proposed to her and she said: None of my guardians is present; 'Umar is small, not pubescent, so he married her to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by her command. 5751 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us, Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, from Thabit al-Bunani who said: Ibn Umm Salamah narrated to me: that when Umm Salamah's waiting period ended, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to her proposing, and she said: Welcome to the Messenger of Allah; I have three traits: I am a very jealous woman, I am a woman with children, and I am a woman none of whose guardians is present to marry me. 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, became angry for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and came to her saying: Do you reject the Messenger of Allah? She said: O son of al-Khattab, in me is such and such. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to her and said: As for your jealousy, I will pray Allah removes it from you; as for your children, Allah will suffice them; as for your saying none of your guardians is present to marry you, none of your guardians present or absent dislikes me. She said to her son: Marry the Messenger of Allah. So he married him. An objector said in this chapter: Umm Salamah said to the Prophet, upon him be peace: None of her guardians is present, and he did not deny that of her saying nor say to her: Do you have a guardian besides yourself? In that is evidence contrary to what you verified the hadith of Ibn 'Abbas upon of negating the guardian from the previously married woman. Our answer was that there is nothing therein contradicting verifying Ibn 'Abbas's hadith as we verified it, and it negates the woman contracting marriage upon herself, even previously married, until she delegates it to another man."
"6778","5752 - And 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah informed us. 5753 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thabit al-Bunani, 'Umar ibn Abi Salamah narrated to me, from his father, from his mother Umm Salamah, then he mentioned this hadith. An objector said: In this hadith is Hammad ibn Salamah's inserting in its chain an unknown man, namely Ibn 'Umar ibn Abi Salamah. Our answer was that the root of the hadith is upon Thabit taking it from 'Umar ibn Abi Salamah by hearing with no intruder between them; thus Ja'far ibn Sulayman al-Duba'i narrated it."
"6779","5754 - As Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Malik ibn Zanjawayh narrated to us, 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Ja'far ibn Sulayman informed us, Thabit al-Bunani narrated to us, 'Umar ibn Abi Salamah narrated to me, from his mother Umm Salamah, from her husband Abu Salamah who said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: No Muslim afflicted with calamity says: To Allah we belong and to Him we return; O Allah, I seek reward for my calamity with You, so replace it for me with better than it, but Allah replaces it for him with better than it. When Abu Salamah died I said: To Allah we belong and to Him we return; O Allah, I sought reward in my calamity, so replace him for me with better than him. She said: And I said to myself: Who is better than Abu Salamah? Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came and proposed to me, and I married him. This hadith indicates the root of the hadith is from 'Umar ibn Abi Salamah with no intruder between them. Zuhayr ibn al-'Ala' agreed with Ja'far ibn Sulayman in this hadith's chain also that it is from Thabit from 'Umar ibn Abi Salamah."
"6780","5755 - As 'Ali ibn al-Husayn ibn Harb narrated to us, Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn al-'Ala' narrated to us, Thabit al-Bunani narrated to us, from 'Umar ibn Abi Salamah, from Umm Salamah, then he mentioned this hadith with its meaning except he said, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you is afflicted with calamity, let him say: To Allah we belong and to Him we return, without his mentioning Abu Salamah therein. The rest of this hadith is like Hammad ibn Salamah's hadith in text. What is in this hadith of her son 'Umar contracting marriage upon her, though he is not her guardian for he was a child, is upon the meaning of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did in his marrying Maymunah, and upon that since Umm Salamah had no guardian present with her and her affair was to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), it may be he gave her to give that to whom she saw, so she gave it to her son; or she did so initially and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) accepted it from her son, which was an enforcement from him of it. In this chapter of jurisprudence is evidence that children's contracts for things by the affairs of adults are permissible as Abu Hanifah and his companions say, for 'Umar ibn Abi Salamah was small when he contracted marriage upon his mother, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) accepted it. An objector said: Perhaps 'Umar was pubescent then. Our answer was that in Umm Salamah's hadith we mentioned is what negated that, by her saying to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): None of my guardians is present, for had he been pubescent he would be among her guardians, either as her son as some scholars say, among them Abu Yusuf, or as her cousin's son, so a guardian inevitably. The Prophet's not denying her saying indicates he was not pubescent. What we mentioned earlier in this book of Hammad ibn Zayd's hadith from Hisham ibn 'Urwah from his father from 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr who said: I and 'Umar ibn Abi Salamah were in a fortress of Hassan; he would stoop for me to look and I would stoop for him to look; I said to my father: I saw you roaming then, and he said: O my son, the Prophet, upon him be peace, had gathered for me therein my parents, also indicates he was small. Some genealogists claimed he was among those born in Abyssinia, and Allah knows its truth. An objector said: What contract of a child is valid when he has no authority therein over himself, so more deserving to have none over others? This is argued by one holding al-Shafi'i's meaning. Our answer: What is among children's affairs was not all made as no affairs; how can it be so when the one arguing against us with this cause is among those who give a seven-year-old child choice when his mother is divorced between his father and mother, narrating therein what was narrated of what we already narrated earlier in this book, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave him choice only with ruling for his choice. In this is what Muslims agreed upon about a child under a hand who cannot express himself: if the possessor says: He is my slave, then the child matures and raises that, his raising is as no raising and he is his slave; had he expressed himself though not pubescent and repelled that from himself claiming freedom, the word is his. Malik ibn Anas said about the bequest of a youth not pubescent: it is permissible, narrating therein what he narrated, and did not make it as no bequest for falling short of puberty. It was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far what confirmed what we held."
"6781","5756 - As Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Nasr ibn 'Ali narrated to me, 'Abdullah ibn Dawud narrated to us, from Fitr - meaning Ibn Khalifah - who said: I heard my father narrate, he said: I heard 'Amr ibn Hurayth narrate, he said: I went with my father to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he marked for me a house with a bow. He said: And he passed by 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far while he was selling some of what boys sell, and said: May Allah bless your bargain, or the bargain of your right hand. This may be that his selling was by the Prophet's permitting that to him. In what we mentioned is evidence that had the bargain not been from him due to minority until puberty, the Prophet's praying for blessing in his bargain would mention that when he matures; his leaving that indicates he has a bargain though not pubescent, by the permission of his guardian thereof to him. It is established by what we mentioned the permissibility of discerning children's contracts by the affairs of those under their guardianship, and the enforcement of contracts in what they contracted upon its owners, and the word therein is as we mentioned from its permitters, not as their opponents mentioned. And Allah knows best."
"6782","923 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: The believer's dream is part of the parts he informed are thereof from prophethood. Abu Ja'far said: We mentioned this chapter and the reports therein with chains from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) earlier in this book, except we brought this chapter here to reflect on his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in these reports: that dreams are part of the parts he informed are thereof part of prophethood, to ascertain what is meant, Allah willing. It was understood the parts mentioned in these reports that they are prophethood mean they are what he used to see apart from prophethood, not that they were prophethood itself, for those who saw them had been prophets before that; they saw them during their prophethood. Evidence that is so is what the Prophet, upon him be peace, informed about others' dreams that they are part of those parts, though that is not that others besides prophets by what they see in sleep deserve part of prophethood's parts whereby they deserve its share of prophethood; rather its meaning is the meaning we mentioned therein, and Allah knows best, and it is Arabic speech the addressed understand. Among what also indicates what we mentioned: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was the seal of prophets, blessings of Allah upon them all, and since he is their seal it is impossible anything of prophethood remained after him. Among what also indicates what we mentioned of what he informed remains after him of prophethood is what his Companions addressed thereby understood from him. 5757 - As Ahmad ibn al-Husayn al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: I heard Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah say: I heard Sulayman ibn Suhaym, from Ibrahim ibn 'Abdullah ibn Ma'bad, from his father, from Ibn 'Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) lifted the curtain while people were in rows behind Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: Nothing remained of the heralds of prophethood except the righteous dream seen by the Muslim or seen for him."
"6783","5758 - And as Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Abu 'Umar al-Darir narrated to us, Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah informed us, Sulayman ibn Suhaym informed us. 5759 - And as Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, al-Firyabi narrated to us, Ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Suhaym, they both said: from Ibrahim ibn 'Abdullah ibn Ma'bad ibn 'Abbas, from his father, from Ibn 'Abbas, then he mentioned the like of it."
"6784","5760 - And as Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Suhaym, from Ibrahim ibn 'Abdullah ibn Ma'bad, from his father, from Ibn 'Abbas, then he mentioned the like of it."
"6785","5761 - And as Ahmad ibn Hammad al-Tujibi narrated to us, Ibn Abi Maryam informed us, then Ibn al-Darawardi narrated to me, Sulayman ibn Suhaym the freedman of Al Jubayr narrated to me, from Ibrahim ibn 'Abdullah, from his father, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ascended the pulpit while Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, was leading the people, and said: O Allah, have I conveyed? O Allah, have I conveyed? O people, nothing remains after me of the heralds of prophethood except the righteous dream seen by the righteous man or seen for him. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed that what remains after him of the heralds of prophethood is the righteous dream mentioned in this hadith, which indicates dreams are only among the heralds of prophethood, meaning among what those of prophethood give glad tidings to whoever follows it as it is, not that they are themselves prophethood. And with Allah is success."
"6786","924 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas of his saying: The dreams of prophets were revelation, of what we know certainly he did not say by opinion but said from taking it whence its like is taken. 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, Qabisah ibn 'Uqbah narrated to us, Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas about His saying: {Indeed I saw eleven stars}. He said: The dreams of prophets, upon them be peace, were revelation. The best of what occurred to us in its interpretation is that the dreams of prophets, blessings of Allah upon them, were among what Allah revealed to them: He reveals to them in their sleep what He wills to reveal to them therein, and reveals to them in their wakefulness what He wills to reveal to them therein, and all of that is revelation from Him to them, making some of it in their sleep and some in their wakefulness."
"6787","925 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas of what is known certainly he did not say by opinion but said by directive: There is no revelation except the Qur'an. Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Rufay' who said: I heard Ibn 'Abbas say: There is no revelation except the Qur'an. An objector said: How do you accept the like of this from Ibn 'Abbas while you narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what contradicts it, in his informing people what Allah reveals to him besides the Qur'an? 5762 - He mentioned in that among what Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, from 'Isa ibn 'Asim, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Anas ibn Malik who said: We prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the dawn prayer, and while in prayer he extended his hand then withdrew it. When he finished prayer we said: O Messenger of Allah, you did in this prayer what you did not do before? He said: Paradise was shown to me, and I saw therein a vine whose clusters were near, its grains like gourds, and I wanted to take from it, but it was revealed to it: Withdraw, so it withdrew. Then the Fire was shown to me between me and you until I saw my shadow and your shadows, so I gestured to you to withdraw, but it was revealed to me: Let them stay, for you submitted and they submitted, you emigrated and they emigrated, so I see no merit for me over you except prophethood."
"6788","5763 - And among what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, 'Ata - meaning Ibn Abi Rabah - narrated to us, from Safwan ibn Ya'la ibn Umayyah: that a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wearing a cloak with traces of khaluq or yellowness upon it, while he was at Ji'ranah, and said: How do you command me to do in my 'umrah? He said: Revelation descended upon the Prophet, upon him be peace. Safwan said: I said to 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him: I wished I had seen the Prophet, upon him be peace, while revelation descended upon him. When revelation overcame him he called me and said: Would you like to look at the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while revelation descended upon him? He lifted the edge of the garment and I looked at him snorting like a young camel's snorting. When it was lifted from him he said: Where is the questioner about 'umrah? Remove your cloak, wash off the yellowness or khaluq, and do in your 'umrah as you did in your hajj. This objector said: In these two reports is mention of revelation revealed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which is not Qur'an. Our answer was that what we narrated from Ibn 'Abbas means in our view - and Allah knows best - not to repel what is in these two reports; rather it came upon how Arabs address one another so the addressed understand them. Ibn 'Abbas meant in our view by his saying: No revelation except the Qur'an, the Qur'an itself and what the Qur'an commands of what is accepted only by the Qur'an, for Allah said: {Whatever the Messenger gives you, take it, and whatever he forbids you, refrain from}, and that is Ibn 'Abbas's intent, as was 'Ali ibn Abi Talib's intent - may Allah be pleased with him - in what he addressed to Abu Juhayfah."
"6789","5764 - As al-Muzani narrated to us, al-Shafi'i narrated to us. 5765 - And as al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Muzani said in his hadith from al-Shafi'i: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us; al-Rabi' said in his hadith from Asad: Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to us; then they agreed and said: from Mutarrif ibn Tarif, from al-Sha'bi, from Abu Juhayfah who said: I asked 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: Do you have from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) anything besides the Qur'an? He said: No, by Him who splits the grain and creates the soul, we have nothing from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) besides the Qur'an, except that Allah gives a servant understanding in the Qur'an, and what is in the leaf. He said: I said: And what is in the leaf? He said: Blood-money, freeing the captive, and that no Muslim is killed for a disbeliever. 'Ali had said this and sworn thereupon while he had from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of Sunnah what he had, among it the revelation revealed to him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) besides the Qur'an, included in the Qur'an whose acceptance from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is by the Qur'an's command thereof to them. Likewise what Ibn 'Abbas said: No revelation besides the Qur'an, meaning thereby that since the Qur'an was at the highest ranks of revelation, among it the Qur'an and among it other than the Qur'an, he said of its loftiness: This Qur'an, as a man says: There is no scholar but so-and-so. And as one said: There is no ascetic but 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz, for his abandoning what came to him of worldliness which others would not abandon less than, though there are many ascetics except they could not do the like 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz could thereof, so they asceticized therein like 'Umar's asceticism."
"6790","926 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in interpreting Allah's saying: Indeed We granted you a clear conquest. Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Abu Ghassan narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, Abu Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Al-Bara' said: As for us, we call what you call the conquest of Makkah the day of Hudaybiyah, the Pledge of Ridwan. And Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas: {Indeed We granted you a clear conquest}. He said: Hudaybiyah."
"6791","5766 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, 'Abd al-A'la ibn Hammad narrated to us, Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Sa'id - meaning Ibn Abi 'Arubah - narrated to us, from Qatadah that he narrated to them, he said: Anas ibn Malik narrated to us: that it was sent down upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on his return from Hudaybiyah, meaning: {Indeed We granted you a clear conquest, that Allah may forgive you your former and latter sin}, while his Companions were mingled with grief and gloom, having been barred from their rites, and they slaughtered the sacrificial animals at Hudaybiyah. The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A verse was sent down upon me dearer to me than the whole world. The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited it, and a man among the people said: Congratulations, O Messenger of Allah, Allah has clarified what He will do with you, so what will He do with us? So Allah sent down: {That He may admit the believing men and women into gardens beneath which rivers flow, abiding therein, and expiate their misdeeds; and that is with Allah a great triumph}. So Allah clarified what He will do with His Prophet and what He will do with them."
"6792","5767 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, 'Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, Qatadah narrated to us, from Anas, and he mentioned the like of it."
"6793","5768 - And Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Iyas Mu'awiyah ibn Qurrah who said: I heard 'Abdullah ibn Mughaffal say: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of the Conquest upon a she-camel or camel, traveling, reciting Surat al-Fath; then Abu Iyas recited a soft recitation, then repeated with trill, then said: Had I not feared people gathering upon us I would have recited that melody, and he had repeated with trill."
"6794","5769 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Iyas Mu'awiyah ibn Qurrah, from 'Abdullah ibn Mughaffal who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it."
"6795","5770 - And 'Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Shu'bah, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. People agreed the conquest mentioned in the verse we recited is what was of Hudaybiyah of the truce between the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the people of Makkah which was cause for its conquest. In this is evidence it may be said something occurred when near occurring, as is said: We entered such city when near entering it though in reality they had not entered it. Among that is what Muslims applied to one of Ibrahim's two sons (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as the sacrificed, not because he was sacrificed but for his nearness to sacrifice. That indicates Arabs may apply realities of things whose attainment and completion of causes is near, though a remainder they await after that remains. And with Allah is success."
"6796","927 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated about the reason for which it was said: The Pledge of Ridwan; its cause was 'Uthman ibn 'Affan despite his absence from it. 5771 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Buhlul narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn Idris al-Awdi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from 'Urwah, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Marwan ibn al-Hakam in the hadith of Hudaybiyah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had sent Khirash ibn Umayyah al-Khuza'i to Makkah, mounting him on a camel of his called al-Tha'lab. When he entered, Quraysh betrayed, wanting to kill Khirash, and the Ahabish protected him until he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He called 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, to send him to Makkah's people, and he said: O Messenger of Allah, I fear Quraysh for myself, and there is none of 'Adi ibn Ka'b there to protect me; Quraysh know my enmity to them and harshness upon them, but I direct you to a man dearer therein than I: 'Uthman ibn 'Affan. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called him and sent him to Quraysh informing them he did not come for war, but came visiting this House honoring its sanctity. 'Uthman went out to Makkah, and Aban ibn Sa'id ibn al-'As met him, dismounted, carried him and took him as companion, and granted him protection to convey the Messenger's message. 'Uthman went to Abu Sufyan and Quraysh's chiefs and conveyed from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what he sent him with. They said to 'Uthman: If you wish to circumambulate the House, do so. He said: I would not do so until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) circumambulates. Quraysh detained him, and it reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the Muslims that 'Uthman was killed. Ibn Ishaq said: 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr informed me that when it reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that 'Uthman was killed, the Pledge of Ridwan occurred. Then it came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that what was mentioned of 'Uthman's affair was false. In this hadith is that pledge was then when it reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what reached him of Makkah's people concerning 'Uthman."
"6797","5772 - As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, from Abu Ja'far al-Razi, from al-Rabi' ibn Anas, from Abu al-'Aliyah and others, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Mughaffal who said: We pledged to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) under the tree upon not fleeing."
"6798","5773 - And as Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, Mahdi ibn Ja'far narrated to us, Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi 'Ubayd, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa' who said: I said to him: Upon what did you pledge to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Upon not fleeing. That pledge from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to people was due to what reached him of Makkah's people concerning 'Uthman, so it was said he was cause for it thereby."
"6799","5774 - As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, 'Isa ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Kulayb ibn Wa'il narrated to us, Hani' ibn Qays narrated to me, from Habib ibn Abi Mulaykah who said: I was sitting beside Ibn 'Umar when a man came and said: O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, inform me about 'Uthman ibn 'Affan: did he witness Badr? He said: No. He said: Did he witness the Pledge of Ridwan? He said: No. He said: Was he among those who turned away the day the two hosts met? He said: Yes. The man turned away. A man said to 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar: He will go and tell people you disparaged 'Uthman. He said: Did I do so?! Ibn 'Umar said: Bring the man to me, so he returned him. He said: Do you know what I said to you? He said: Yes: you asked me did 'Uthman witness Badr? I said: No. You asked me did 'Uthman witness the Pledge of Ridwan? I said: No. You asked me was he among those who turned away the day the two hosts met? I said: Yes. Ibn 'Umar said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on Badr day: 'Uthman went forth for Allah's need and His Messenger's need, so he struck for him a share though he struck for no other absentee; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him on the day of the Pledge of Ridwan while intending to enter Makkah. He said: 'Uthman went forth for Allah's and His Messenger's need, and I pledge to Allah for him, and he clapped one hand upon the other. Allah said: {Those of you who turned away the day the two hosts met, Satan only caused them to slip for some of what they earned, and Allah pardoned them; indeed Allah is Forgiving, Forbearing}. Allah pardoned him, so exert yourself."
"6800","5775 - And as Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn 'Amr al-Azdi narrated to us, Abu Ishaq al-Fazari narrated to us, he narrated to me from Kulayb ibn Wa'il, from Hani' ibn Qays, from Habib ibn Abi Mulaykah al-Nahdi who said: I was sitting with Ibn 'Umar when a man came to him and said: O 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, did 'Uthman witness the Pledge of Ridwan? He said: No. He said: Was he among those who turned away the day the two hosts met? He said: Yes. The man turned away. A man said to 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar: He will go and tell people you disparaged 'Uthman, then he mentioned the hadith. It became clear that 'Uthman had in that pledge despite absence what none who witnessed it besides him had, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pledged for him and clapped his hand upon his hand; what merit like this merit he had in the Pledge of Ridwan."
"6801","928 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying to the group among whom was Samurah: The last of you to die will be in the Fire. 5776 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Basri al-Tammar narrated to us, Mu'adh ibn Mu'adh narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Maslamah, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Hurayrah: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to ten of his Companions among whom was Samurah: The last of you to die will be in the Fire."
"6802","5777 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated to us, Abu Hilal al-Rasibi narrated to us, Jabir ibn 'Amr Abu al-Wazi' narrated to us, from Abu Amin, from Abu Hurayrah who said: I, 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar and Samurah went seeking the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); it was said: He headed toward the mosque of piety, so we came to him, and he had come placing his hand on Abu Bakr's shoulder, may Allah be pleased with him, and the other on 'Umar's shoulder, may Allah be pleased with him. When we saw him we sat. He said: Who are these? Abu Bakr said to him: This is Abu Hurayrah, 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, then Samurah."
"6803","5778 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: al-Aswad ibn 'Amir narrated to us, and Abu Hilal al-Rasibi narrated to us, from Jabir Abu al-Wazi', from Amin - thus in Abu Ja'far's book, the transmitter from Abu Umayyah, while some of our companions say: from Abu Amin - from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, then he mentioned the like of it."
"6804","5779 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Fahd ibn 'Awf narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from 'Ali ibn Zayd, from Aws ibn Khalid who said: Whenever I came to Abu Mahdhurah I asked him about Samurah, and whenever I came to Samurah he asked me about Abu Mahdhurah. I said to Abu Mahdhurah: You ask about him and he asks about you?! He said: I, Abu Hurayrah and Samurah were in the Prophet's house (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he took the two doorposts and said: The last of you to die will be in the Fire. Abu Hurayrah died, then Abu Mahdhurah died, then Samurah died."
"6805","5780 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah ibn Sa'id, from a man called Hajar who said: I came to Medina to Abu Mahdhurah and he said: From where are you? I said: From Basrah. He said: What happened to Samurah ibn Jundub? I said: He is alive. He said: There is none on earth I love to live longer than he; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me and him: The last of you to die will be in the Fire. He narrated another time saying: He said to me, Hudhayfah and him: The last of you to die will be in the Fire. Abu Ja'far said: Al-Bukhari mentioned 'Ubaydullah ibn Sa'id, the narrator of this hadith, by Sharik's narration from him, and did not mention him otherwise. We reflected on these reports seeking what is meant thereby, and found his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned therein to whom he said it may mean by the fire he mentioned the fire of this world, so that is a merit for whom the saying befell among his Companions, for thereby he is of the kind he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed are among his nation's martyrs, as we mentioned from him earlier in this book; or it may be upon the fire of the Hereafter, so that is punishment for whom the saying befell for what he did in this world, then Allah returns his affair to what He returns the affairs of His monotheist servants He admits to the Fire. Hence the Prophet's Companions, may Allah be pleased with them, whom he addressed with that saying cared when some asked about the life and death of others, to know by what he ascertains his safety from that meaning or its befalling him. Since the last of them to die was Samurah, it was known he is meant by those reports to him; his death was in fire, not that he is among the people of the Fire."
"6806","As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Marwan ibn Ja'far narrated to us, Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar al-Bakrawi narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn 'Ubaydullah ibn al-Rabi' al-Ziyadi who said: We said to Muhammad ibn Sirin: O Abu Bakr, inform us about Samurah, what was his affair and what was said about him? He said: Samurah was afflicted with severe lockjaw so he could hardly get warm; a great pot was brought, filled with water, fire kindled beneath it, and he made above it a seat, climbing to it to feel its heat warming him. While so, it collapsed with him, and it was seen that is it. This hadith is widespread among people about Samurah. We understood thereby the fire the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant in the reports was from worldly fires not Hereafter fires, so what in these reports returning to Samurah became a merit he deserves in the Hereafter. This from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Samurah was like what he did with his wives in his saying: The quickest of you to join me is the longest-handed among you. She said: We - meaning the Prophet's wives (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - stretched our hands against the wall; when Zaynab daughter of Jahsh died, a short woman, industrious, spending what she produced in Allah's path, we knew thereby she was the longest-handed among us in good, and that became clear to them only after her death; likewise Samurah's affair became clear to people only after his death. And with Allah is success."
"6807","929 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Jibril, upon him be peace, came to me and commanded me to order my Companions to raise their voices. 5781 - Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith, from Khallad ibn al-Sa'ib, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that Jibril commanded me to order my Companions to raise their voices with ihlal."
"6808","5782 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us: that Malik narrated to him from 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, from Khallad ibn al-Sa'ib, from his father that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Jibril, upon him be peace, came to me and commanded me to order my Companions and those with me to raise their voices with talbiyah or ihlal - meaning one of them. This hadith is among what Malik and Sufyan agreed in narrating from 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr by hearing from him as we mentioned. Ibn Jurayj had reported saying: 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr wrote to me saying: 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to me that Khallad ibn al-Sa'ib ibn Khallad ibn Suwayd al-Ansari narrated to him from his father - al-Sa'ib ibn Khallad - that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Jibril, upon him be peace, came to me and said: Allah commands you to order your Companions to raise their voices with talbiyah or ihlal."
"6809","5783 - And as Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, Hamid ibn Yahya al-Balkhi narrated to us, Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr informed us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, from Khallad ibn al-Sa'ib, from his father al-Sa'ib ibn Khallad who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Jibril, upon him be peace, came to me and said to me: Order your Companions to raise their voices with ihlal - or he said: with talbiyah. Sufyan said: I came to Ibn Jurayj when Ibn Abi Bakr left and narrated to him this hadith, and he said to me: Are you not a Muslim, hearing a hadith then concealing it from me until when Ibn Abi Bakr leaves you come to me with his hadith for me to narrate from you? No, unless 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr writes it to me. So Ibn Jurayj used to narrate it: 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr wrote it to me. Musa ibn 'Uqbah differed with 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr in this hadith's chain and in its narration from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), mentioning it is Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani not al-Sa'ib ibn Khallad al-Ansari."
"6810","5784 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, 'Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, Musa ibn 'Uqbah narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn Abi Labid narrated to me, from al-Muttalib ibn 'Abdullah, from Khallad ibn al-Sa'ib, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Jibril, upon him be peace, came to me and said to me: Raise your voice with ihlal, for it is among the rites of hajj."
"6811","5785 - And as Yahya ibn 'Uthman narrated to us, Musa ibn Harun al-Burdi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Zibriqan Abu Hammam al-Ahwazi narrated to us, Musa ibn 'Uqbah narrated to us, from al-Muttalib al-Makhzumi, from Khallad ibn al-Sa'ib, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, the Companion of the Messenger of Allah, that he informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the like thereof. 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr was also differed with in its chain by Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah, who stopped it at Khallad ibn al-Sa'ib without mentioning anyone between him and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein."
"6812","5786 - As Malik ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Khallad ibn al-Sa'ib who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Among the rites of hajj is raising the voice with talbiyah."
"6813","And 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr was differed with therein by Muhammad ibn Ishaq, who returned it to al-Sa'ib without exceeding it, as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from 'Abdullah ibn Abi Labid, from al-Muttalib ibn 'Abdullah ibn Hantab, from al-Sa'ib - without mentioning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that Jibril said: O Muhammad, be loud-voiced, profusely slaughtering. An objector said: You narrated in these reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raising voices with talbiyah, while you narrated what contradicts that."
"6814","5787 - He mentioned among what Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Yunus al-Tha'labi narrated to us, Abu Mu'awiyah al-Darir narrated to us, from 'Asim al-Ahwal, from Abu 'Uthman, from Abu Musa who said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey; we descended into a depression, and people raised their voices. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O people, be gentle with yourselves; you do not call upon one deaf nor absent; you call upon One hearing, near. Then he called me - and I was near him - and said to me: O 'Abdullah ibn Qays, shall I direct you to a word from the treasure of Paradise? I said: Yes. He said: There is no might nor power except with Allah."
"6815","5788 - And among what al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from 'Ali ibn Zayd, from Sa'id al-Jurayri and Thabit al-Bunani, from Abu 'Uthman al-Nahdi, from Abu Musa al-Ash'ari who said: When we went out to Medina with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), people faced and raised their voices. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O people, you do not call upon one deaf nor absent; the One you call is between you and the necks of your shoulders. Then he said: O Abu Musa, shall I direct you to a treasure among Paradise's treasures? I said: Yes. He said: There is no might nor power except with Allah. He said: In this is the Prophet's command (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to be gentle with themselves in raising voices with takbir in what they raised them with, and informing them therewith they do not call upon one deaf nor absent. Talbiyah is likewise: thereby is meant Allah's remembrance, and He is not deaf nor absent to need raising voices therewith. These two hadiths contain contradiction to what you narrated of raising voices with talbiyah in this chapter, not hidden. Our answer was that the matter is not as he mentioned necessitating contradiction; rather the aspect is that talbiyah is among hajj rites: raising voices therewith upon what is in the reports about it."
"6816","Fahd b. Sulayman narrated to us... from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: which Hajj is best? He said: raising the voice with the talbiya (al-ajj) and slaughtering sacrificial camels (al-thajj). The author explains al-ajj here is clamouring with the talbiya and al-thajj is the sacrifice of camels."
"6817","Sulayman b. Shuayb narrated likewise from his father, from Muhammad b. al-Hasan, from Abu Yusuf, from Abu Hanifa: raising voices with the talbiya is among the rites of Hajj, and Hajj is distinguished thereby, as by other rites such as shaving heads at release from ihram and avoiding shaving hair and clipping nails therein. The raising of voices with the takbir in the hadith of Abu Musa does not carry these two aspects, so the two reports do not contradict one another."
"6818","Chapter on the problem of what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his wish that he had not entered the Kaaba after having entered it. Yahya b. Uthman... from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) grieved, and he said: I entered the Kaaba and I wish I had not entered it, fearing I have burdened my ummah. An objector said entering the Kaaba is nearness like other acts for the ummah to follow. We answered: he feared people would take it as obligatory for Hajj, so it grieved him, just as he hesitated to draw Zamzam water with Banu Abd al-Muttalib."
"6819","As al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated... from Jabir b. Abd Allah: when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) poured forth to the House in his Hajj he prayed Zuhr at Makkah, then passed Banu Abd al-Muttalib drawing Zamzam water and said: Draw, O Banu Abd al-Muttalib; were it not that people would overpower you for your watering, I would have drawn with you. They handed him a bucket and he drank from it. So he refrained fearing people would follow him, causing hardship to its keepers, like his refraining from joining the Ansar lest hijra disappear."
"6820","As al-Muzani narrated... from Abu Huraira: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were it not for the hijra I would have been a man of the Ansar, and if people took a valley or pass I would take the valley or pass of the Ansar. He refrained from becoming one of the Ansar for the reason stated: had he done so all people would have joined the Ansar in imitation of him and hijra would have vanished, though there is merit in being Ansari."
"6821","Chapter on the problem of his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): the muhrim does not marry nor give in marriage nor propose, and when he married Maymuna, in ihram or not. Yunus... from Aban b. Uthman, who said: I heard my father Uthman b. Affan say: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: the muhrim does not marry, nor give in marriage, nor propose."
"6822","Ahmad b. Dawud... from Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: the muhrim does not marry nor give in marriage."
"6823","Muhammad b. Jafar... from Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): the muhrim does not marry nor give in marriage. In this hadith the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the muhrim what is mentioned. Scholars disputed its intent: some, including Malik b. Anas and al-Shafii with many Hijazis, said such marriage is invalid for himself or others because intercourse is forbidden in ihram; Malik's reported consequence is separation, reported as divorce or as annulment without divorce."
"6824","What Yunus narrated... from Malik: they are separated, as divorce; Abd al-Rahman b. al-Qasim reported it as separation as annulment without divorce. Such a contract either confers ownership of the marital right or not; if it does, divorce the owner does not intend is meaningless, and if not, divorce or annulment has no object. Others said the prohibition is dislike of lewd talk (rafath) in ihram lest it lead to it, not invalidity, citing his marriage to Maymuna in ihram."
"6825","As Bakkar b. Qutayba... and al-Muzani... from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him): the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Maymuna while in ihram. Amr said Ibn Shihab told me from Yazid b. al-Asamm that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Maymuna, his aunt, while not in ihram. Amr said to al-Zuhri: what does Yazid b. al-Asamm, a Bedouin, know against Ibn Abbas? This is consistently reported from Ibn Abbas, and Aisha is reported to agree with him."
"6826","As Muhammad b. Khuzayma and Fahd b. Sulayman narrated... from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married some of his wives while in ihram. We know no contrary report from Aisha, and something agreeing with it is reported from Abu Huraira also."
"6827","As Sulayman b. Shuayb narrated... from Abu Huraira, who said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married while in ihram. We know no disagreement from Abu Huraira about it. An objector said Abu Rafi reported the marriage to Maymuna was while not in ihram."
"6828","What Ibrahim b. Marzuq narrated... from Abu Rafi: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Maymuna while not in ihram and consummated while not in ihram, and I was the messenger between them. The reply is that this was narrated as stated by Matar al-Warraq, though one more retentive and firm, Malik b. Anas, narrated it from Rabia differently."
"6829","As Yunus narrated... that Malik narrated from Rabia b. Abi Abd al-Rahman, from Sulayman b. Yasar: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent his freedman Abu Rafi and an Ansari man, and they married him to Maymuna bint al-Harith while he was at Madina before departing. So this report stops at Sulayman b. Yasar without reaching Abu Rafi, and ceases to be proof. The objector said Matar reported from Maymuna herself that it was while not in ihram."
"6830","What Yunus narrated... from Yazid b. al-Asamm, who said: Maymuna informed me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married her while not in ihram."
"6831","What the two Rabis and Muhammad b. Khuzayma narrated... from Maymuna bint al-Harith, who said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married me when we were both not in ihram after returning from Makkah. But Ibn Abbas reported he married her earlier while in ihram, and it is reported he sought to consummate at Makkah but her family refused."
"6832","As al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated... from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him): the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Maymuna bint al-Harith while in ihram, stayed three days at Makkah, then Khuwaylid b. Abd al-Uzza came with Quraysh on the third day saying your term has ended so leave us; he said: what harm if you left me to consummate among you and I made food for you? They said: we need no food, leave us. So he left and consummated with Maymuna at Sarif. This shows a different time than Matar's Madina report. If asked how Maymuna could not know, we say: he proposed and delegated to al-Abbas, who contracted it; she missed the contract time and knew only the consummation, while Ibn Abbas witnessed his father's contracting. As for Uthman's prohibition report, he may have heard it before or after, intending others, since the Prophet was protected and master of his desire unlike others."
"6833","As Muhammad b. Khuzayma narrated... from Ibrahim: Ibn Masud saw no harm in the muhrim marrying. If called disconnected, it is said Ibrahim's unattributed reports from Ibn Masud are from a group from him."
"6834","As Ibrahim b. Marzuq narrated... from al-Amash, who said: I said to Ibrahim: when you narrate, give the chain. He said: when I say Abdullah said, I say it only after more than one narrated it to me from Abdullah; when I say so-and-so narrated to me from Abdullah, that is the one who told me."
"6835","As Muhammad b. Khuzayma narrated... from Ata: Ibn Abbas saw no harm in two muhrims marrying."
"6836","As Rawh b. al-Faraj narrated... Abdullah b. Muhammad b. Abi Bakr said: I asked Anas b. Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) about a muhrim marrying, and he said: no harm; is it but like a sale. Rawh named him Abdullah b. Muhammad b. Abi Bakr, though some say between Abdullah and Anas is Muhammad b. Abi Bakr, the Thaqafi, from whom Malik and others reported."
"6837","Chapter on the cause of revelation of: When those who disbelieved plotted against you to restrain or kill or expel you. Yahya b. Uthman... from Ibn Abbas on His saying about plotting to bind: Quraysh conferred at night at Makkah: in the morning bind him with bonds, meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); some said kill him, some expel him. Allah informed His Prophet, so Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) slept that night on the Prophet's bed until he reached the cave, while the polytheists guarded Ali thinking him the Prophet. In the morning they rushed, saw Ali, and Allah turned back their plot. They asked where his companion was; he said I do not know. They tracked him to the mountain, reached the cave, saw spider web on its mouth and said had he entered there would be no web; he stayed three nights. This shows the revelation cause, supported by the earlier Abu Balj report of Ali sleeping in the Prophet's cloak."
"6838","Chapter on disagreement about a sale at prices without mutual wronging, whether concluded. Yunus... from Umar b. al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I gave a horse in Allah's path, its keeper neglected it, I wanted to buy it cheap. I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: do not buy it even if he gives it for one dirham, for one who takes back his charity is like a dog returning to its vomit."
"6839","Al-Muzani... from Malik... from Umar, then he mentioned the like."
"6840","Yazid b. Sinan... from Umar b. al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I gave a horse in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like. Abu Jafar said: this shows the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade Umar buying that horse even for a dirham because he had given it; had he not given it, buying for a dirham would be lawful. This is the view of jurists of Hijaz, Iraq and others; only some later ones said such a sale is no sale, though one who can transfer a thing can transfer it for little compensation."
"6841","Chapter on his supplication for the Ansar, whether their sons are included. Muhammad b. Ali... from Anas b. Malik, who said: I grieved for my people struck on the day of Harra, so Zayd b. Arqam wrote to me that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: O Allah, forgive the Ansar and the sons of the Ansar; Ibn al-Fadl doubted: and the sons of the sons of the Ansar."
"6842","Ibrahim b. Abi Dawud... from Zayd b. Arqam, who said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, forgive the Ansar and the sons of the Ansar."
"6843","Al-Qasim b. Jafar... from Anas b. Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like."
"6844","Ali b. Shayba... from Abu Bakr b. Anas, who said: Zayd b. Arqam wrote to Anas b. Malik informing him of those struck among his children and people on the day of Harra, and giving glad tidings: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: O Allah, forgive the Ansar, the sons of the Ansar, the sons of the sons of the Ansar, the women of the Ansar, the women of the sons of the Ansar, and the women of the sons of the sons of the Ansar."
"6845","Ibrahim b. Abi Dawud... from Anas, who said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, forgive the Ansar, the sons of the Ansar, and the sons of the sons of the Ansar."
"6846","As Muhammad b. Humayd narrated... from Anas: Umm Sulaym bore Abdullah b. Abi Talha at night and disliked tahnik until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did it; I came with Ajwa dates while he was branding camels; he chewed dates, mixed with saliva, fed him, the boy smacked his lips, and he said: the beloved of the Ansar is dates. He said: name him; he said: he is Abdullah. So he counted Abdullah b. Abi Talha among the Ansar though of their sons, proving sons enter the supplication. As for Muhajirin, the name came by actual hijra from their land, while the Ansar pledged at Makkah to defend him as themselves and their sons, binding their households and offspring like treaty peoples."
"6847","Continuation: the pledge entered their sons by their entering it themselves and others of their land, as sons of hostile peoples enter what the Muslim imam settles with them for future affairs."
"6848","Chapter on his saying on livestock charity: do not separate what is joined nor join what is apart fearing charity, and two partners settle equally. Ibrahim b. Marzuq... from Anas: when Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) became caliph he sent Anas b. Malik to Bahrayn... and in his letter: whoever is asked for it duly let him give, and if asked above it let him not give; and in that letter: let there be no joining of apart nor separating of joined fearing charity, and two partners settle between them equally."
"6849","Bakkar b. Qutayba and al-Rabi al-Muradi... Hammad b. Salama said: Thabit al-Bunani sent me to Thumama b. Abdullah b. Anas to send Abu Bakr's letter to Anas on charity, and he sent it with the seal of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... containing what is in Ibrahim b. Marzuq's hadith."
"6850","Yazid b. Sinan... from Asim b. Damra and al-Harith al-Awar, from Ali, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - Zuhayr said: I think it is from the Prophet, and it is from the Prophet - peace be upon him - but I deem I think dearer to me - and among it: let there be no separating of joined nor joining of apart fearing charity."
"6851","Yunus... Ibn Shihab said: this is the copy of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) letter on charity, with Al Umar; Salim b. Abdullah b. Umar read it to me and I memorized it, and Umar b. Abd al-Aziz copied it from Salim and Abdullah sons of Abdullah b. Umar when he passed Madina and ordered his agents by it. In it was like what we mentioned. Abu Jafar discusses the intent: scholars differed greatly; the best is what al-Muzani quoted from al-Shafii: undivided partners are partners, and partners may mix livestock though each knows his own; they are not partners until they rest, pasture, milk and water together with mixed stallions; then after a year from mixing, being Muslims, they pay as one man; if separated in resting, pasture, water, or a year passes for one before the other, they pay as two. Not separating joined and not joining apart means: three partners with 120 sheep owe one; if split they owe three; and a man with 101 and another with 100 left apart owe two, joined owe three; fear is the collector's lest charity lessen and the owner's lest it increase."
"6852","As Sulayman b. Shuayb narrated... from Abu Yusuf, who said: I said to Abu Hanifa: what is His saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): do not separate joined? He said: a man with 120 sheep owes one; if the collector splits into forties there are three. I said: what is not joining apart? He said: two men with forty between them; if joined there is one, if split twenty-twenty there is none. I said: if they are close partners are their flocks not joined? He said yes, they are not joined. And Sufyan al-Thawri held likewise."
"6853","As Abu Ghassan narrated... from Sufyan: not joining apart nor separating joined fearing charity; separating joined is a man with a hundred sheep here and there, not taken from these and those together; joining apart is a man with forty and another with fifty mixed together so no sheep is taken, or one man with forty counted like Abu Hanifa and al-Thawri held. This shows they regarded ownership not mixing. It rebuts al-Shafii's claim of no dissent that three partners with 120 pay one as one man. Allah mentioned zakat like prayer, fasting, Hajj: establish prayer, give zakat; whoever witnesses the month let him fast; pilgrimage upon men. Each person's obligation is on himself, showing no ruling for mixing but for ownerships. He said: take from their wealth charity purifying them. It is intelligible none is purified from another's wealth but his own. As for two partners settling equally: two men with 120, one owning two-thirds and one a third; the collector demands without waiting their division."
"6854","What Yunus narrated... Malik said: Umar's saying not separating joined means two partners each with a hundred sheep; when the collector seeks them they split so each owes one, and this is forbidden. That is when herdsman, stallion, pasture, resting place and bucket are one; they are partners. No charity is due on a partner until each has the minimum: if one has forty and the other less than forty, none on the lesser and charity on the forty-holder; if each has a thousand or less yet liable, or one forty or more, they are partners settling excess equally by shares of what would be due on one owner. This is clear: either mixing is meaningless and each is as before, as Abu Hanifa said, then he returned to what al-Shafii said that knowing one's property with one stallion, pasture and water makes them partners, which is unintelligible: how are they partners with distinct properties? If by mixing stallions, pasture and such, zakat is not on those but on the animals themselves, in which they are not mixed. Scholars before you opposed you."
"6855","As Ibrahim b. Marzuq narrated... from Tawus, who said: if two partners know their properties they are not joined in charity; I told Ata and he said: I see it but true. So Tawus and Ata did not regard stallion, milking, watering, resting, bucket or such as you did. If you say their words imply that unknowing partners are joined, we say: it may mean joined in collection to be taken at once, then settling between them as your opponent says."
"6856","Chapter on what was reported from Ali b. Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) on raising hands in the opening takbir and otherwise. Al-Rabi al-Muradi... from Ubayd Allah b. Abi Rafi, from Ali b. Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): when he stood for the prescribed prayer he said Allahu Akbar and raised his hands to his shoulders, did the like when he finished recitation and wished to bow, did it when finished and raised from bowing, did not raise his hands in anything while sitting, and when he rose from the two prostrations he raised his hands likewise and said takbir."
"6857","Abu Ayyub... from Abd al-Rahman b. Abi al-Zinad with the like. In this hadith is raising at the prescribed takbir, at rising from bowing, and at rising from prostration. We know none who mentioned this raising except Abd al-Rahman b. Abi al-Zinad; others did not, among them Abd al-Aziz al-Majishun, who narrated from Abdullah b. al-Fadl and his uncle al-Majishun without mentioning it."
"6858","As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated... Abd al-Aziz al-Majishun narrated from al-Majishun and Abdullah b. al-Fadl, from al-Araj, from Ubayd Allah b. Abi Rafi, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him): the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he opened said takbir then said: I have turned my face to Him Who originated the heavens and earth, upright, Muslim, and I am not of the polytheists."
"6859","Likewise Yazid b. Sinan... from Abd al-Aziz al-Majishun from his uncle, without Abdullah b. al-Fadl, the like, without mentioning raising hands anywhere. This hadith is of two possibilities: Ibn Abi al-Zinad added this by mistake, or truly. If mistaken, no proof in a mistake; if true, we found from Ali after the Prophet what he practised: Ali would raise his hands at the first takbir then not raise after."
"6860","As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated... from Asim b. Kulayb from his father, among Ali's companions, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), the like. In this is proof that Ibn Abi al-Zinad's addition, if sound, is the stronger proof for leaving raising after the opening takbir; for Ali would not after the Prophet do contrary to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except upon proof of abrogation."
"6861","Chapter on what was reported from Abdullah b. Masud from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on this. Muhammad b. al-Numan... from Alqama, from Abdullah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): he would raise his hands at the first takbir then not repeat. There is no disagreement from Ibn Masud on it. This hadith from Abdullah b. Masud is agreed with."
"6862","What Bakkar b. Qutayba narrated... from al-Mughira, who said: I said to Ibrahim: the hadith of Wail that he saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raising his hands when opening prayer, when bowing and when raising his head from bowing. He said: if Wail saw him once, Abdullah saw him fifty times not doing that. We accepted this from Ibrahim though he did not name between him and Abdullah, for his saying to al-Amash: when I say Abdullah said, a group narrated it to me from him; when I say so-and-so narrated to me from Abdullah, that is who told me."
"6863","Chapter on what was reported from Abdullah b. Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) on this. Yunus... from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he opened prayer raised his hands till his shoulders, when he wished to bow, and after he raised; and he did not raise between the prostrations."
"6864","Ibrahim b. Marzuq... from Malik, from al-Zuhri, the like. What is in this hadith, and he would not do that between the prostrations: it is unknown from whose قول it is, whether Ibn Umar's or below him. In it is raising at opening, at bowing, at rising from bowing; to this al-Shafii and many who hold raising besides the opening takbir went."
"6865","Ishaq b. Ibrahim... and Ahmad b. Shuayb... al-Mutamir b. Sulayman: I heard Ubayd Allah b. Umar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): he would raise his hands when he entered prayer, when he wished to bow, when he raised his head from bowing, and when he rose from the two rak'as he raised his hands, all to the shoulders. In this is like the first hadith plus raising upon standing from sitting after two rak'as. We know thereby hands are raised only at the first takbir; if one argues by Malik's and Sufyan's two hadiths from al-Zuhri he is refuted by Ubayd Allah's hadith with rising from sitting; what excuses one in differing with Ubaydullah excuses the other in leaving Malik's and Sufyan's, for Ubayd Allah is not below Malik or Sufyan in this hadith. We also found Nafi's narration agreeing with Ubayd Allah and adding more raisings."
"6866","As Ishaq b. Ibrahim narrated... from Ubayd Allah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: he would raise his hands at every lowering and raising, bowing, prostration, standing, sitting and between the prostrations, claiming the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did that. This Nafi narration is anomalous against what Ubayd Allah narrated. This hadith is reported from Nafi contrary to what Ubayd Allah reported from him."
"6867","What Yahya b. Uthman narrated... from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he entered prayer raised his hands to his shoulders, and when he wished to bow did the like. This agreed with Malik and Sufyan from al-Zuhri and opposed what Ubayd Allah reported from him. Our answer: Ayyub reported nothing from Nafi that others did not report contrary to Ubayd Allah; and what confirms Ubayd Allah from him are the acts he practised in his prayer."
"6868","As Ahmad b. Dawud narrated... from Ayyub, who said: I saw Tawus and Nafi raising their hands between the prostrations; Hammad said: I saw Tawus and Ayyub doing it. Nafi's act proves what others reported from him, and Ayyub's holding to it proves the matter was so with him, either by omission in Nafi's hadith or taken from Ubayd Allah from Nafi and acted upon. Hammad b. Zayd also held this."
"6869","Ibn Abi Dawud... Wahb b. Jarir said: Hammad b. Zayd would raise his hands between the prostrations. In this is confirmation for accepting this addition over Malik's and Sufyan's from al-Zuhri; else one must oppose what Nafi reported from Ibn Umar and Ubayd Allah from al-Zuhri from Salim from his father from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); for nothing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did should be left except upon proof requiring leaving. Whoever raises only at the opening takbir is excused, since it is reported what Ibn Umar practised after the Prophet contrary to it; Ibn Umar would not leave what the Prophet did except for abrogation or such. An objector said Tawus reported, so Tawus and Ibn Umar were upon what was reported from the Messenger, then proof of abrogation arose and they left it, as is first in carrying reports, especially with what we reported from Umar agreeing."
"6870","As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated... from al-Aswad, who said: I saw Umar b. al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) raising his hands at the first takbir then not repeating; he said: and I saw Ibrahim and al-Shabi doing that."
"6871","Ibn Abi Dawud... Abu Bakr b. Ayyash said: I never saw a jurist doing it, raising his hands in other than the first takbir. Since Umar, Ali, Abdullah b. Masud - with their standing in prayer with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as Muhajirin and Ansar - then Ibn Umar after them were upon the like, nothing reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is more worthy of acceptance than what they reported."
"6872","As Abu Bakra and Ibn Marzuq narrated... from Qays b. Ubad, who said: Ubayy b. Kab said to me: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us: be in the row next to me."
"6873","As Ibrahim b. Marzuq narrated... from al-Amash, from Umara b. Umayr, from Mamar, from Abu Masud al-Ansari, who said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would say: let those of understanding and sense be next to me, then those next to them, then those next to them."
"6874","As Bakkar b. Qutayba and Ali b. Mabad narrated... from Anas, who said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) liked the Muhajirin and Ansar to be next to him to preserve from him. In what they reported in this chapter is sufficiency from else adduced therein."
"6875","Chapter on what Abu Huraira reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on this. Ubayd b. Rijal... from Abu Bakr b. Abd al-Rahman b. al-Harith b. Hisham, from Abu Huraira: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he said takbir for prayer put his hands to his shoulders, when he bowed did the like, when he rose for prostration did the like, and when he rose from the two rak'as did the like. In this is strengthening of what Ubayd Allah reported from al-Zuhri."
"6876","Chapter on what was reported from Malik b. al-Huwayrith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on this. Ahmad b. Shuayb... from Qatada, from Nasr b. Asim, from Malik b. al-Huwayrith: he saw the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raise his hands in his prayer, when he bowed, when he raised his head from bowing, when he prostrated, and when he raised his head from prostration, until level with the tops of his ears."
"6877","Ahmad... from Abd al-Ala, from Said with the like."
"6878","Ahmad... from Muadh b. Hisham, from his father, from Qatada, from Nasr b. Asim, from Malik b. al-Huwayrith: the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he entered prayer, then the like, adding: when he bowed did the like, and when he raised his head from prostration did the like. In this is proof of what Ubayd Allah reported from Nafi from Ibn Umar on this; for what is needed in this discussion has entered what came in these chapters."
"6879","Chapter on his answer to one who said at his words: none of you will be saved by his deeds; they said: not even you, O Messenger of Allah? Yunus... from Busr b. Said, from Abu Huraira, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: none of you will be saved by his deeds; a man said: not even you, O Messenger of Allah? He said: not even I, unless Allah covers me with mercy from Him and bounty; but be upright."
"6880","Al-Rabi al-Muradi... from Bukayr with the like. This with us, and Allah knows, was before Allah sent down at Hudaybiyya: Indeed We have given you a clear victory, that Allah may forgive you your former and latter sins, complete His favour upon you, guide you a straight path, and help you mightily; as discussed earlier in this book. Then Allah sent down in the sura He sent, informing him of his state not before informed, and sent down about his companions (may Allah be pleased with them): that He may admit believing men and women into gardens beneath which rivers flow. As they said after what was sent about himself: Allah has clarified for you about yourself, what for us? So Allah sent: that He may admit believing men and women into gardens. He mentioned good for them and did not mention it in what He sent about himself; that, and Allah knows, addresses Arabs in their tongue: when the addressed know what he intends, that spares addressing the rest; his companions deserved it by companionship, support and his calling them and increase upon it; if by falling short of him they deserve Paradise, he by surpassing them is worthier of entering it."
"6881","Chapter on his leaving the owner of the camel that complained of hunger and overwork without seizing it for feeding. Al-Rabi al-Muradi... from Abdullah b. Jafar: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) put me behind him one day, told me a secret I tell no one, screened himself for need by a target or dense palms, entered an Ansari's garden where a camel moaned and wept on seeing the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he wiped the top of its head and temples till it calmed, then said: who owns this camel? An Ansari youth said: mine, O Messenger of Allah. He said: do you not fear Allah about the beast Allah has owned you; it complained you starve it and overwork it. Dhifra is behind its ears, sarw its highest part; he wiped to calm it. Then a fiqh question: some, like Abu Hanifa and his companions, say the owner of a starved beast is ordered but not compelled to feed, only to fear Allah; others say he is compelled, seized and detained as with owned humans in need."
"6882","Chapter on his leaving killing Musaylima the liar when he came to Madina refusing belief unless given rule after him. Ibn Abi Dawud... from Ibn Abbas: Musaylima came in the Prophet's time to Madina saying: if Muhammad gives me rule after him I follow him, with many of his people; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came with Thabit b. Qays b. Shammas, a palm-stick piece in hand, stood over Musaylima among his companions and said: if you asked me for this piece I would not give it; you will not exceed Allah's decree about you; if you turn away Allah will cut you off; I see you but as I saw about you; and this Thabit answers you; then he left. Ibn Abbas asked about his words; Abu Huraira said: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: while asleep I saw two gold bracelets on my hands, it concerned me, it was revealed blow them, I blew and they flew; I interpreted them as two liars after me: al-Ansi of Sana and Musaylima of Yamama. Why did he not kill Musaylima for refusing Islam? Answer: he likely came under safe-conduct with his people to address and be addressed, so he did not kill him, following: if one of the polytheists seeks your protection, grant it till he hears Allah's words, then convey him to safety."
"6883","Chapter on those Allah shades in His shade when no shade but His. Yunus... from Khubayb b. Abd al-Rahman, from Hafs b. Asim, from Abu Said al-Khudri or Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with them): the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: seven Allah shades in His shade when no shade but His: a just imam, a youth grown in Allah's worship, a man whose heart hangs to the mosque till he returns, two men loving for Allah meeting and parting on it, a man remembering Allah alone whose eyes overflow, a man called by a woman of rank and beauty saying I fear Allah, and a man hiding charity so his left knows not what his right spends. Malik narrated it doubtfully between Abu Said and Abu Huraira; we sought the truth in others' hadith."
"6884","Fahd b. Sulayman... al-Layth: Ubayd Allah b. Umar narrated from his grandfather's father, from Abu Huraira, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): six Allah shades in His throne's shade when no shade but His: a youth grown in Allah's worship, a just imam, a man called by a beautiful ranked woman to herself saying I fear Allah Lord of worlds, a man hiding his right charity from his left, a man whose heart hangs in Allah's mosques, and two brothers in Allah parting on it. By Ubayd Allah's narration the reporter is Abu Huraira not Abu Said. Then we sought whether Ubayd Allah heard it from his grandfather by audition or otherwise."
"6885","Muhammad b. Ibrahim al-Razi... and Ibn Abi Dawud... Yahya b. Said al-Qattan, from Ubayd Allah b. Umar, from Khubayb b. Abd al-Rahman, from Hafs b. Asim, from Abu Huraira: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: seven Allah shades under His throne when no shade but His: the just imam, a youth grown in Allah's worship, two men loving in Allah meeting and parting on it, a man sought by rank and beauty saying I fear Allah Lord of worlds, a man remembering Allah alone whose eyes overflow from fear of Allah, a man whose heart hangs to mosques, and a man hiding charity so his left knows not what his right spent. So Ubayd Allah did not take it by hearing from his grandfather Hafs but from Khubayb from Hafs. The intended shade is the shade of Allah's throne. Likewise His saying: and extended shade."
"6886","What Ibrahim b. Marzuq narrated... from Abu Salama, from Abu Huraira, raised: in Paradise is a tree under whose shade a rider travels a hundred years; recite if you will: and extended shade. This shade differs from the first hadith's shade. Then we examined the shade itself what it is."
"6887","Wallad the grammarian... from Abu Ubayda on: and extended shade: the sun does not efface it, lasting; time is called extended, and lasting life extended; Labid said. Al-Farra said in Meanings of the Quran on extended shade: no sun in it, like between dawn and sunrise."
"6888","Chapter on what was from him about one whose leg was pierced by a horn, who asked retaliation and was retaliated, then the retaliator's leg became paralyzed and the retaliated healed. Yunus... from Amr b. Dinar, from Muhammad b. Talha b. Yazid b. Rukana: a man pierced another with a horn in a crowd, came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: retaliate for me. He said: wait. He came a second time or as Allah willed saying: retaliate for me, so he retaliated him; the other healed and the first's leg became paralyzed. He came saying: retaliate again. He said: you have nothing; I told you wait but you refused. Yunus dictated it in year 55; al-Muzani had narrated it earlier in 53 from al-Shafii from Sufyan without doubt, mentioning Wait three times and retaliation at the fourth. We included it despite break because Uthman b. Abi Shayba narrated it from Ibn Ulaya from Ayyub from Amr from Jabir b. Abd Allah. Ibn Jurayj narrated it from Amr as Ibn Uyayna, mentioning waiting till healing from the wound."
"6889","As Yunus narrated... Ibn Wahb: I heard Ibn Jurayj narrate from Amr b. Dinar, from Muhammad b. Talha b. Yazid b. Rukana: a man pierced a man with a horn, came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: retaliate. He said: till you heal, three times, then he retaliated him; the requiter limped, came saying: my right. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah keep that far, you have nothing. We reasoned his first refusal was not after retaliation became due, else he would have granted the due; and his later granting shows it had become due, else he would not take the undue. The upshot: scholars differ whether retaliation for a wound is due at occurrence or only after seeing its outcome in death, safety, loss of limbs, or healing. Some, like Abu Hanifa and his companions: not due till the outcome, treated as what it ends to, with retaliation and else as if intended. Others: due at once by the like, then see outcome of parity or excess, doing two acts for one; among them al-Shafii. Since the Messenger refused at first when the wound existed..."
"6890","Chapter on Abu Bakr al-Siddiq's (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: by Allah if they withheld a young goat or a rope, on what was rendered to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I would fight them. Ibrahim b. Marzuq... from Ubayd Allah b. Abd Allah, from Abu Huraira: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I am commanded to fight people till they say there is no god but Allah; when they say it their blood and wealth are safe from me except by its right, and their reckoning is upon Allah. When the ridda came I narrated this to Abu Bakr, and he said: if they withheld a rope I would fight them for it. In this: if they withheld a rope rendered to the Messenger."
"6891","Yazid b. Sinan... from Ubayd Allah b. Abd Allah b. Utba, from Abu Huraira: when Allah took His Prophet, Abu Bakr was made caliph and those Arabs apostatized; Abu Bakr sent to fight them; Umar said: did you not hear the Messenger say: I am commanded to fight till they say there is no god but Allah. He said: shall I not fight peoples over prayer and zakat obligations? By Allah if they withheld a young she-goat rendered to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) I would fight them. When I saw Allah expand Abu Bakr's breast for fighting I knew it the truth. Abu Jafar: in this from Sulayman is young goat, in the first rope; so the difference in the two words is from those who reported from Sulayman b. Kathir, Abu al-Walid and Muhammad b. Kathir, Allah knows the truth with him."
"6892","Ahmad b. Shuayb... from al-Zubaydi, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah b. Abd Allah, from Abu Huraira, then he mentioned this hadith save he said: if they withheld a young she-goat rendered to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); we find no disagreement from al-Zubaydi therein."
"6893","Al-Layth b. Abda... from Shuayb b. Abi Hamza, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah b. Abd Allah that Abu Huraira said, then he mentioned it saying: if they withheld a young she-goat; we know no disagreement from Shuayb from al-Zuhri therein."
"6894","As Ahmad b. Shuayb narrated... al-Layth b. Sad from Uqayl from al-Zuhri from Ubayd Allah from Abu Huraira, then he mentioned it saying: if they withheld a rope."
"6895","We narrated it... from Abdullah b. Salih from al-Layth from Uqayl from al-Zuhri from Ubayd Allah from Abu Huraira, and he said: if they withheld a young she-goat. So Abdullah b. Salih and Qutayba differed over Uqayl in what he reported from al-Layth, each saying what we mentioned, Allah knows the truth with him."
"6896","Ubayd b. Muhammad b. Rijal... from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah b. Abd Allah, then he mentioned this hadith without mentioning Abu Huraira therein. It was said to Abd al-Razzaq: from Abu Huraira? He said: no. No disagreement from Mamar with us therein."
"6897","Harun b. Kamil... from Abd al-Rahman b. Khalid b. Musafir, from Ibn Shihab, from Ubayd Allah b. Abd Allah that Abu Huraira said, then he mentioned it saying: if they withheld a rope. Abu Jafar: we know no disagreement from Abd al-Rahman b. Khalid therein."
"6898","Ubayd... Anbasa b. Khalid, from Yunus b. Yazid, from Ibn Shihab. And Yahya b. Uthman... Ibn al-Mubarak, from Muhammad b. Abi Hafsa, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah b. Abd Allah, from Abu Huraira, then he mentioned it saying: by Allah if they withheld a young she-goat; we know no disagreement from Muhammad b. Abi Hafsa from al-Zuhri therein."
"6899","Ali b. Shayba... Salih b. Abi al-Akhdar, from al-Zuhri, from Said b. al-Musayyab and Abu Salama, from Abu Huraira, then he mentioned it saying: if they withheld a young she-goat. Abu Jafar: we know no disagreement from Salih from al-Zuhri therein. So the difference in the two words is from the narrators not from Abu Bakr's (may Allah be pleased with him) speech, though most narrated: if they withheld a young she-goat. As for rope, some said the intended rope is the cord tying the charity obligation, as Ali b. Abd al-Aziz quoted from Abu Ubayd from al-Waqidi; this is Malik's and Ibn Abi Dhib's view, though unknown from Malik and corrupt by analogy: if the payer of livestock must give a rope, the payer of dirhams and dinars must give a purse, and of palms baskets, which none says, proving corruption."
"6900","Some said rope means a year's charity, citing what Ali quoted from Abu Ubayd: Ibn al-Kalbi told me Muawiya employed his nephew Amr b. Utba over Kalb charities, he wronged them, so Amr b. al-Ada al-Kalbi said about it: a rope, two ropes. This interpretation with us is also corrupt; for Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) meant only if they withheld little of what they rendered to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he would fight as for all charity. We found no plausible interpretation of rope except what is reported from Ibn al-Arabi: when the collector takes from charity other than what is in it it is said he took a rope, and when he takes price it is said he took cash. The worthier is young goat not rope. Here is a fiqh chapter: scholars differ over sheep all prime with no aged: some say nothing therein, some one thereof; all reported from Abu Hanifa. Ahmad b. Abi Imran... from Abu Yusuf of his shifting among them: his first that therein is an aged; Zufar held this; Abu Yusuf held therein one thereof."
"6901","Chapter on his commanding the Jews when they brought the man and woman of theirs who fornicated, judging him in them, to bring the Torah about stoning, and stoning them after. Yunus... Malik from Nafi. And al-Muzani... al-Shafii from Malik from Nafi, together from Abdullah b. Umar: the Jews came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioning a man and woman of theirs fornicated; he said: what do you find in the Torah about stoning? They said: we disgrace them and they are flogged. Abdullah b. Salam said: you lie, in it is stoning. They brought the Torah, spread it, one put his hand over the stoning verse reading before and after; Abdullah b. Salam said: lift your hand; he lifted, and there was the stoning verse. They said: you told truth, O Muhammad, in it is the stoning verse. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered them and they were stoned. Abdullah b. Umar said: I saw the man bending over the woman shielding her from stones."
"6902","Yunus... from Ubayd Allah b. Umar, from Abd al-Karim b. Malik, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned a Jew and Jewess when they sought his judgment. An objector: how do you accept his return to the Torah when Allah told him its people altered it, writing what is not of it, as in: woe to those who write the book with their hands then say this is from Allah to buy a small price. Our answer: he did it because Allah informed him stoning is in the Torah among what Jews hid not revealed; he ordered bringing it to establish proof and bind them by the Torah's duty upon the two, since of them was like those who sought him."
"6903","As Ahmad b. Shuayb and Ishaq b. Ibrahim narrated... Ali b. al-Husayn from his father, from Yazid al-Nahwi, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas: whoever disbelieves in stoning disbelieves in the Quran unawares; that is Allah's saying: O People of the Book, Our Messenger has come clarifying much of what you hid of the Book. Among what they hid was stoning. The wording is Ahmad b. Shuayb's. So the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned to the Torah only finding stoning therein as Allah sent down unaltered; clear is why he ordered the Jews to bring it, contrary to the objector."
"6904","Chapter on what was reported from Abdullah b. Masud from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the prayer for missing which the Messenger threatened to burn houses, which prayer is it? Ali b. Shayba... Israil b. Yunus, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have intended to order a man to lead people, then order men not witnessing prayer to kindle their houses with fire upon them."
"6905","Al-Husayn b. Nasr... al-Firyabi... Israil... the like from Abdullah from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6906","Al-Rabi b. Sulayman al-Muradi... Asad b. Musa... Israil... the like. Abu Jafar: we examined this hadith and what is in it of his intending to burn those missing prayer, and began seeking which prayer it is."
"6907","Yazid b. Sinan... Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi... Zuhayr b. Muawiya, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I have intended to order a man to lead people, then burn upon men houses missing Friday."
"6908","Fahd b. Sulayman... Abu Ghassan... Zuhayr b. Muawiya... Abu Ishaq heard Abu al-Ahwas mention from Abdullah b. Masud: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have intended to order a man to lead people, then burn upon men missing Friday their houses."
"6909","Ibrahim b. Abi Dawud... Ahmad b. Abd Allah b. Yunus... Zuhayr... the like. Abu Jafar: by Zuhayr's hadith we know the prayer threatened in the first hadith is Friday. That with us, Allah knows, because the duty of coming to Friday is what Allah clarified: O you who believe, when called for prayer on Friday hasten to Allah's remembrance and leave trade; then stressed it is prayer by: when prayer is concluded disperse and seek Allah's bounty. He permitted after prayer what He barred before. Since this duty binds each and is not discharged for the common by the elite like washing the dead, praying over them and burying them, but hastening to Friday till concluded binds all, the threat was in this sense. An objector: why burning houses as penalty? Answer: it may be to make an example, and may be among rulings then abrogated, like his saying about zakat withholders: we take it and half their wealth as a resolve of our Lord's, and about theft of mountain herds: therein double fine and exemplary lashes. Scholars agree that is abrogated, and penalties for bodily omissions returned..."
"6910","Chapter on what was reported from Abu Huraira from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about which prayer the threat for missing in the first chapter's hadith is. Yunus... Malik from Abu al-Zinad from al-Araj from Abu Huraira: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: by Him in Whose hand is my soul, I have intended to order firewood gathered, then order prayer and call made, then order a man to lead people, then go to men and burn their houses upon them. By Him in Whose hand is my soul, if one knew he would find a fat bone or two good trotters he would witness Isha."
"6911","Al-Rabi al-Muradi... Ibn Wahb, from Abd al-Rahman b. Abi al-Zinad and Malik, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Huraira, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like."
"6912","Fahd b. Sulayman... Umar b. Hafs b. Ghiyath... from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Huraira, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): no prayer is heavier on hypocrites than Fajr and Isha; if they knew what is in them they would come though crawling. I have intended to order the caller to establish, then order a man to lead, then take firebrands and burn upon whoever does not come out to prayer his house."
"6913","Ibrahim b. Marzuq... Affan... Hammad b. Salama, from Asim b. Bahdala, from Abu Salih, from Abu Huraira, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): he delayed the last Isha to a third of the night, then came while people were few and scattered; he angered greatly then said: if a man called people to a shank or two trotters they would answer, yet they miss this prayer. I have intended to order a man to lead, then stay behind to these houses missing this prayer and kindle fires upon them."
"6914","Fahd... Abu Ghassan... Abu Bakr b. Ayyash from Asim with the like. Abu Jafar: in Abu Salih's hadith is clarification of the prayer left unnamed in al-Araj's hadith, both to Abu Huraira: it is the last Isha. An objector: this prayer with the other five requires gathering and not missing, yet it is a collective duty discharged for the common by some elite, so how accept this threat? Answer: the five prayers require attendance and congregational establishment, and though discharged for the rest by some, all are commanded and held till prayer is established as Allah ordered till the duty falls as it falls. What confirms it is his answer to Ibn Umm Maktum asking leave from the mosque."
"6915","As Abu Umayya narrated... from Abu Razin, from Abu Huraira: Ibn Umm Maktum came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: I am blind, far house, no constant guide; have I leave not to come? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: no. Abu Jafar notes Abu Sinan is Said b. Sinan, some ascribe to Qazwin for residence, a Kufan acceptable in narration. Shuba opposed him in chain from Amr b. Murra."
"6916","As Bakkar b. Qutayba narrated... Shuba, from Amr b. Murra: I heard Ibn Abi Layla say: among us was a blind man who said: O Messenger of Allah, between me and the mosque are palms. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: do you hear the call? He said: yes. He said: when you hear the call answer it. We examined: Ibn Abi Layla says among us a blind man, and Ibn Abi Layla is Ansari while Ibn Umm Maktum is Qurashi, so it may be an Ansari man, each of the two men other than the other."
"6917","As Ibrahim b. Abi Dawud narrated... from Husayn b. Abd al-Rahman, from Abdullah b. Shaddad, from Abdullah b. Umm Maktum: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out of the mosque seeing fewness among people and said: I intend to appoint an imam, then go out and burn upon any man missing prayer his house. I said: O Messenger of Allah, between me and the mosque are palms and trees, not always a guide; may I pray at home? He said: do you hear the iqama? I said: yes. He said: come to it. Shuba narrated this from Husayn stopping at Abdullah b. Shaddad."
"6918","As Abd al-Aziz b. Abi Aqil narrated... Shuba, from Husayn b. Abd al-Rahman, from Abdullah b. Shaddad b. al-Had: Ibn Umm Maktum said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): between me and the mosque are things, sometimes I find a guide sometimes not. He said: do you not hear the call? I said: yes. He said: when you hear the call walk to it. Then another man asked the like? He said: when you hear the call announce; and he did not license him. Then he said: I have intended to order a man to lead, then come to peoples not witnessing prayer and burn upon them. In his answer to the disabled, with his disability, that the duty of congregation does not fall, as one without disability, we understood attendance is obligatory on the able, addressed to all before the duty falls by others' performance. In Abu Huraira's hadith he said it seeing fewness: the attending group was not the required congregation, hence the threat."
"6919","Chapter on what was reported from Jabir b. Abd Allah from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said it because of something from a man. Al-Rabi al-Muradi... Abdullah b. Lahia, from Abu al-Zubayr: I heard Jabir say: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: were it not for something I would order a man to lead, then burn houses upon their contents. Jabir said: he said it because of a man about whom something reached him, saying: if he does not desist I shall burn his house upon its contents. An objector: here the threat's cause was one man, so how accept he addressed others besides him? Answer: it was of his beautiful character and highest manners: when something reached him about one he would not confront him but say a general word including that man as one hearer without disgracing him before others, that he may stop."
"6920","As Ishaq b. Ibrahim narrated... al-Amash, from Muslim, from Masruq, from Aisha: when something reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about a man he would not say: what is with so-and-so saying so, but: what is with peoples saying so."
"6921","As Fahd b. Sulayman narrated... al-Amash, from Muslim, from Masruq: Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did something he licensed, some left it; it reached him, so he preached, praised Allah and said: what is with peoples refraining from what I do; by Allah I am most knowing of Allah and most fearing Him."
"6922","As Fahd narrated... al-Amash, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq: Aisha said: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did a matter among what was licensed him; it reached him some desire away from it, so he rose preaching: what is with men desiring away from a matter I do, and I am most knowing of Allah and most fearing Him. His manners were best; he would not face one with dislike but address a group till the doer knows his stance as restraint. Likewise reported from Anas as from Aisha."
"6923","As Ahmad b. Shuayb narrated... Hammad b. Zayd, from Salm al-Alawi: I heard Anas b. Malik narrate: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seldom faced a man with dislike. One day a man entered with saffron trace while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ate pumpkin which he liked; when he left he said: order this to wash it. So his word about absentees from congregation is of this kind, Allah knows his intent. An objector: how ascribe one man's act to a group? Answer: it is allowed; the Quran has the like: His saying about Abdullah b. Ubayy's: if we return to Madina the mightier will expel the humbler, then He said: they are those who say; the saying was one's, Abdullah b. Ubayy b. Salul."
"6924","As Ibn Abi Maryam narrated... al-Firyabi... from al-Agharr, from Khalifa b. Husayn, from Zayd b. Arqam: I sat with Abdullah b. Ubayy b. Salul; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed with companions, they winked; when he passed Abdullah said: if we return to Madina the mightier will expel the humbler. I came to Sad b. Ubada, told him, he came to the Prophet and mentioned it; he sent to Abdullah who threatened; Abdullah swore by Him Who sent prophethood he did not speak it. The Prophet looked to Sad, who said: O Messenger of Allah, the boy Zayd b. Arqam told me; Sad took my hand, brought me, Abdullah scolded me, I sobbed to the Messenger weeping: by Him Who sent prophethood he said it. The Prophet fell silent toward him; then Allah sent: When the hypocrites come saying we bear witness you are Allah's Messenger, and Allah knows you are His Messenger, and Allah bears witness the hypocrites are liars."
"6925","As Ahmad b. Dawud narrated... Muhammad b. Fulayh, from Musa b. Uqba, from Abdullah b. al-Fadl al-Hashimi, from Anas b. Malik: Zayd b. Arqam complained to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he heard Abdullah b. Ubayy b. Salul in the Banu al-Mustaliq campaign say: if we return to Madina the mightier will expel the humbler. Abdullah came, excused, swore; the Ansar belied Zayd; Allah sent: they say if we return to Madina the mightier will expel the humbler. He called Zayd on a march, took his hand, saying: this is who I saw speaking what he heard. Do you not see the speaker of if we return was one, then Allah ascribed after it: they are those who, to a group, since they did not deny or rebut him, so by leaving it they were like him in saying; likewise one staying home from prayer without excuse, with neighbours seeing and not denying, they are like him in staying though they did not do the blameworthy staying; so the Messenger could include all in the threat in the mentioned hadith."
"6926","Chapter on his lifting retaliation from the slave who cut another's slave's ear. Ahmad b. Dawud b. Musa... Qatada, from Abu Nadra, from Imran b. al-Husayn: a slave of rich people cut the ear of a slave of poor people, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made no retaliation between them."
"6927","Yahya b. Uthman... Muadh b. Hisham with the like, save he said: a slave of poor people cut the ear of a slave of rich people. Here is a fiqh point on slaves' sub-lethal injuries to one another: some, like Abu Hanifa and companions, say no retaliation between them therein, though retaliation between them for lives; others obligate retaliation therein as between free. The first cite Imran's hadith, and for lives' retaliation as between free they cite."
"6928","What Ibn Abi Dawud narrated... Musaddad... Yahya b. Said, from Said b. Abi Aruba, from Qatada, from al-Hasan, from Qays b. Ubad: I and al-Ashtar went to Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) asking: did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) covenant to you what he did not to people? He said: no, except what is in this writing; he produced a writing from his sword scabbard: believers' bloods are equal, the lowest seeks protection for them, they are one hand against others; no believer is killed for a disbeliever, nor a covenanter in his covenant; who innovates an event it is on himself; who innovates or shelters an innovator upon him is Allah's, angels' and all men's curse. In it: Muslims' bloods equal, and the lowest, the slave, grants protection; showing slaves' bloods equal free Muslims', requiring retaliation between slaves and free, and among themselves more so. Correcting both this and Imran's is worthier than dropping some by some: retaliation among slaves for lives, and leaving it for lesser, since lives admit no return to values and are made equal, while lesser returns to equality and value equivalence..."
"6929","As Sulayman b. Shuayb narrated... Zuhayr b. Muawiya, from al-Hasan b. al-Hurr, from al-Hakam: the slave is not retaliated from the slave for intentional or mistaken wounds; the wounded's compensation is by his price upon the injurer's folk till the injurer's master chooses: ransom his slave or surrender him wholly. Al-Hakam mentioned it from Ibrahim and al-Shabi from Abdullah b. Masud. He made him wealth, showing Abdullah's view that slaves' sub-lethal injuries return to wealth requiring equivalence in values, while for lives they are like free with no value return. If asked: Ibrahim and al-Shabi did not meet Abdullah? Answer: we reported from him earlier his saying to al-Amash: when I say Abdullah said I say it only after a group narrated it from him; when I say so-and-so narrated from Abdullah, that is who told me; what he unattributed is stronger than by a named man."
"6930","Chapter on his commanding Abdullah b. Amr to fast, and making for fasting one day in ten days, two days nine, three days eight. Malik b. Yahya... al-Jurayri, from Yazid b. Abd Allah b. al-Shikhkhir Abu al-Ala, from his brother Mutarrif, from Abdullah b. Amr b. al-As (may Allah be pleased with him): I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: O Messenger of Allah, command me to fast. He said: fast one day and you have nine. I said: I find strength, add. He said: fast two days and you have eight days. I said: I find strength. He said: fast three days and you have seven days. He kept lowering till he said: the best fast is Dawud's, peace be upon him: fasting a day and breaking a day. Abdullah said: how hard; would I had accepted what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me."
"6931","Ibrahim b. Marzuq... Hammad b. Salama... Thabit, from Shuayb b. Abd Allah b. Amr, from his father that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: fast a day and you have ten days. He said: add. He said: fast two days and you have nine days. He said: add, I have strength. He said: fast three days and you have eight days. Thabit said: I narrated it to Mutarrif, who said: I see but he added in work and decreased in reward."
"6932","Ali b. Shayba... Rawh b. Ubada... Hammad with the like. In this: he made for Abdullah b. Amr in the first day ten days meaning reward of ten days, then with the added day nine days meaning nine days' reward, and with the next added day eight days meaning eight days' reward. An objector: how, when more work deserves more reward, each day standing alone deserving its reward, so two days less than one and three less than two? Answer: the first day he commanded for its recompense ten likes, with strength left for prayer, Quran recitation and other nearness deeds some better than fasting, like what we reported from Abdullah b. Masud that he would not fast, asked why, saying: when I fast I weaken from the Quran; in another's hadith: from prayer and Quran, and prayer is dearer to me than fasting. So the Messenger commanded Abdullah the fast with which remains his strength reaching these deeds, and continues..."
"6933","Chapter on Dawud's (peace be upon him) fasting a day breaking a day, the most beloved fast to Allah. Yunus and Isa b. Ibrahim al-Ghafiqi... Sufyan b. Uyayna, from Amr b. Dinar, from Amr b. Aws, from Abdullah b. Amr (may Allah be pleased with him): the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: the most beloved fast to Allah is Dawud's: he fasted a day and broke a day."
"6934","Bakr b. Idris... Adam b. Abi Iyas. And Ibrahim b. Marzuq... Rawh b. Ubada: Shuba, from Ziyad b. al-Fayyad: I heard Abu Iyad: I heard Abdullah b. Amr narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then the like."
"6935","Bakkar b. Qutayba and Ali b. Shayba... Rawh... Ibn Jurayj, from Amr b. Dinar that Amr b. Aws told him from Abdullah b. Amr: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: the most beloved fast to Allah is Dawud's: he fasted half the time. An objector: herein Dawud's fast is most beloved, with increase over the mentioned fast in the prior chapter! Answer: no conflict with the first chapter's hadith; for this reports Dawud's (peace be upon him) fast, and he and other prophets bear in fasting what others do not; see his continuous fasting after forbidding people, explaining he is unlike them, fed and given drink, not they."
"6936","As Yunus and al-Muzani narrated... Malik from Nafi from Abdullah b. Umar: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade continuous fasting; it was said: you fast continuously. He said: I am not like you; I am fed and given drink."
"6937","As al-Muzani narrated... Abd al-Wahhab b. Abd al-Majid al-Thaqafi, from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas b. Malik: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fasted continuously, so they fasted; it reached him and he said: if the month were extended I would continue such that the extremists leave extremism; I am not like you; my Lord feeds me and gives drink. Do you not see he bore in fasting what his ummah does not, sufficing without breaking as they cannot, so continuous fasting permitted him for what is with him not others; others therein blameworthy, he praiseworthy. So Dawud, peace be upon him, in his fast likewise; hence Allah praised his fast. What also shows little fasting preferred over much with accompanying nearness causes."
"6938","What Abu Umayya narrated... Misar b. Kidam, from Habib b. Abi Thabit, from Abu al-Abbas, from Abdullah b. Amr: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: have I not been told you fast the time and stand the night? I said: I am able. He said: if you do, the soul wearies and the eye sinks. I said: I am able. He said: fast three days each month. I said: I am able. He said: fast my brother Dawud's fast: he fasted a day broke a day, and would not flee when meeting."
"6939","What Yunus narrated... Shuba, from Habib b. Abi Thabit: I heard Abu al-Abbas, a Makkan, a poet, unaccused in hadith: I heard Abdullah b. Amr, then the like. Do you not see he reported of Dawud (peace be upon him) with the mentioned fast that he would not flee when meeting for remaining strength, and his fast did not remove such strength, unlike others weakened in body cutting from that? So what Allah praised of Dawud therein was for that meaning, and what the Messenger loved for Abdullah and chose of fasting is what does not cut from the like, as in the reports we narrated. We found the Messenger prefer some breakers over fasters in some stations."
"6940","Muhammad b. Amr b. Yunus... Abu Muawiya al-Darir, from Asim, from Murraq al-Ijli, from Anas b. Malik (may Allah be pleased with him): we went with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, alighting a fiercely hot day; among us fasters and breakers, most shaded the cloak-owner, some shielded from sun by hand; the fasters fell, the breakers rose pitching buildings and watering mounts; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: the breakers took the reward today. See his preferring breakers strong by breaking for the deeds they did, made thereby with breaking better than fasters disabled by fasting. In this is uncovering the meanings we mentioned in the prior chapter."
"6941","Chapter on his forbidding selling food till two measures run in it. Ishaq b. Ibrahim... Muslim al-Jarmi... Makhlad b. al-Husayn, from Hisham, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Huraira: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade selling food till two measures run in it, so the owner has increase and upon him decrease. Abu Jafar: the best present is that it means the buyer's measuring after buying from his seller before selling it to another by measure; sale is unlawful for the buyer therein till he measures the due measuring by the sale between them after his seller's prior measuring from his seller before his second sale, so sale is unlawful for the second buyer of what he bought by measure till preceded by the two measurings with the measure. If asked why mention between seller and his seller when the food may come by gift or charity without measuring: answer: the folk were traders buying and selling, addressed concisely; they knew the ruling in two sales each requiring other than the other's measuring; had he addressed only the latter they'd not know the former's. He added a fine meaning jurists need: increase in the second measure over the first is the seller's..."
"6942","Chapter on his commanding Dubaa bint al-Zubayr b. Abd al-Muttalib to stipulate in ihram: her release where detained. Al-Rabi b. Sulayman al-Muradi... Said b. Salim, from Ibn Jurayj: Abu al-Zubayr told me he heard Tawus and Ikrima freedman of Ibn Abbas report from Ibn Abbas: Dubaa bint al-Zubayr, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her when she told him: I am a heavy woman wanting Hajj, how order me to enter? He said: enter and stipulate: my release where You detain me; so she attained Hajj."
"6943","Ahmad b. Khalid b. Yazid... Ali b. al-Madini... Hisham b. Yusuf... Ibn Jurayj... Abu al-Zubayr heard Tawus and Ikrima report from Ibn Abbas, then the like."
"6944","Yazid b. Sinan... Abu Amir al-Aqadi... Rabah b. Abi Maruf, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Dubaa: perform Hajj and stipulate: my release where You detain me."
"6945","Fahd b. Sulayman... Arim Abu al-Numan... Thabit b. Yazid... Hilal b. Khabbab: I asked Said b. Jubayr about a man in Hajj stipulating? He said: stipulation is between people. He said: Ikrima narrated to me from Ibn Abbas that Dubaa bint al-Zubayr b. Abd al-Muttalib came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: O Messenger of Allah, I want Hajj, how shall I say? He said: say Labbayka, and my release from the earth where You detain me, for you have on that what you excepted."
"6946","Muhammad b. Amr b. Yunus... Abdullah b. Numayr... Hisham b. Urwa, from his father, from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her): the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon Dubaa bint al-Zubayr visiting her and said: perhaps you want Hajj. She said: I am ill. He said: perform Hajj and stipulate; say: O Allah, my release where You detained me."
"6947","Ahmad b. Khalid b. Zayd... Ali b. al-Madini... Abd al-Razzaq... Mamar, from Hisham b. Urwa, from his father, from Aisha, the like."
"6948","Ahmad b. Shuayb... Ishaq b. Ibrahim... Abd al-Razzaq... Mamar, from Hisham, from his father, from Dubaa bint al-Zubayr: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon her complaining, she mentioned Hajj, and he said: perform Hajj and stipulate, saying: O Allah, my release where You detained me. Mamar and al-Thawri differed over Hisham in this chain as mentioned."
"6949","Nasr b. Marzuq... al-Khasib... Umar b. Ali al-Muqaddami, from Hisham b. Urwa, from his father, from Abu Dhuayb al-Aslami: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Dubaa, then he mentioned the hadith like Mamar's and al-Thawri's besides other narrators from Hisham b. Urwa; so Hisham's hadith became confused to us thereby."
"6950","Ahmad b. Khalid... Ali b. al-Madini... Abd al-Razzaq... Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwa, from Aisha: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon Dubaa bint al-Zubayr, and she said: I want Hajj, ill. He said: perform Hajj and stipulate to release where detained. Abu Jafar: we found this hadith from al-Zuhri from Urwa only in what we narrated without confusion. Then we returned to Hisham's hadith."
"6951","Al-Rabi b. Sulayman al-Muradi... Asad... Hammad b. Salama, from Hisham b. Urwa, from his father, from Dubaa bint al-Zubayr: she said: O Messenger of Allah, I want Hajj and I see I cannot. He said: perform Hajj and stipulate, saying: O Allah, my release where detained. Abu Jafar: so al-Rabi narrated from Asad from Hammad from Hisham."
"6952","Muhammad b. Khuzayma... Hajjaj... Abu Salama... Hisham b. Urwa, from his father that Dubaa said: O Messenger of Allah, I see but ill, I see I cannot Hajj. He said: perform Hajj and stipulate: O Allah, my release where You detained me. So al-Hajjaj opposed Asad from Hammad b. Salama from Hisham in what we mentioned of their difference."
"6953","Ibrahim b. Abi Dawud... Yusuf b. Adi... al-Qasim b. Malik al-Muzani, from Uthman b. Hakim: Abu Bakr b. Abd Allah b. al-Zubayr narrated to me from his grandmother: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon Dubaa asking: what kept you, O aunt, from Hajj? She said: I am sick fearing detention. He said: go and stipulate: my release where You detained me. These are the reports we found on Dubaa's stipulation, some free of the confusion mentioned establishing proof. We looked for what repels it: we found the Messenger's hadith of al-Hajjaj b. Amr al-Aslami earlier in this book: whoever breaks or lames has released, and upon him another Hajj. Ikrima mentioned this hadith, and we mentioned choosing his saying has released means: it is lawful to release, without standing on a narration requiring it and barring other interpretation; then it became clear after Dubaa's hadith: the worthier in that release is exiting the ihram in which the preventing event befell blocking his Hajj. We understood, since neither therein nor in Dubaa's did the Prophet command a hady as the besieged is commanded to release thereby, that was the first ruling; then Allah made the ruling for one kept from Hajj by bodily inability or enemy barring: upon him hady, released only by its slaughter, as in: complete Hajj and Umra for Allah, if besieged then what is easy of hady, and shave not heads till hady reaches its place. It is a firm verse. It is reported from Ibn Abbas with confirming al-Hajjaj b. Amr, and what we mentioned his confirming."
"6954","What Yazid b. Sinan narrated... Yahya b. Said al-Qattan, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Alqama on: complete Hajj and Umra for Allah, if besieged: when a man is besieged he sends hady. And shave not heads till hady reaches its place; then whoever among you is ill or has ailment of head, ransom of fasting, charity or rite: fasting three days; if he hastens shaving before hady reaches, ransom of fasting, charity or rite: three days fasting, or feeding six poor each half sa, and rite a sheep; when safe from what was with him. Then whoever enjoys Umra to Hajj. If he proceeds on that face, upon him Hajj; if he delays Umra to next, upon him Hajj and Umra. Then what is easy of hady; whoever finds not, fasting three days in Hajj, last day Arafah, and seven when returned. Ibrahim said: I mentioned it to Said b. Jubayr, who said: this is Ibn Abbas's saying, and he عقد thirty."
"6955","What Abu Shurayh Muhammad b. Zakariya and Ibn Abi Maryam narrated... al-Firyabi... Sufyan al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Alqama on: if besieged: from detention or illness. Ibrahim said: I narrated it to Said b. Jubayr, who said: so Ibn Abbas said. So we understood Ibn Abbas's confirming al-Hajjaj b. Amr on release without hady at breaking and lameness, that was the first ruling in that hadith, and his saying reported by Said b. Jubayr barring release with breaking and lameness till hady slaughtered per the verse we recited, that is the ruling the matter returned to in this event; Dubaa's hadith is like al-Hajjaj b. Amr's, abrogated by this verse, the ruling returned to what is therein, barring the besieged by breaking, lameness or else from releasing till hady slaughtered for him. Abdullah b. Umar would deny stipulation in Hajj, saying: the Messenger's sunna suffices you, meaning the later besieged and its ruling in the verse we recited. Some reported from him: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not stipulate in his Hajj."
"6956","As Yunus narrated... Yunus b. Yazid, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim: Ibn Umar would deny stipulation in Hajj, saying: the sunna of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) suffices you: if one of you is detained, he circumambulates the House and Safa-Marwa, then releases from everything till next year's Hajj, offering or fasting."
"6957","As Ubayd b. Rijal narrated... Ahmad b. Salih... Abd al-Razzaq... Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar: he disliked stipulation in Hajj, saying: does not your Prophet's sunna suffice you that he did not stipulate; if a detainer detains one, when he reaches the House he circumambulates and between Safa-Marwa, then shaves or shortens, then releases, with Hajj next year. Abu Jafar (may Allah have mercy on him): Ubayd b. Rijal told us: Ahmad said: this phrase he did not stipulate none says but Mamar. So Ibn Umar says what we mentioned; impossible he denied it only after it reached him from those we mentioned or others; impossible with his piety and knowledge he repels what is reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except by what requires repelling, as abrogation or else. If one says: though Ibn Umar repelled it, others of the Prophet's companions allowed and acted by it."
"6958","He mentioned what Muhammad b. Khuzayma narrated... Hajjaj b. Minhal... Hammad, from Ayyub, Hisham and Habib, from Muhammad b. Sirin: Uthman b. Affan stood at Arafah when a man came; Uthman said to him: did you not stipulate, or why did you not stipulate. Our answer: this is broken in chain; hadith folk do not argue by its like. He said: it is reported from Aisha therein."
"6959","He mentioned what Muhammad b. Amr narrated... Abdullah b. Numayr, from Hisham, from his father: Aisha ordered me to stipulate when I Hajj, saying: O Allah, I intend Hajj, to it I head; if eased for me it is Hajj, if detained it is Umra. Our answer: what is in Aisha's hadith here differs from her Dubaa hadith; for in the Dubaa story the Prophet (peace be upon him) ordered her to stipulate: my release where You detained me, that is release exiting Hajj not to Umra, while in what she ordered Urwa therein is exiting if detained from Hajj to Umra; and that Umra may be the Umra obligatory on one missing Hajj till released thereby from that Hajj. In Urwa's hadith is sound proof of abrogating Dubaa's mentioned hadith. This speaker said: people after Aisha would stipulate."
"6960","He mentioned what Rawh b. al-Faraj narrated... Yusuf b. Adi... Abu al-Ahwas, from al-Mughira, from Ibrahim: they liked to stipulate at ihram. Our answer: he did not mention in this hadith what they stipulated thereat; it may be what is in Dubaa's hadith, and may be what is in Urwa's hadith of what Aisha ordered therein. Then we ourselves examined what they stipulated."
"6961","Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: They used to stipulate in Umrah and Hajj, saying: O Allah, I intended Hajj if it becomes easy, otherwise Umrah if it becomes easy, otherwise there is no blame upon me. Through that we learned that what they stipulated meant sincerity, as in the hadith of Urwah which Aishah instructed him with, and in that is confirmation of the abrogation of the hadith of Dubaa. This objector said: but in this hadith is “otherwise there is no blame upon me.” Our answer: their saying “otherwise there is no blame upon me” was not explained by those they refer to. Its meaning in our view, and Allah knows best, is: no blame upon me for not performing what I entered ihram for, for that is not by my choice but compelled by necessity. Then we examined the jurists of the regions, of the two Sanctuaries and others whose fatwa is followed, like Abu Hanifah and his companions, Malik and his companions, al-Shafii and his companions, and found them all contrary to the hadith of Dubaa, so their opposition is a proof by consensus for rejecting it, and Allah Almighty does not unite the ummah of His Prophet upon misguidance, and success is from Allah."
"6962","Chapter explaining the problematic reports about the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying with the people while carrying Umamah on his neck, putting her down when he bowed and taking her back when he rose. 5917 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, Ibn Ajlan narrated to us, from al-Maqburi, from Amr ibn Sulaym al-Zuraqi, from Abu Qatadah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with them with Umamah bint Abi al-As on his neck; when he bowed he put her down, and when he stood he carried her."
"6963","5918 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan, Amir ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr and Said ibn Abi Said al-Maqburi narrated to us, from Amr ibn Sulaym al-Zuraqi, from Abu Qatadah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"6964","5919 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Khalid ibn Makhlad al-Qatawani narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ajlan narrated to me, Amir ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr and Said ibn Abi Said al-Maqburi informed me, from Amr ibn Sulaym al-Zuraqi, from Abu Qatadah al-Ansari, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"6965","5920 - Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, Amir ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr informed me that Amr ibn Sulaym al-Zuraqi informed him that he heard Abu Qatadah saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did so, then he mentioned the same."
"6966","5921 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him from Amir ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from Amr ibn Sulaym al-Zuraqi, from Abu Qatadah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray while carrying Umamah bint Zaynab bint Rasul Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), whose father was Abu al-As ibn Rabiah ibn Abd Shams; when he prostrated he put her down, and when he stood he carried her."
"6967","5922 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us, Abd al-Hamid ibn Jafar narrated to us, al-Maqburi narrated to us, from Amr ibn Sulaym al-Zuraqi, he said: I heard Abu Qatadah saying: While we were sitting in the mosque awaiting prayer, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us with his daughter's daughter Umamah bint Abi al-As, whose mother was Zaynab bint Rasul Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), carrying her on his shoulder; he said takbir while she was on his shoulder until he completed his prayer, doing that with her throughout."
"6968","5923 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Attab, from Amr ibn Sulaym al-Zuraqi, from Abu Qatadah: that he saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) carrying Umamah, or Umayyah bint Abi al-As, his daughter's daughter, while standing in prayer; he carried her when he stood and put her down when he bowed until he finished. An objector said: this is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with these sound acceptable chains, so how do you forbid the like? Our answer: there were things the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did in his prayer upon which there is no disagreement among the scholars that people may not do them in their prayers; among them is his stretching his hand to take the cluster he saw from Paradise while praying."
"6969","5924 - As Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abdullah ibn Abbas, he said: The sun eclipsed, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed, then he mentioned the eclipse prayer and how he prayed it, then he said: They said, O Messenger of Allah, we saw you reach for something in this place, then we saw you step back, so he said: I saw Paradise, or I was shown Paradise, and I reached for a cluster from it, and had I taken it you would have eaten from it as long as the world remains. And there is no disagreement among the scholars that a praying person should not do the like of this in his prayer. And among that is what he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did with Iblis while praying."
"6970","5925 - As Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, from Rabiah ibn Yazid, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, from Abu al-Darda, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood, and we heard him saying: I seek refuge in Allah from you, then he said: I curse you with the curse of Allah Almighty three times, then he extended his hand as if reaching for something. When he finished prayer they said, O Messenger of Allah, we heard you say in prayer something we never heard before, and we saw you extend your hand, so he said: The enemy of Allah Iblis came with a flame of fire to cast it in my face, so I said: I seek refuge in Allah Almighty from you, but he did not retreat; I said: I curse you with the perfect curse of Allah, but he did not retreat; then I intended to seize him, and had it not been for the supplication of our brother Sulayman ibn Dawud, he would have remained tied up for the children of Madinah to play with. And there is no disagreement that a praying person should not do this. So we understood these words and deeds were permissible in prayers at the times he did them, then abrogated afterwards, and rulings returned to what the scholars hold; for they do not unite contrary to what he did except after abrogation is established; for they, may Allah be pleased with them, are trusted in what they did as in what they narrated. An objector said: do you narrate any evidence from his sayings? Our answer is:"
"6971","5926 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, Sharik ibn Abdullah informed us, from al-Amash, from al-Musayyab ibn Rafi, from Jabir ibn Samurah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the mosque and saw people praying with raised hands, so he said: Why do I see you raising your hands as if they are the tails of restive horses; be still in prayer. What he commanded in this hadith is evidence against the earlier reports we narrated; for the stillness commanded here is the opposite of the movements done in the earlier reports. If he says: is there clearer evidence of abrogation? Our answer is:"
"6972","5927 - Al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, and Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, then both agreed, each saying: Ismail ibn Abi Khalid informed us, from al-Harith ibn Shubayl, from Abu Amr al-Shaybani, from Zayd ibn Arqam, he said: We used to speak in prayer until there was revealed: Guard strictly the prayers, and the middle prayer, and stand before Allah devoutly. So we were commanded to be silent. And qunut was humility and attention to what the devout is engaged in, undistracted by other word or deed. In what we mentioned is evidence of the abrogation of what we described of its opposites, and that those opposites were permissible at that time then prohibited afterwards, and practice continued upon what opposes them and agrees with what we narrated, and Allah Almighty would not unite the ummah of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon misguidance, and in this chapter is sufficiency, and Allah is the Granter of success."
"6973","Chapter explaining the problematic statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever cuts off the wealth of a Muslim man by his oath, Allah forbids Paradise to him and makes Hell obligatory for him. 5928 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq and Abu Umayyah narrated to us, they said: Umar ibn Yunus narrated to us, the wording being Ibrahim's, Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, Tariq ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Kab, whose father Kab was one of the three who were left behind, Abu Umamah narrated to me, leaning his back against this pillar of the pillars of the mosque of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: I, your father Kab ibn Malik, and your brother Muhammad ibn Kab were sitting at this pillar, discussing a man who swears over another's wealth and cuts it off falsely, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Any man who swears falsely over wealth and cuts it off by his oath, Paradise is absolved of him and Hell is obligatory for him. So your brother Muhammad ibn Kab said: O Messenger of Allah, even if little? He turned a miswak between his fingers and said: Even if a miswak of arak, even if a stick of arak."
"6974","5929 - Al-Muzani narrated to us, al-Shafii narrated to us, from Malik ibn Anas, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Mabad, from Abdullah ibn Kab, from Abu Umamah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever cuts off the right of a Muslim by his oath, Allah forbids Paradise to him and makes Hell obligatory for him. They said: Even if something small? He said: Even if a stick of arak, he said it three times."
"6975","5930 - Al-Muzani narrated to us, al-Shafii narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, Jami and Abd al-Malik narrated to us, they heard Abu Wail narrating, from Abdullah ibn Masud, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever swears an oath to cut off thereby the wealth of a Muslim man will meet Allah Almighty while He is angry with him, then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited to us from the Book of Allah Almighty: Indeed those who purchase with the covenant of Allah and their oaths a small price, the verse."
"6976","5931 - Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Jannad narrated to us, Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Yazid ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Humayd ibn Hilal narrated to us, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever swears an oath to cut off thereby the wealth of a Muslim man will meet Allah Almighty while He is angry with him."
"6977","5932 - Fahd narrated to us, Umar ibn Abd al-Wahhab al-Riyahi Abu Hafs narrated to us, Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Umayyah, from Umar ibn Ata ibn Abi al-Khuwar, from Ubayd ibn Jurayj, from al-Harith ibn al-Barsa, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while walking between two sheets of Hijaz: Whoever takes something of his brother's wealth by a sinful oath, let him take a house in Hell."
"6978","5933 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Umayyah, from Ibn Abi al-Khuwar, he said: I heard al-Harith ibn Malik ibn al-Barsa: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, and he did not mention Ubayd ibn Jurayj in his hadith: Whoever cuts off the wealth of a Muslim man by a false oath will meet Allah Almighty while He is angry with him. In these reports is a man cutting off his brother's wealth by a false oath. A questioner asked about that cutting off, what is it? Our answer: the cutting off here in our view, and Allah knows best, is that when a man usurps something from another, the victim may demand it from the usurper, the usurper must produce it, and the judge must not intervene between claimant and defendant until he helps him against the one claimed upon and administers oath; when he swears, the judge leaves the defendant with the thing sworn over to dispose as he wishes, thus cutting it off. If he refuses to swear, scholars differ, except their consensus on refusal of oath indicates a proof is established for the claimant against the defendant. One group says: judgment is given for him so he deserves it against the judged one; among them Abu Hanifah, al-Thawri and their followers. Another group says: the oath transfers to the claimant so he deserves it thereby and judgment is given; before the defendant's refusal he did not deserve it by his oath, but only after refusal. When refusal is a proof for the claimant, one with a proof is not asked for another proof, just as with admission or with evidence judgment is given without demanding another proof. So judgment must be given by his proof without burdening another. We found the same meaning from Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him."
"6979","As Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, Qudamah ibn Muhammad ibn Qudamah al-Madini the freedman of Ashja narrated to us, Makhramah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from his father, he said: I heard Jafar ibn Rabiah saying: I heard Kab ibn Alqamah saying: I heard Abdullah ibn Awf of the people of Palestine saying: A woman ordered her slave girl to lie with her husband, so he thought she was his own slave girl and had intercourse unknowingly. So Uthman ibn Affan said: Make him swear he did not know; if he refuses to swear then stone him, and if he swears then flog him one hundred lashes, flog his wife one hundred lashes, and flog the slave girl the hadd. In this hadith Uthman judged refusal of oath as admission, and we know of no Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) opposing or objecting to him in that, and in that is strengthening of what we described, and success is from Allah."
"6980","Chapter explaining the problematic obligation concerning people differing over whether magic endures and acts, or is null and does not act, from what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 5934 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Farwah ibn Abi al-Magh ra narrated to us, Ali ibn Mushir informed us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was bewitched until he would imagine doing something while he had not done it. She said: He supplicated in my house, then said to me: O Aishah, did you know Allah Almighty has given me a ruling in what I sought His ruling about? Two men came to me; one sat at my head and the other at my feet. One said to his companion: What ails the man? He said: Bewitched. He said: Who bewitched him? He said: Labid ibn al-Asam. He said: In what did he bewitch him? He said: In a comb and combings, and the sheath of a male palm spathe. He said: Where? He said: In the well of Dharwan. So I went to it, its water was like henna infusion and its palm tops like devils heads, so I ordered it filled up. I said: O Messenger of Allah, did you take it out? He said: No. Allah has cured me, and I disliked stirring up evil for people from it."
"6981","5935 - Fahd narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Yazid ibn Hayyan, from Zayd ibn Arqam, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was bewitched by a Jewish man, so he fell ill, and Jibril, blessings of Allah upon him, came to him with the two Muawwidhatayn and said: A Jewish man bewitched you, and the magic is in such-and-such well. So he sent Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who brought it; he ordered him to undo the knots and recite a verse, so he recited and untied until the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood as if released from bonds. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned nothing of it to that Jew nor showed it in his face. In these two hadiths is evidence that magic continued to act until the time the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was bewitched as therein, and when its continuance until that time is possible, its continuance afterwards is possible."
"6982","Chapter explaining the problematic obligation concerning scholars differing over a man saying: So-and-so has upon me from such to such, by what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 5936 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Said, he said: Umar said: O Messenger of Allah, I heard so-and-so praising you well, claiming you gave him two dinars? He said: But so-and-so does not say that; he received from me from one hundred to ten, then he said: One of you leaves me with his begging tucked under his arm or the like, and it is nothing for him but Fire. So Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Why do you give him? He said: What should I do? They ask me, and Allah Almighty refuses miserliness for me. In this hadith is the saying: he received from me from one hundred to ten, which falls under a fiqh chapter disputed: a man saying: you have upon me from one dirham to ten dirhams. Some say: nine dirhams are due, among them Abu Hanifah; some say: eight, among them Zufar; some say: ten, among them Abu Yusuf and Muhammad; some say: nothing is due, for he acknowledged what is between one and ten entirely, and there is nothing between them. What is in this hadith refutes this last view; for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the most eloquent of people, informed he had given that man a gift deserving thanks which he did not thank. What we found of Arab speech agrees: they say: we were rained upon from Zubalah to Thalabiyyah, and: he has twenty from she-camel to camel, meaning between she-camel and camel, the number being twenty of those two kinds. Likewise al-Kisai reported hearing a Bedouin upon seeing the crescent say: praise be to Allah from your crescent to your concealment; both crescent and concealment enter therein. Likewise: shanq from five to five, meaning between five and five including the first and last five mentioned. So likewise the saying: he received from me from one hundred to ten includes the hundred with the ten. In this is confirmation of Abu Yusuf and Muhammad's view. An objector said: they agree that one saying: so-and-so has from this wall to this wall, gets what is between them, nothing of the walls, though standing on the meaning requiring the first ruling, with no difference. Our answer: the walls are two specified things acknowledged between them, so what is between enters his admission; but the other admission is not specified, only an admission between two unqualified things, like what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with Arab speech. Endpoints of mentioned things are sometimes not included, as His saying: then complete the fast until night, night not included; and sometimes included, as: wash your faces and hands up to the elbows and wipe your heads and your feet up to the ankles, elbows and ankles included. This shows they sometimes include the endpoint and sometimes not. Hence Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, said of the tenth dirham: since it may be included or not, he did not include it; yet he said of one selling his slave with choice until tomorrow: he has choice until tomorrow passes, since tomorrow may enter or not, so he did not make the sale binding until its obligation is known. As for the first ruling with what came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), it sufficed us from further speech, and success is from Allah."
"6983","Chapter explaining the problematic report indicating that when a man is asked: Did so-and-so narrate such to you? and he says: Yes, he thereby stands as the originator uttering all of it. 5937 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Muslim informed me, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I attended the Fitr prayer with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and with Abu Bakr, Umar and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them; each prayed it before the sermon, then preached afterwards. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) descended, and I can see him seating men with his hand. Then he came cutting through them until he reached the women, with Bilal; he recited: O Prophet, when the believing women come to you pledging that they will not associate anything with Allah, until His saying: Indeed Allah is Forgiving, Merciful. When he finished he said: Are you upon that? One woman among them, none else answering, said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah, Hasan not knowing who she was. He said: Then give charity. Bilal spread his garment and said to them: Cast in; so they cast rings and finger rings into Bilal's garment. In this hadith the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sufficed with her saying Yes, intending them to be upon it, establishing them thereby in the place of speakers, and the like is narrated from him in other meanings."
"6984","5938 - As Bahr ibn Nasr ibn Sabiq narrated to us, he said: It was read to Shuayb ibn al-Layth: your father informed you, from Said ibn Abi Said, from Sharik ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Namir: that he heard Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: While we were sitting in the mosque, a man entered upon a camel, made it kneel in the mosque, tied it, then said: Which of you is Muhammad the Messenger of Allah? The Messenger of Allah reclining among them. We said: The white reclining man. The man said: O son of Abd al-Muttalib. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have answered you. The man said: O Muhammad, I will ask you, pressing hard in questioning, so do not resent me. He said: Ask whatever occurs to you. The man said: I adjure you by your Lord and the Lord of those before you, did Allah send you to all people? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, yes. He said: I adjure you by Allah, did Allah command you to pray the five prayers in day and night? He said: O Allah, yes. He said: I adjure you by Allah Almighty, did Allah command you to fast this month of the year? He said: O Allah, yes. He said: I adjure you by Allah, did Allah command you to take this charity from our rich and distribute among our poor? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, yes. The man said: I believe in what you brought, and I am the messenger of those behind me of my people, and I am Dimam ibn Thalabah, brother of Banu Sad ibn Bakr."
"6985","5939 - And as al-Husayn ibn al-Hakam al-Hibari narrated to us, Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, Thabit narrated to us, from Anas, he said: We were forbidden in the Quran to ask the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about anything, and we liked that an intelligent Bedouin come and ask him, for he was bolder than us. So a man came and said: O Muhammad, your messenger came to us claiming you claim Allah Almighty sent you. He said: Yes, he spoke truth. He said: Who created the heaven? He said: Allah. Who created the earth? He said: Allah. Who erected these mountains? He said: Allah. He said: By the One who created heaven, earth and these mountains, did Allah send you? He said: Yes. He said: Your messenger claimed we owe five prayers in our day and night. He said: Yes. He said: By the One who sent you, did Allah command you this? He said: Yes. He said: Your messenger claimed we owe fasting a month in our year. He said: He spoke truth. He said: By the One who sent you, did Allah command you this? He said: Yes. The man left saying: By the One who sent you with truth, I will neither add nor detract anything. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If he is truthful he will enter Paradise. In what we narrated is evidence that answering with Yes is affirmation of the speaker's words by the tongue. We found higher than this in the Book of Allah Almighty: And the companions of Paradise will call to the companions of Fire: we found what our Lord promised us true, did you find what your Lord promised true? They said: Yes. By saying Yes they were as if saying: we found what our Lord promised us true. In this is evidence that one to whom a hadith is read, when the reader addresses him: did you hear so-and-so? Did so-and-so inform you? Did so-and-so narrate such to you? and he says: Yes, it is as if he utters those things with his tongue until he says: I heard from him. Likewise scholars agree on a man saying Yes to one wanting him as witness, saying: bear witness that he made me witness of such, and acknowledged such before me."
"6986","Chapter explaining the problematic reports about what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say when bidding farewell to one he bade farewell. 5940 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, Anas ibn Iyad al-Laythi informed me, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, from Yahya ibn Ismail ibn Jarir, from Qazaah, he said: I was with Abdullah ibn Umar and wanted to leave, so he said: Stay as you are until I bid you farewell as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bade me farewell. He said: He took my hand and shook it, then said: I entrust to Allah your religion, your trust, and the final outcomes of your deeds."
"6987","5941 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Said ibn Abi Ayyub and al-Layth ibn Sad informed me, from al-Hasan ibn Thawban: that he heard Musa ibn Wardan saying: I came to Abu Hurayrah to bid him farewell for a journey I intended, so Abu Hurayrah said: Shall I not teach you, O son of my brother, something the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught me to say at farewell? I said: Yes. He said: Say: I entrust you to Allah whose trusts are never lost. Abu Jafar said: What is in this hadith falls short of the first hadith, and one who memorized is worthier than one who fell short."
"6988","5942 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abu Jafar al-Khatmi, from Muhammad ibn Kab, from Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Khatmi, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dispatched an army he would reach Thaniyyat al-Wada and say: I entrust to Allah your religion, your trust, and the final outcomes of your deeds. Abu Jafar said: We reflected on the two hadiths of Ibn Umar and Abdullah ibn Zayd about the Messenger entrusting the trust of the bidden one along with his religion. That in our view, and Allah knows best, is because trust among people stands like faith which is religion, as narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which we mentioned earlier in this book: There is no faith for one without trust. So faith which is the existence of religion only exists with trust and is negated with its absence; thus we understood it was made like it, each guaranteed by the other, so he entrusted it as he entrusted his religion to Allah, Glory be to Him, and success is from Allah."
"6989","Chapter explaining the problematic report about the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying Marhaban wa Ahlan, what is meant by them? Abu Jafar, may Allah have mercy on him, said: We mentioned earlier in this book the hadith of Masruq from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, about Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her, coming to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his fatal illness, and his saying to her: Welcome, my daughter. 5943 - Muhammad ibn Sulayman al-Baghandi narrated to us, al-Himmani narrated to us, Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from Ibn Abi Juhayfah, from his father: that a group of Banu Amir came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said to them: Welcome."
"6990","5944 - Al-Baghandi narrated to us, Abu Ghassan narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Humayd narrated to us, Abd al-Karim ibn Salit narrated to us, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father: that Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, met the Prophet, peace be upon him, and he said to him: Welcome and family."
"6991","5945 - Al-Baghandi narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, from Firas, from al-Shabi, from Masruq, from Aishah: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Fatimah: Welcome."
"6992","5946 - Al-Baghandi narrated to us, al-Himmani narrated to us, Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Yazid al-Raqashi, from Anas: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the Ansar: Welcome. Abu Jafar said: A questioner asked about the meaning of these two words, Marhaban wa Ahlan, what is it? Our answer: spaciousness of places is their vastness, as in Allah's saying: until the earth straitened upon them despite its spaciousness. As for family, what is meant is: when you descend to the station of a man among his family in whose stay is your comfort. Likewise what is narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in addressing Ali when he came proposing Fatimah to him."
"6993","5947 - As Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Ismail al-Nahdi narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Humayd narrated to us, Abd al-Karim ibn Salit informed us, Abu Ghassan said: I asked about him and they said: Basran of Khurasan people, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, he said: A group of Ansar said to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: You have Fatimah in mind, so he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and greeted him. He said: What does the son of Abu Talib need? He said: O Messenger of Allah, Fatimah daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Welcome and family, adding nothing more. Ali went out to those Ansar awaiting him; they said: What happened? He said: I do not know, but he said to me: Welcome and family. They said: One of the two suffices you from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he gave you family and gave you welcome. Later after he married her he said: O Ali, a wedding must have a feast. Sad said: I have a ram, and a group of Ansar gathered sa's of corn. On the wedding night he said: Do nothing until you meet me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for water, performed ablution from it, then poured it upon Ali and said: O Allah, bless them both, bless upon them both, and bless in their progeny. Abu Ghassan said: Progeny is from women. What is in this hadith of the Messenger addressing Ali with Marhaban wa Ahlan, and what the Ansar understood from it, is evidence for our interpretation of these two words, and success is from Allah."
"6994","Chapter explaining the problematic reports about combining two paternal aunts, two maternal aunts, and combining maternal and paternal aunt. 5948 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Sulayman al-Muaddib narrated to us, Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, Marwan ibn Shuja narrated to us, from Khusayf, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade combining paternal aunt and maternal aunt, two maternal aunts, and two paternal aunts. Abu Jafar said: What is in this hadith of forbidding combining agrees with what is narrated about combining a woman with her paternal aunt and with her maternal aunt; for each of the woman and her paternal aunt, and the woman and her maternal aunt, if one of them were a man it would be unlawful for him to marry the other, so it is not proper to combine them by marriage with him. Some held that combining two paternal aunts or two maternal aunts only meant one named by the other's name by proximity, as it is said al-Umaran for Abu Bakr and Umar and the like of Arab speech, applied only at necessity, and there is no necessity here, for we found two paternal aunts possible otherwise. As for two maternal aunts: that two men each marry the other's daughter, so daughters born to each, if female, are each the other's maternal aunt, so it is unlawful to combine them; for if one were a man it would be unlawful to marry the other. As for two paternal aunts: that two men each marry the other's mother and beget a daughter, so each daughter is the other's paternal aunt, for each daughter is the other's sister from his mother, thus the paternal aunt of his daughter, so it is unlawful to combine them; for if one were a man it falls under his forbidding marrying a woman upon her paternal or maternal aunt."
"6995","5949 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade marrying a woman upon her paternal aunt or upon her maternal aunt."
"6996","5950 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur and Surayj ibn al-Numan narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, Umar ibn Abi Salamah informed us, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"6997","5951 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, Dawud informed us, from al-Shabi, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade marrying a woman upon her paternal aunt or upon her maternal aunt, forbade marrying her upon her brother's daughter or her sister's daughter, and forbade marrying the elder upon the younger or the younger upon the elder. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is an addition over other narrations, namely forbidding marrying the younger upon the elder and the elder upon the younger, and its meaning in our view, and Allah knows best, is upon the elder in lineage and upon the younger in lineage, as it is said concerning wala: wala is for the kubr, meaning seniority in lineage."
"6998","5952 - Nasr ibn Marzuq and Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Qabisah ibn Dhuayb, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade combining a woman with her paternal aunt, and a woman with her maternal aunt."
"6999","5953 - Al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"7000","5954 - Al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us. 5955 - And Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, my father and Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, then both agreed, each saying: al-Layth narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Abd al-Malik, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: A woman is not married upon her paternal aunt nor upon her maternal aunt."
"7001","5956 - Al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Irak ibn Malik, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade four women to be combined: a woman with her paternal aunt, and a woman with her maternal aunt."
"7002","5957 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same. Abu Jafar said: This is what we found of the chains by which this hadith is narrated from Abu Hurayrah. It is also narrated from Jabir ibn Abdullah from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) likewise."
"7003","5958 - What Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Asim, he said: I presented to al-Shabi a book containing: from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: A woman is not married upon her paternal aunt nor upon her maternal aunt, so al-Shabi said: I heard it from Jabir."
"7004","5959 - And what Yazid narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, Sufyan informed us, from Asim al-Ahwal, from al-Shabi, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"7005","5960 - And what Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Asim, from Amir, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same. This is what we found of the paths by which this hadith is narrated from Jabir from the Prophet, peace be upon him. It is also narrated from Abdullah ibn Amr from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"7006","5961 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami narrated to us, Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Tawus narrated to us, he said: I heard Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he forbade marrying a woman upon her paternal aunt or upon her maternal aunt. Abu Jafar said: We do not know it narrated from Abdullah ibn Amr except from this path. It is also narrated from Abu Said al-Khudri from the Prophet, peace be upon him."
"7007","5962 - As Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, Mualla ibn Mansur narrated to us, Ibn Luhayah narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Musa, from Makhul, from Ibn Muhayriz, from Abu Said al-Khudri, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he forbade marrying a woman upon her paternal aunt or upon her maternal aunt. Abu Jafar said: We do not know this meaning narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from other than these paths we narrated, and success is from Allah."
"7008","Chapter explaining the problematic report of his saying: I witnessed with my uncles the pact of al-Mutayyabin. 5963 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Ismail ibn Ulayyah narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from al-Zuhri, from Muhammad ibn Jubayr ibn Mutim, from his father, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I witnessed as a boy with my uncles the pact of al-Mutayyabin, and I would not love red camels in exchange for breaking it."
"7009","5964 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, Yaqub ibn Ibrahim, meaning al-Dawraqi, informed us, from Ibn Ulayyah, then he mentioned the same with his chain."
"7010","5965 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Wahb ibn Baqiyyah al-Wasiti narrated to us, Khalid, meaning Ibn Abdullah al-Wasiti, narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from al-Zuhri, from Muhammad ibn Jubayr ibn Mutim, from his father, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"7011","5966 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, Ismail ibn Masud narrated to us, Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal informed us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from al-Zuhri, from Muhammad ibn Jubayr ibn Mutim, from his father, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same. Abu Jafar, may Allah have mercy on him, said: We examined this hadith and found it only from this path, narrated from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, who is not like other narrators of al-Zuhri above his rank in transmitting from al-Zuhri. In it is the Messenger witnessing the pact of al-Mutayyabin, while genealogists agree that pact was long before the Year of the Elephant, in eight clans of Quraysh: Hashim, al-Muttalib, Abd Shams, Nawfal sons of Abd Manaf, Taym ibn Murrah, Asad ibn Abd al-Uzza, Zuhrah ibn Kilab, and al-Harith ibn Fihr, when Banu Abd Manaf sought to take siqayah and liwa from Banu Abd al-Dar; these eight allied, and Umm Hakim bint Abd al-Muttalib sent them a bowl of perfume in which they dipped hands then struck the Kaaba confirming their pact, hence called al-Mutayyabin. Then they left what was with Banu Abd al-Dar as it was, fearing fighting and Arab intervention. The birth of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was afterwards in the Year of the Elephant."
"7012","5967 - As Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Yahya ibn Main narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq, from Abu Ishaq, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was born in the Year of the Elephant."
"7013","5968 - And as Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Yahya ibn Main narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from al-Muttalib ibn Abdullah ibn Qays ibn Makhramah, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: I and the Prophet were born in the Year of the Elephant."
"7014","5969 - And as Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sinan narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, my father narrated to me, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrating from al-Muttalib ibn Qays ibn Makhramah, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: I and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were born in the Year of the Elephant. He said: Uthman ibn Affan asked about him, Qubath ibn Ashyam, saying: Are you older or the Messenger of Allah? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is greater, and I am earlier in birth."
"7015","5970 - As Ahmad ibn Khalid ibn Yazid al-Farisi narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Thabit narrated to us, al-Zubayr ibn Musa narrated to us, from Abu al-Huwayrith, he said: I heard Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan saying to Qubath ibn Ashyam al-Kinani, then al-Laythi: O Qubath, are you older or the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is greater than me, and I am older than him in age; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was born in the Year of the Elephant. Matters proceeded as we mentioned until a man of Zubayd came to Makkah with merchandise, sold it to al-As ibn Wail al-Sahmi, who delayed and overpowered him over it. That drove him to ascend Abu Qubays where Quraysh held councils, then he began saying: Ashath Sahm. When Quraysh heard that they deemed grave what al-Sahmi did, so they allied at that the pact of al-Fudul. What they contracted is what Muhammad ibn Ishaq mentioned."
"7016","As Abu al-Rawwad Abdullah ibn Abd al-Salam narrated to us, Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham narrated to us, Ziyad ibn Abdullah al-Bakkai narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Muttalibi, he said: As for the pact of al-Fudul: tribes of Quraysh gathered in the house of Abdullah ibn Judan: Banu Hashim, Banu al-Muttalib, Asad ibn Abd al-Uzza, Zuhrah ibn Kilab, and Taym ibn Murrah. They contracted and allied that they would not find in Makkah any wronged one among its people or strangers entering it except they would stand with him against his wrongdoer until they restore his right. Quraysh called that pact Hilf al-Fudul. Its people mentioned in this hadith were all Mutayyabin, for they were of the Mutayyabin of the first pact we mentioned. So the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith we narrated: I witnessed with my uncles the pact of al-Mutayyabin, is the Hilf al-Fudul which the Mutayyabin allied, they being those who were in the first pact which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not witness. Thus by praise of Allah it is clear that hadith was not contradictory, having this explanation we mentioned."
"7017","5971 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, Abu Musab al-Zuhri narrated to us, Imran ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Zuhri narrated to me, Rabiah ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman informed me, he said: I entered upon Abu al-Abbas, meaning the Commander of the Believers, and he asked me about nothing except wiping over the khuffs and Hilf al-Fudul, and that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I witnessed a pact in the house of Ibn Judan: Banu Hashim, Zuhrah and Taym, I among them; had I been called to it in Islam I would have answered, and I would not love to break it even for red camels. He said: Their alliance was upon commanding good, forbidding evil, and leaving no due with anyone except taking it. Hence called Hilf al-Fudul. Rabiah omitted who of those three clans were of Banu Asad ibn Abd al-Uzza. That pact was the noblest pact of Jahiliyyah, which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) witnessed; called Hilf al-Fudul and also pact of al-Mutayyabin, since its people were all Mutayyabin. With this pact al-Husayn ibn Ali threatened al-Walid ibn Utbah in the dispute between them, when al-Walid attempted injustice against him."
"7018","As Abu al-Rawwad narrated to us, Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham narrated to us, Ziyad ibn Abdullah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq said: Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn Usamah ibn al-Had al-Laythi narrated to me that Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith al-Taymi narrated to him that there was a dispute between al-Husayn ibn Ali and al-Walid ibn Utbah ibn Abi Sufyan over property between them at Dhi al-Marwah, and al-Walid was overbearing toward al-Husayn ibn Ali with his authority concerning his right. So al-Husayn ibn Ali said: I swear by Allah, you will do me justice in my right, or I will take my sword, then stand in the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then call for Hilf al-Fudul. Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, who was with al-Walid when al-Husayn said that, said: I swear by Allah, if he calls for it I will take my sword and stand with him until he is given justice or we all die. It reached al-Miswar ibn Makhramah ibn Nawfal al-Zuhri, and he said the like; it reached Abd al-Rahman ibn Uthman ibn Abdullah, meaning al-Taymi, and he said the like. When that reached al-Walid ibn Utbah he gave al-Husayn justice until he was satisfied. Banu Abd Shams, Banu Nawfal and Banu al-Harith ibn Fihr, though of the first pact's people, had not entered that pact."
"7019","As Abu al-Rawwad narrated to us, Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham narrated to us, Ziyad narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, he said: Muhammad ibn Jubayr ibn Mutim ibn Adi ibn Nawfal Abd Manaf came, he being the most knowledgeable of Quraysh, to Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan when he killed Ibn al-Zubayr and people united upon Abd al-Malik. When he entered upon him he said: O Abu Said, were we and you, meaning Banu Abd Shams ibn Abd Manaf and Banu Nawfal ibn Abd Manaf, not in Hilf al-Fudul? He said: You know better. Abd al-Malik said: Inform me, O Abu Said, with the truth of that. He said: No, by Allah, we and you left it. He said: You spoke truth. That was for the Zubaydi reaching his right. The custodian of that pact and its upholder was al-Zubayr ibn Abd al-Muttalib ibn Hashim, uncle of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"7020","Chapter explaining the problematic report of his saying: Circumambulation of the House is prayer, except that Allah Almighty permitted speech therein, so whoever speaks let him speak only good. 5972 - Al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us. 5973 - And Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, then both agreed, each saying in his hadith: al-Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Circumambulation of the House is prayer, except that Allah Almighty permitted speech for you, so whoever speaks let him speak only good. Abu Jafar said: We examined this hadith, finding it with this chain only from this path. We found its narrator al-Fudayl ibn Iyad, and others besides him narrating from Ata ibn al-Saib, other than al-Thawri, the two Hammads: Hammad ibn Salamah, Hammad ibn Zayd, and Yazid ibn Zuray, are weakened by isnad scholars; for their hearing from him was after confusion, while the four we mentioned heard before that."
"7021","5974 - We found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj informed me, from al-Hasan ibn Muslim, from Tawus, from a man who met the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Circumambulation is but prayer, so when you circumambulate minimize speech."
"7022","5975 - Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, Nuaym narrated to us, from Ibn al-Mubarak, from Ibn Jurayj, then he mentioned the same with his chain. Thus we established this is the origin of this hadith, from a man who met the Prophet, peace be upon him, not from Ibn Abbas; that man may have met the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) without seeing him. Since so, no proof stands with this hadith by isnad scholars. What is intended by this hadith is a fiqh meaning disputed: one group says: whoever performs the obligatory tawaf of the House junub must repeat it; if he does not until he returns home he owes blood, and that tawaf suffices him; among them Abu Hanifah and his companions, may Allah have mercy on them. Others among Hijaz scholars and besides say: that tawaf does not suffice him, with them like one who did not circumambulate. Since they differed thus, finding nothing in Allah's Book nor the Prophet's Sunnah, we return to qiyas. The agreed origin is that entering ihram for Hajj and Umrah people are ordered to do only pure, as ordered not to circumambulate the House except so; one who enters ihram impure, whether junub or without wudu, wrongs in doing so, but his wrong does not prevent his ihram being a valid entry into what he entered. So likewise tawaf: one who circumambulates as described deserves blame yet his tawaf is valid and sufficing. Likewise they agree one who stands at Arafah or spends night at Muzdalifah junub or without wudu, that suffices him despite the wrongdoing contrary to Allah's command."
"7023","Chapter explaining the problematic report of his answer to one asking: When were you a prophet? by his saying: While Adam was between spirit and body. 5976 - Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi narrated to me, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, from Ibn Abi al-Jada, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, when were you a prophet? He said: While Adam was between spirit and body."
"7024","5977 - Fahd narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Awaqi narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, from Budayl ibn Maysarah, from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, from Maysarah al-Fajr, he said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): When were you a prophet? He said: I was a prophet while Adam was between spirit and body. An objector said: How do you accept this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the most eloquent Arab, while all linguists object to it; for bayn with them is only for two, not one? Our answer: as you said, but when one is described with two attributes he enters the meaning of two, and what applies to two may be used for him. Of that is Allah's saying: And know that Allah comes between a man and his heart. Man and his heart are one, but since described differently he became as two. Likewise Adam, first a body without spirit, then Allah made him a body with spirit, described with two different states, so bayn is allowed in his description as in the hadith. As for his saying: I was a prophet while Adam was between spirit and body: though he was then a prophet, Allah had written him in the Preserved Tablet as prophet, then rewrote him at the time mentioned, as He said: And We wrote in the Zabur after the Dhikr that My righteous servants shall inherit the earth. He had written that in the Preserved Tablet, then rewrote it in the arranged Zabur afterwards. Likewise His writing the Prophet, peace be upon him, while Adam was between spirit and body, after writing him before in the Preserved Tablet as such, and success is from Allah."
"7025","Chapter explaining the problematic reports by which scholars differing over taking wage for judging decide, some permitting and others forbidding. Abu Jafar said: We know no predecessor reported to forbid that except Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, from a chain, with the contrary reported from him from another chain. As Fahd narrated to us, Abu Ghassan narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father: that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Do not take wage for any Muslim judgment. What was narrated from him contrary to that from the other chain is:"
"7026","5978 - As Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, my uncle Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Busr ibn Said, from Ibn al-Saidi, thus he said: He said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, employed me over charity; when I delivered it to him he gave me my wage, so I said: I worked for Allah Almighty, my reward is upon Allah. He said: Take what I gave you; I worked at the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he paid me, so I said like your saying, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: When I give you something without your asking, eat and give charity."
"7027","5979 - And what al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Shuayb ibn al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah, from Busr ibn Said, from Ibn al-Saidi al-Maliki that he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, employed me over charity, then he mentioned the same word for word."
"7028","5980 - And what Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from Bukayr, from Busr ibn Said, from Ibn al-Sadi, then he mentioned the same. Thus al-Layth narrated this hadith in Iraq saying: from Ibn al-Sadi, while before in Egypt he said: from Ibn al-Saidi. In this is from Umar contrary to the first hadith. The correct in the disagreement from al-Layth of Ibn al-Sadi or al-Saidi is Ibn al-Sadi. Al-Sadi is a man of Banu Amir ibn Luayy among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his name Abdullah ibn Waqdan, called al-Sadi because nursed among them. We mentioned what is narrated from him with his name and lineage earlier in this book in the chapter of Hijrah: has it ceased or not while disbelievers are fought?"
"7029","5981 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Abu al-Yaman al-Hakam ibn Nafi al-Bahrani narrated to us, Shuayb ibn Abi Hamzah informed us, from al-Zuhri, al-Saib ibn Yazid narrated to me that Huwaytib ibn Abd al-Uzza informed him: that Abdullah ibn al-Sadi informed him that he came to Umar ibn al-Khattab in his caliphate, and Umar said to him: Was I not informed that you undertake works of Muslims, and when given wage you dislike it? He said: Yes. He said: What do you intend by that? I said: I have horses, slaves, and I trade, and I want my wage to be charity upon Muslims. Umar said: Do not do; I intended what you intended, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would give me gifts, so I would say: Give it to one poorer than me. Until once he gave me, and I said that to him, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take it and acquire it; what comes to you of this wealth without aspiring nor asking, take it, else do not follow it with yourself. In this hadith about Abdullah, disputed in narrations from al-Layth, is evidence the correct is Ibn al-Sadi, not Ibn al-Saidi."
"7030","5982 - Muhammad ibn Uzayz al-Ayli narrated to us, Salamah ibn Rawh narrated to us, from Uqayl ibn Khalid, from Ibn Shihab, al-Saib ibn Yazid nephew of Namir narrated to me: that Huwaytib ibn Abd al-Uzza informed him: that Abdullah ibn Sad ibn Abi Sarh informed him that he came to Umar ibn al-Khattab in his caliphate, then he mentioned the same, except he said: Take it, draw near with it, and give charity. In this hadith in place of Abdullah ibn al-Sadi is Abdullah ibn Sad ibn Abi Sarh, while people oppose him in this chain. Among opposers is Amr ibn al-Harith."
"7031","5983 - As Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim ibn Abdullah, from his father: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would give Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, gifts, and Umar would say to him: O Messenger of Allah, give it to one poorer than me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Take it and acquire or give charity with it; what comes to you of this wealth without aspiring, thus Yunus said, nor asking, take it, and what not, do not follow it with yourself. Salim said: Hence Ibn Umar would neither ask anyone anything nor refuse anything given him."
"7032","5984 - And as Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Amr said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me the same, from al-Saib ibn Yazid, from Huwaytib ibn Abd al-Uzza, from Abdullah ibn al-Sadi, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Except we found for what Salamah narrated from Uqayl from Ibn Shihab in opposing people a supporter agreeing with him."
"7033","5985 - As Musab ibn Ibrahim ibn Hamzah al-Zubayri narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from al-Darawardi, from Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Muslim ibn Shihab, from Muhammad, then he mentioned the same as the hadith of Muhammad ibn Uzayz from Salamah exactly, saying therein that Abdullah ibn Sad ibn Abi Sarh in place of what others said: Abdullah ibn al-Sadi. In what is narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, in this second section is contrary to the first section. We examined this disagreement; worthier is the second section permitting wage for such judgments; for we found in Allah's Book evidence permitting wage for the like: wage upon charity for its workers for their work and collection for its people, though workers are not of its people due to wealth and its prohibition upon them thereby, in His saying: Charity is but for the poor, the needy, and those employed upon it. Likewise wage for governors of Muslim cities for guarding them, fighting behind them, repelling aggressors, is allowed from Muslim wealth; likewise pay for their troops indispensable, and collectors of kharaj in gathering, تحصيل and guarding for its due channels, wage allowed for undertaking it. Since so, one undertaking Muslim judgments, taking from bodies what is due to Allah therein, from wealth what is due, from some for others in body and wealth, preventing non-obligatory claims by his authority, wage for him from Muslim wealth is likewise allowed."
"7034","Chapter explaining the problematic report about his writing blood-money upon each clan. 5986 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote upon each clan its blood-moneys, and said: No freedman of a people shall ally except by their permission. He said: And I found in his sheet: and he cursed. Abu Jafar said: We examined this hadith, finding in it the Prophet, peace be upon him, writing blood-moneys of each clan's offenses upon that clan. It is understood therein are relatives of the offender of varying closeness among that clan, yet he wrote it upon all his clan, not intending nearest to exclusion of farther, but the clan he is of, making its members' offenses upon that clan. This shows one should not single out for blood-money any one of the clan to exclusion of another. This supports what Kufan and Madinan jurists hold in loading aqilah compensations upon offenders' clansmen united with them in that clan, unless unable, then nearest clans are joined until the due is borne. Contrary to others like al-Shafii: that the aqilah is seen from the offender's paternal brothers bearing his compensation; if unable, raised to sons of his grandfather, then sons of his great-grandfather, and so upward when nearer unable; for all, though varying in closeness, are of the clan he is of. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote each clan's blood-money upon that clan, not upon its nearest to the offender to exclusion of farther members. Also narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, indicating this meaning."
"7035","As Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, Said ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, Sad ibn Tariq narrated to us, from Nuaym ibn Abi Hind, from Salamah ibn Nuaym, he said: I witnessed with Khalid ibn al-Walid the day of Yamamah; when we pressed the people I wounded a man among them; when he fell he said: O Allah, upon Your religion and Your Messenger's religion, and I am quit of what Musaylimah is upon. So I tied a thread on his leg and went on with the people. When I returned I called: Who knows this man? Some people of Yemen passed me and said: This is a man of Yemen people of the Muslims. I returned to Madinah in Umar's time and narrated this hadith to him. He said: You did well; go, upon you and your people is the blood-money, and upon you is freeing a believing slave. Do you not see Umar in this hadith said to Salamah ibn Nuaym: upon you and your people is the blood-money? He did not say: upon your nearest kin of your asabah. Al-Shafii mentioned, as al-Muzani quoted in his Mukhtasar: that Umar sent for a woman who panicked and miscarried her fetus; Umar consulted Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, about it, and he said: Upon you is its blood-money. He said: I adjure you to stand until you distribute it upon your people; Ali's people are Banu Hashim, Umar's people Banu Adi. That shows he intended loading the due upon Banu Adi and others besides them, and in that is evidence for what we mentioned."
"7036","Chapter explaining the problematic report of his forbidding calling a hypocrite: chief. 5987 - Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Jawaribi narrated to us, Uthman ibn Talut narrated to us, Muadh ibn Hisham narrated to us, from his father, from Qatadah, from Abdullah ibn Buraydah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Do not say to a hypocrite: chief, for if he is your chief you have angered your Lord. Abu Jafar said: We examined this hadith; the chief deserving leadership is one with lofty qualities deserving it, distinguished thereby from those he leads, as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the Ansar when Sad ibn Muadh came after judging Banu Qurayzah with what he judged, after the Messenger said of that judgment: You judged among them with Allah's judgment from above seven heavens: Stand to your chief. We mentioned that with its chain earlier in this book. Of that is his saying, peace be upon him, to Banu Salamah: Who is your chief, O Banu Salamah? They said: al-Jadd ibn Qays. Then he was mentioned with miserliness, so he said: He is not your chief, but your chief is Bishr ibn al-Bara ibn Marur. We mentioned that with its chain earlier too."
"7037","And as Jabir ibn Abdullah said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, is our chief, and he freed our chief, meaning Bilal. As Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Salamah informed us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir, then he mentioned it. One deserving this name and station is of this description; the hypocrite is opposite, so he does not deserve to be chief. Whoever names him so places him contrary to the station Allah placed him in, thereby angering his Lord."
"7038","Chapter explaining the problematic report of his saying: Worship in turmoil is like emigration to me. 5988 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from al-Mualla ibn Ziyad, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from Maqil ibn Yasar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Worship in turmoil is like emigration to me."
"7039","5989 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, al-Mualla ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from Abu Iyas Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from Maqil ibn Yasar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same. Abu Jafar said: We found turmoil distracts its people from what is worthier: worshipping their Lord Almighty and keeping praiseworthy states obligatory upon them. Whoever occupies himself with worship in that state, busy with what he is commanded while others busy with blameworthy turmoil forbidden to enter and be of its people, thereby deserves the reward the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned in this hadith, and success is from Allah."
"7040","Chapter explaining the problematic reports over which scholars differed about allies: do they bear with those they allied the offenses of some, or do those allied bear their offenses, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? 5990 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Sad ibn Ibrahim, from his father, from Jubayr ibn Mutim: that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: There is no pact in Islam, and whatever pact was in Jahiliyyah, Islam only strengthened it. Thus Ibn Abi Maryam informed us this hadith with this chain."
"7041","5991 - Then Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated it to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn Sallam informed us, Ishaq al-Azraq narrated to us, from Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah, from Sad ibn Ibrahim, from Nafi ibn Jubayr ibn Mutim, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the same exactly."
"7042","5992 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, al-Wahbi narrated to us, Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered Makkah in the year of conquest he stood preaching and said: O people, whatever pact was in Jahiliyyah, Islam only strengthened it; there is no pact in Islam."
"7043","5993 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Ismail narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather Abdullah ibn Amr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same, he said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered Makkah in the year of conquest he stood preaching and said: O people, whatever pact was in Jahiliyyah, Islam only strengthened it; there is no pact in Islam."
"7044","5994 - Al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad narrated to us, Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Mughirah, from his father, from Shubah ibn al-Tawam al-Dabbi, he said: Qays ibn Asim asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about alliance, so he said: There is no pact in Islam, but hold to the pact of Jahiliyyah. That is: they continue it in Islam as they continued it in Jahiliyyah. But the pact contracted in Jahiliyyah that allies be as one clan in bearing for one another, since by pact they became of them in that station, and the allied tribe would bear blood-moneys of its offenders among them, so one entering by pact is reasonably likewise. This is a fiqh issue disputed: some say this, among them Abu Hanifah and his companions; some deny the pact is of this rank. What we mentioned of the pact in Jahiliyyah ordered to be held in Islam evidences what Abu Hanifah and his companions said. What confirms our saying is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"7045","5995 - Of what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Adi al-Kufi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Muhallab, from Imran ibn Husayn, he said: Thaqif captured two men of the Prophet's Companions, and the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), may Allah be pleased with them, captured a man of Banu Amir ibn Sasah. He was brought past the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bound. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to him, and he said: For what am I detained? He said: For the offense of your allies. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went on, and he called him, so he came to him. The captive said to him: I am Muslim. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had you said it while controlling your affair you would have succeeded fully."
"7046","5996 - And what Fahd narrated to us, Abu Nuaym al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Muhallab, from Imran ibn Husayn, he said: Al-Adba belonged to a man of Uqayl who was captured, so al-Adba was taken from him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by him, and he said: O Muhammad, for what do you seize me and seize the forerunner of pilgrims, while I have become Muslim? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Had you said it while controlling yourself or your affair you would have succeeded fully. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You were taken for the offense of your allies. Thaqif had captured two men of the Prophet's Companions. When allies are held for allies' offenses as they are held for cousins' offenses, as we mentioned, they are for taking blood-moneys of their offenses, and allies taking them from them is worthier. In this is evidence that allies bear for those they allied as a clan bears for one another."
"7047","Chapter explaining the problematic reports about the fastest good in reward and fastest sins in punishment. 5997 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Salih ibn Musa al-Talhi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Ishaq narrated to me, from Aishah bint Talhah, from Aishah Mother of the Believers, may Allah be pleased with her: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The fastest good in reward is righteousness and joining kinship, and the fastest evil in punishment: transgression and severing kinship."
"7048","5998 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Ansari narrated to us, Uyaynah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Jawshan narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Bakrah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no sin more deserving that Allah hasten its punishment for its doer in this world, along with what He stores for him in the Hereafter, than transgression and severing kinship."
"7049","5999 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yunus al-Basri narrated to us, Abu Abd al-Rahman Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Muqri narrated to us, Uyaynah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Bakrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same. Abu Jafar said: An objector said: Is punishment for transgression and severing kinship faster than punishment for disbelief in Allah Almighty? Our answer: what is in these two hadiths we mentioned is not what this objector thought. Nothing is graver with Allah Almighty than disbelief, nor punishment graver than upon it, unless repentance reaches its doer. What is meant in the two hadiths is punishment of one guilty of transgression and severing kinship among people of the Shariah who did not exit it thereby, and what he is threatened with is punishment for his transgression and severing kinship Allah commanded to join. Punishment for disbelief is severer than that, and success is from Allah."
"7050","Chapter explaining the problematic report about his commanding Uthman ibn Abi al-As to take a muadhdhin who takes no wage for his adhan. 6000 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kisai narrated to us, Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Said al-Jurayri narrated to us, from Abu al-Ala, from Mutarrif ibn al-Shikhkhir, from Uthman ibn Abi al-As, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Take a muadhdhin who takes no wage for his adhan. An objector said: In this hadith is evidence permitting wage for adhan. Our answer: We saw wage is by contracted hires before its due, taken by hirees exiting to hirers thereupon, and may be without contracted hires, but by rewards and bestowals upon doers. The Quran came with both meanings. As for wage due by prior contracts: His saying: If they suckle for you, give them their wages. Then He said: And consult among yourselves in kindness. Consultation is only upon disagreement over what hires are contracted. As for wage otherwise: His saying: Say: I ask you no wage for it, nor am I of the pretenders. And: Say: Whatever wage I asked you is yours. That is upon rewards for deeds; for hire contracts were before them. The saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Uthman ibn Abi al-As in this hadith is upon wage given as reward and bestowal, as people do with those doing praiseworthy deeds like adhan in their mosques, maintaining them and attending them without hiring, so they bestow upon them to continue and strengthen them therein, without prior hires. That is praiseworthy of doers; those done to among them accept or not, knowing its cause; one refusing is superior, one accepting inferior. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded Uthman to take the best muadhdhin, highest in rank, for reward upon adhan and leaving worldly compensation. Qiyas also prevents deserving wage by hires upon adhan; for hires are transfer of hirees' benefits to hirers for the wealth hired with, and upon each transferrer for a consideration is delivery manifestly distinct. Adhan and its likes are incapable of that, so qiyas is that hires upon them are impermissible, and success is from Allah."
"7051","Chapter explaining the problematic report of his saying: Whichever Muslim I flogged, cursed or reviled, make that for him purification and nearness. 6001 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ikrimah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her: that she saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: O Allah, I am but human, so whichever Muslim man I reviled or harmed, do not punish me for it."
"7052","6002 - Al-Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, Abu Zurah Wahbullah ibn Rashid al-Hijri narrated to us, Haywah ibn Shurayh informed us, Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, that he heard Urwah ibn al-Zubayr saying: I heard Aishah wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Two men came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him, but he gave them nothing; then they asked again and he gave nothing; then they asked again, so he reviled and cursed them. He entered with reddened face showing anger. I said: The two men failed and perished; nothing befell them from you, yet you cursed them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I covenanted with my Lord a covenant, saying: O Lord, I am human, angered as humans anger; so whichever believer I reviled or cursed, do not punish him for it nor torment him, and make it for him purification and reward."
"7053","6003 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, al-Amash narrated to us, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, from Aishah: She said: Two men entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), secluded with him, so he reviled and cursed them and expelled them. I said: O Messenger of Allah, none received good from you like these two did. He said: Do you not know what I stipulated upon my Lord Almighty? I said: O Allah, I am but human, so whichever Muslim man I reviled or cursed, make it for him purification and reward."
"7054","6004 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, Abu al-Zubayr informed us that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: I am but human, and I stipulated upon my Lord Almighty: whichever Muslim servant I reviled or insulted, that it be for him expiation and reward."
"7055","6005 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq both narrated to us, they said: Umar ibn Yunus narrated to us, Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah narrated to me, Anas ibn Malik narrated to me, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I stipulated upon my Lord Almighty, saying: I am but human, pleased as humans please, angered as humans anger; so whichever one of my ummah I supplicated against with a supplication he does not deserve, make it for him purification, purity and nearness bringing him near You on the Day of Resurrection."
"7056","6006 - Abu Umayyah and Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abd al-Rahman al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whichever Muslim I cursed or reviled, make it for him charity and mercy."
"7057","6007 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Arim Abu al-Numan narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, then he mentioned the same with his chain, except he said: either prayer or mercy."
"7058","6008 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, Said ibn al-Musayyab informed me, from Abu Hurayrah: that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: O Allah, whichever believing servant I reviled, make that for him nearness to You on the Day of Resurrection."
"7059","6009 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from al-Hajari, he said: I heard Abu Iyad that he heard Abu Hurayrah narrating from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: O Allah, I am but human, angered as humans anger, pleased as humans please; so whichever Muslim I cursed undeservedly, make it for him prayer and reward."
"7060","6010 - Muhammad ibn al-Numan narrated to us, al-Humaydi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, Abu al-Zinad narrated to us, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, I take with You a covenant You will never break: whichever Muslim man I harmed; flogged him, reviled him, cursed him, make it for him prayer, purity and supplication for him. Abu al-Zinad said: It is the dialect of Abu Hurayrah; it is but jaladtuhu."
"7061","6011 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Muadh ibn Muadh al-Anbari narrated to us, al-Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from his father, al-Sumayt narrated to us, from Abu al-Sawwar, Abu al-Sawwar narrates from his maternal uncle, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) walking with people following him, so I followed with them. The people shielded themselves with me, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came upon me and struck me, either he said: with a palm stalk, a stick, a miswak, or something with him. By Allah, it did not pain me. I spent the night saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) struck me only for something Allah Almighty informed him of me. I resolved to come to him in the morning. He said: Jibril, blessings of Allah upon him, descended upon the Prophet and said: You are a shepherd, so do not break the horns of your flock. He said: When he prayed Fajr, or he said: we morninged, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: People follow me, and I dislike them following me. O Allah, whomever I struck or reviled, make it for him expiation and reward, or he said: forgiveness, or as he said."
"7062","6012 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Arim narrated to us, Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from his father, then he mentioned the same with his chain. Abu Yusuf used to say about these reports: they prove that when a man says to another: Free whichever of my slaves you wish, he may thereby free them all, and ayy may be upon all of them, as the Prophet's saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): whichever Muslims I did to what is mentioned, is upon whoever is done to as in these reports. Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated that of his saying from his father from him. Muhammad ibn al-Hasan opposed him, holding herein that what is upon one of the speaker's slaves, not all. Muhammad ibn al-Abbas narrated that of his saying from Ali ibn Mabad from him. Evidence is cited for him from the Quran and reports in Arab tongue. As for the Quran: His saying in the story of the Cave companions: Send one of you with this silver to the city, let him see which is purest food. That was upon one food, not all food. Of that is His saying in the story of Musa, blessings of Allah upon him: Whichever of the two terms I fulfill, there shall be no transgression against me. Ma is connective, so that was upon one term, not both, with like examples from the Quran. As for reports evidencing that:"
"7063","6013 - Of what Muhammad ibn al-Harith ibn Salih al-Makhzumi al-Madani and Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud both narrated to us, they said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdullah al-Uwaysi narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sad al-Zuhri narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf said: When we came to Madinah the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) brothered me with Sad ibn al-Rabi. Sad ibn al-Rabi said to me: I am the wealthiest Ansar in wealth; I will split half my wealth for you, and whichever of my two wives you desire I will relinquish for you, then when lawful you marry her. Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf said to him: I need none of that; is there a market with trade? He said: The market of Qaynuqa. Abd al-Rahman went to it in the morning and brought churned cheese and ghee. He said: Then he continued mornings, and soon he came with saffron trace upon him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Did you marry? He said: Yes. He said: Whom? He said: A woman of the Ansar. He said: How much dowry did you give her? He said: The weight of a date-stone of gold. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Give a feast even with a sheep."
"7064","6014 - And what al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: When Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf came to Madinah emigrating, he, meaning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), brothered him with Sad ibn al-Rabi al-Ansari. He spent that night with him; in the morning Sad said to him: Welcome, my brother; I am of the best Ansar in two wives and best in two gardens. Look at my two wives; whichever pleases your eye more I will divorce, then you marry her, for her people will not oppose me. And take my two gardens in the lowland, for they are dearer to me than my two in the highland. Abd al-Rahman said to him: May Allah bless for you your family and wealth; guide me to the market. He went to the market and returned with half a mudd profit. Then he frequented the market until he earned a date-stone weight of gold, married a woman with it, then came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Did you marry? He said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: How much did you give her? He said: A date-stone weight of gold. He said: Give a feast with a sheep. Sad's saying to Abd al-Rahman: whichever of my two wives you desire I relinquish for you, was not upon both wives, but upon one of them. Likewise a man's saying: Free whichever of my slaves you wish, is upon one slave, not all. We needed the ruling of ayy in these two meanings we mentioned: in the reports we began this chapter with it was upon uncountable, indeterminable ones, impossible to apply to all without leaving any; while in the second section it was upon countable known ones, whose sayer is capable of all. Thus we understood: upon uncountable, unachievable wholes it is upon what the addressed applies it to; while in countable measurable ones it is upon one of the mentioned kind, no more, as Muhammad ibn al-Hasan said therein, and success is from Allah."
"7065","Chapter explaining the problematic report of his saying to his questioner that he performed sai before tawaf: No harm. 6015 - Musa ibn Harun al-Burdi narrated to us, Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, meaning al-Shaybani, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out for Hajj; people would come to him, some saying: O Messenger of Allah, I performed sai before tawaf, delayed something, advanced something, and he would say: No harm, no harm, except a man who slanders a Muslim's honor unjustly; that is unto harm and ruin. This is a fiqh issue: most hold that sai between Safa and Marwah before tawaf of the House does not suffice, as one who did not perform sai. This is the saying of most regional jurists of Hijaz, Madinah and Iraq. We know no opponent except al-Awzai, from whom is narrated that sai suffices and he need not repeat after tawaf, and the like is narrated from Ata ibn Abi Rabah. Then returning to regional jurists besides Ata and al-Awzai, we found them differing about a qarin who shaves before slaughtering his hady sufficing for qiran: Abu Hanifah, Malik and Zufar say: upon him is fidyah for doing so, for he shaved before shaving was lawful. Most like Abu Yusuf, Muhammad and al-Shafii say: nothing upon him, citing what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein."
"7066","6016 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Abu Ahmad narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Said ibn Masruq al-Thawri narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Abi Rabiah, from Zayd ibn Ali, from his father, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I performed ifadah before shaving. He said: Shave, no harm. Another came and said: I slaughtered before stoning. He said: Stone, no harm."
"7067","6017 - And as Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from Mansur, meaning Ibn Zadham, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about one who shaved before slaughtering, or slaughtered before shaving. He said: No harm, no harm."
"7068","6018 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, al-Mualla ibn Asad narrated to us, Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that he was asked on the Day of Sacrifice, while at Mina, about slaughtering, shaving, stoning, advancing and delaying, and he said: No harm."
"7069","6019 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, Wuhayb narrated to us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was not asked that day about advancing anything before another except he said: No harm, no harm."
"7070","6020 - And what Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us: that Malik and Yunus narrated to him from Ibn Shihab, from Isa ibn Talhah ibn Ubaydullah, from Abdullah ibn Amr that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood in the Farewell Hajj for people asking him. A man came and said: O Messenger of Allah, unknowingly I shaved before slaughtering. He said: Slaughter, no harm. Another came and said: O Messenger of Allah, unknowingly I sacrificed before stoning. He said: Stone, no harm. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was not asked that day about anything advanced or delayed except he said: Do, no harm."
"7071","6021 - And as Yunus narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Isa ibn Talhah, from Abdullah ibn Amr, he said: A man asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: I shaved before slaughtering. He said: Slaughter, no harm. Another said: I slaughtered before stoning. He said: Stone, no harm."
"7072","6022 - And what Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Usamah ibn Zayd informed me that Ata ibn Abi Rabah narrated to him that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same, meaning he stood for people in the year of the Farewell Hajj while they asked him. A man came and said: Unknowingly I sacrificed before stoning. He said: Stone, no harm. Another said: O Messenger of Allah, unknowingly I shaved before slaughtering. He said: Slaughter, no harm. He was not asked about anything advanced or delayed except he said: Do, no harm. Abu Jafar said: What is in these reports is no proof for one citing them against opponents who say fidyah is upon the qarin shaving before slaughter; for the questioner of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) may be non-qarin, so that slaughter is non-obligatory, and what he did he did with nothing preventing him, and the Prophet's saying No harm therein means no sin upon you therein. If qarin, no sin upon him therein does not prevent fidyah with lifting of sin; for he did what he did unknowingly that better was otherwise. So harm is lifted, yet fidyah upon him, as in the hadith of Usamah ibn Sharik of the Prophet's answer to: I performed sai before tawaf, saying No harm, not preventing that he circumambulates then repeats sai afterwards. So likewise the other hadiths of lifting harm do not prevent fidyah upon doers. What strengthens that is that Ibn Abbas, one narrating that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this meaning:"
"7073","What Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, Wuhayb narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas."
"7074","And what Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Whoever advances something of his Hajj and delays, let him shed blood. That shows the Prophet's saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) No harm does not prevent fidyah upon one lifted of harm, as said by those we mentioned in this chapter, and success is from Allah."
"7075","Chapter explaining the problematic reports about commanding comprehensive supplication. 6023 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Yazid ibn Abd Rabbihi narrated to us, Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us, Shubah narrated to me, Jabr ibn Habib narrated to me, he said: I stayed with Fatimah daughter of Abu Bakr in Madinah, and she narrated to me from Aishah, she said: Abu Bakr entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while I was praying, and spoke to him words as if he disliked me to hear. He said: Upon you is the comprehensive perfect supplications. She mentioned this speech. Aishah said: I came to him and said: What is your saying comprehensive perfect? He said: Say: O Allah, I ask You of all good, immediate and deferred, what I know and what I do not know; I seek refuge in You from all evil, immediate and deferred, what I know and what I do not know; I ask You Paradise and what brings near to it of word and deed; I ask You of the good Your servant and Messenger Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked You; I seek refuge in You from Hell and what brings near to it of word and deed; I seek refuge in You from what Your servant and Messenger Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sought refuge from; and I ask You that what You decreed for me of affairs, make its outcome guidance."
"7076","6024 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, Shubah informed us, Jabr ibn Habib informed us, he said: I heard Umm Kulthum bint Ali narrating from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her: that Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to speak to him, while Aishah was praying. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Aishah, say: O Allah, I ask You of all good, then he mentioned the rest of the supplication therein. Baqiyyah and al-Nadr differed over Shubah about the woman this hadith is from: Baqiyyah said in his hadith she is Fatimah bint Abi Bakr, and al-Nadr said in his she is Umm Kulthum bint Ali. If the Fatimah mentioned is Abu Bakr's daughter, she is whom Abu Bakr told Aishah in his death illness: the one in the womb of Ibnat Kharijah occurred to my heart she is a girl, and she was born after his death. Then we examined the disagreement of al-Nadr ibn Shumayl and Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid over Shubah about the woman between Jabr ibn Habib and Aishah in this hadith, to stand upon the truth therein, Allah willing."
"7077","6025 - We found Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Darir narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah informed us. 6026 - And we found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us. Bakkar said in his hadith: from Jabr ibn Habib, and Ibrahim said in his: he said: Jabr ibn Habib informed us, then both agreed, saying: from Umm Kulthum bint Abi Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her. Bakkar said in his: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would say, and Ibrahim said in his: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught us this supplication, then both mentioned the supplication in the two hadiths of al-Nadr and Baqiyyah exactly. It strengthened in hearts that the correct in the disagreement of al-Nadr and Baqiyyah from Shubah about this woman's name is that she is Abu Bakr's daughter, not Ali's daughter. That is also strengthened by:"
"7078","6027 - What Bakkar narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Said al-Jurayri, from Umm Kulthum daughter of Abu Bakr, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, the like."
"7079","6028 - Abu Naamah narrated this hadith from Jabr, differing with Shubah and Hammad therein, saying in place of Umm Kulthum, from al-Qasim. As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, Abu Naamah narrated to us, from Jabr ibn Habib, from al-Qasim, from Aishah: that Abu Bakr sought permission upon Aishah while she was praying, and she kept clapping. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came while she was upon that and said: What prevents you from taking the comprehensive openings of knowledge? She said: What are its comprehensives and openings? He said: You say, then he mentioned this supplication exactly."
"7080","6029 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, al-Aswad ibn Shayban narrated to us, from Abu Nawfal ibn Abi Aqrab, he said: Aishah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) liked comprehensive supplications, and would supplicate with what is between that. Abu Jafar said: We examined comprehensive supplications and preferring them over other supplications; that in our view, and Allah knows best, is upon hastening good deeds fearing what cuts off therefrom of what is not safe upon people. So he commanded comprehensive supplications for that, like what he commanded people in Hajj to hasten to it fearing what cuts them therefrom."
"7081","6030 - As Fahd narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, Abu Israil narrated to us, from Fudayl ibn Amr, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, al-Fadl or Abdullah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that he was likewise, he said: Whoever of you intends Hajj let him hasten, for a stray may stray, a sick may sicken, or a need appear."
"7082","6031 - And as Ahmad ibn Khalid ibn Yazid and Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, they said: al-Husayn ibn Mahdi al-Ubulli narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Sufyan informed us, from Ismail, meaning Abu Israil al-Mulai, from Fudayl ibn Amr, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Hasten Hajj, for none of you knows what befalls him."
"7083","6032 - And as al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Ismail Abu Israil, from al-Fudayl, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, raised to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever intends Hajj let him hasten, for a sick sickens, a stray strays, and a need occurs. Like that is what he intended of comprehensive supplication, fearing that attempting supplication with non-comprehensive speech be cut off by what cuts off the like. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded comprehensive speech so that supplication exits thereby. Like that is what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what he addressed to Juwayriyah his wife in this meaning."
"7084","6033 - As Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Kurayb, from Ibn Abbas: that Juwayriyah bint al-Harith was named Barrah, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) changed her name, disliking it be said: he exited from Barrah, so he named her Juwayriyah. He exited from her after praying Fajr while she was sitting in the mosque, and returned to her after forenoon while she was upon her state. He said: Are you still upon your state? She said: Yes. He said: I said after you four words three times, had they been weighed against all you said they would outweigh them: Glory be to Allah by the number of His creation, the pleasure of Himself, the weight of His Throne, and the ink of His words."
"7085","6034 - And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Qabisah narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman freedman of Al Talhah, from Kurayb, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Juwayriyah was named Barrah. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Fajr, then he mentioned the same."
"7086","6035 - And as Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, Khalid, meaning Ibn al-Harith, narrated to us, from Shubah, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Kurayb, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by Juwayriyah while she was in - he mentioned a place - then passed by her near midday and said to her: Are you still here? Shall I not teach you words... then he mentioned the words in the hadith before this hadith."
"7087","6036 - And as Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Abu Dawud narrated to us, al-Masudi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman freedman of Al Talhah informed me, from Kurayb, from Ibn Abbas: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by Juwayriyah while she was in her prayer place, then came after forenoon and said to her: O Juwayriyah, are you still in your seat? She said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: I said four words repeating thrice better than everything; I said: Glory be to Allah by the number of His creation, glory be to Allah by the pleasure of Himself, glory be to Allah by the ink of His words, glory be to Allah by the weight of His Throne, and praise be to Allah Lord of the worlds likewise."
"7088","6037 - And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq and Ibrahim ibn Sad al-Khawlani narrated to us, they said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, al-Masudi narrated to us, then he mentioned the same with his chain. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith of Juwayriyah of this meaning is also evidence that all speech people need to use seeking nearness to their Lord, they exemplify therein this mentioned meaning; and when so in speech spoken seeking nearness to Him Almighty, deeds done seeking nearness to Him are likewise, and success is from Allah."
"7089","Chapter explaining the problematic reports about Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, in what he did with what others narrated to him from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 6038 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr al-Asadi al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Misar ibn Kidam narrated to us. 6039 - And Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi and Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, they said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, from Misar, from Uthman ibn al-Mughirah, from Ali ibn Rabiah al-Asadi, from Asma ibn al-Hakam: that Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: When I heard something from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Allah Almighty benefited me thereby as He willed; when another narrated to me from him I made him swear, and when he swore I believed him. Abu Bakr narrated to me, and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, spoke truth: that no man commits a sin then performs ablution excellently, then stands praying two rakahs and seeking Allah's forgiveness except He forgives him."
"7090","6040 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, Harun ibn Ishaq al-Hamdani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab al-Qannad narrated to us, from Misar ibn Kidam, then he mentioned the same with his chain. They all did not mention Abu Bakr narrating that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), except its meaning indicates it is from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by Ali's saying in the hadith: When I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) something Allah benefited me thereby as He willed; when another narrated to me from him I made him swear, and when he swore I believed him. And Abu Bakr narrated to me, that is: from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Abu Bakr spoke truth."
"7091","6041 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Abu Amir al-Aqadi and Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, they said: Shubah narrated to us, from Uthman ibn al-Mughirah, from Ali ibn Rabiah, from Asma or Ibn Asma, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: When I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) something Allah benefits me thereby as He wills to benefit me. Abu Bakr narrated to me, and Abu Bakr spoke truth, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No servant commits a sin then performs ablution, then stands praying two rakahs, then seeks Allah's forgiveness for that sin except He forgives him. And he recited: And whoever does evil or wrongs himself then seeks Allah's forgiveness will find Allah Forgiving, Merciful. And: And those who when they commit indecency or wrong themselves remember Allah and seek forgiveness for their sins. He recited the two verses or one of them."
"7092","6042 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir and Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, they said: Shubah narrated to us, from Uthman ibn al-Mughirah, Affan adding: he informed him, he said: I heard Ali ibn Rabiah, Wahb saying: a man of Banu Asad, informing from a man of Banu Fazarah called Asma or Ibn Asma, narrating from Ali, he said: When I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say something, then he mentioned the same, except he did not mention reciting other than His saying: And whoever does evil or wrongs himself, the verse. The wording is Affan's."
"7093","6043 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Abu Asim al-Dahhak ibn Makhlad narrated to us, Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Uthman ibn al-Mughirah al-Thaqafi, from Ali ibn Rabiah, from Asma ibn al-Hakam al-Fazari, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, he said: When I was narrated a hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) I would not believe its narrator until I made him swear; when he swore I believed him. Abu Bakr narrated to me, and Abu Bakr spoke truth, that he, peace be upon him, said: No servant commits a sin then performs ablution and prays two rakahs, then seeks forgiveness except he is forgiven."
"7094","6044 - Al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Waki narrated to us, from Misar and Sufyan, from Uthman, he being Ibn al-Mughirah, from Ali ibn Rabiah Abu al-Mughirah al-Walibi, from Asma ibn al-Hakam al-Fazari, he said: I heard Ali saying: When I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a hadith Allah benefits me thereby as He wills thereof; when another narrated to me from him I made him swear, and when he swore to me I believed him. Abu Bakr narrated to me from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Abu Bakr spoke truth, that he said: No man commits a sin then performs ablution excellently, then prays two rakahs. Misar said: then seeks Allah's forgiveness except He forgives him."
"7095","6045 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, Abu Awanah informed us, from Uthman ibn al-Mughirah al-Thaqafi, from Ibn Rabiah al-Asadi, from Asma ibn al-Hakam al-Fazari, he said: I heard Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: When I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a hadith Allah benefits me thereby as He wills to benefit me thereof; when some of his Companions narrated to me I made him swear and believed him. Abu Bakr narrated to me, and Abu Bakr spoke truth, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: No servant commits a sin then purifies himself excellently, prays two rakahs, and seeks Allah Almighty's forgiveness except He forgives him. Then he recited: And establish prayer at the two ends of the day and approaches of the night; indeed good deeds remove evil deeds."
"7096","6046 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, Qutaybah narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, then he mentioned the same with his chain."
"7097","6047 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us, Qays ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, Uthman ibn al-Mughirah Abu Zurah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Rabiah, from Asma ibn al-Hakam al-Fazari, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, he said: When I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) something Allah benefited me thereby as He willed thereof; when another narrated to me I would not believe him unless he swears, and when he swore I believed him. Abu Bakr narrated to me, and he spoke truth, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No Muslim servant commits a sin then performs ablution and prays two rakahs, then seeks Allah Almighty's forgiveness except He forgives him."
"7098","6048 - Ismail ibn Ishaq al-Kufi narrated to us, Ali ibn Qadim narrated to us, Sharik informed us, from Uthman ibn al-Mughirah Abu Zurah al-Thaqafi, from Ali ibn Rabiah, from Asma ibn al-Hakam, from Ali, he said: When I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) something Allah benefited me thereby as He willed thereof; when another narrated to me from him I made his oath binding. Abu Bakr narrated to me, and Abu Bakr did not lie, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No servant commits a sin then performs ablution, or he said: excellently, then prays two rakahs and seeks Allah Almighty's forgiveness except Allah forgives him. An objector said: In your narrating that Ali would make swear whoever narrated to him from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what he had not heard from him: the narrator is either acceptable or not; if acceptable, no meaning in swearing him; if not acceptable, no point engaging what he narrates, since it does not oblige taking from him. Our answer: Ali's doctrine was in testimony witnessing in established rights of proven justice that judgment is not given thereby except after the beneficiary swears upon its truth in what was testified for him."
"7099","As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Hanash: that Ali made Abdullah ibn al-Rabi swear along with his evidence. So he did in hadith narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of what he had not heard from him before; the justice of his narrator did not suffice him until adding to his justice his oath thereupon, as he was not sufficed with proven-just evidence until adding thereto the beneficiary's oath upon its truth. This was his swearing, may Allah be pleased with him, in what he mentioned. This objector said: How did he leave swearing Abu Bakr in the like, while Abu Bakr, though in highest ranks of justice, does not deserve judgment by his testimony therewith except like what is judged with a just one not of his rank therein? Our answer: He left that for what he recited to him from Allah's Book establishing proof for him upon his truth in what he narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) unheard from him, sufficing him from seeking his oath as he seeks others' oaths thereupon, and success is from Allah."
"7100","Chapter explaining the problematic reports about commanding conveyance from him, praising its doer, what enters this meaning, and what is narrated from Umar detaining prolific narrators after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Jafar said: We mentioned earlier in this book from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what is narrated about that. As for what is narrated from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, after him. Of what Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Abu al-Nadr Hashim ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Shubah, from Sad ibn Ibrahim, from his father: that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, detained Abu Masud, Abu al-Darda and Abu Dharr until he was afflicted, saying: What is this hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? And it is what Musa ibn Abi Musa al-Ansari narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Man ibn Isa, from Malik ibn Anas, from Abdullah ibn Idris, from Shubah, from Sad ibn Ibrahim, from his father: that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to Abu Masud, Abu al-Darda and Abu Dharr: What is this hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)?! He said: And I think he detained them until he was afflicted. An objector said: What is the explanation of what you narrated from Umar, a rightly-guided rightly-led imam, while you know people only learn what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was upon by what his Companions narrate from him, and what of Umar's cutting them therefrom? Our answer: Umar's doctrine was safeguarding what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), though narrators be just; for imams must consider what is testified with them by proven-just ones. Umar was likewise in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what he did not memorize from him. Likewise he did with Abu Musa despite his justice with him, in what he narrated from him from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what was not with him about seeking permission, which we mentioned earlier in this book. Ubayy ibn Kab and other Companions who learned that from him approved, neither objecting nor opposing, evidencing their agreement. Since so, he did in the affairs of those he detained as he did for this meaning, not to cut them from conveying to people what they heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Likewise Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, before him in the like."
"7101","6049 - As Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us: that Malik narrated to him from Ibn Shihab, from Uthman ibn Ishaq ibn Kharashah, from Qabisah ibn Dhuayb that he said: The grandmother came to Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, asking her inheritance. Abu Bakr said: You have nothing in Allah's Book, nor do I know for you anything in the Prophet's Sunnah; return until I ask people. He asked people, and al-Mughirah ibn Shubah said: I attended the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) giving her a sixth. Abu Bakr said: Is there another with you? Muhammad ibn Maslamah al-Ansari stood and said like al-Mughirah said, so Abu Bakr executed it for her. Then the other grandmother came to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, asking her inheritance. He said: You have nothing in Allah's Book Almighty, and the judgment judged was but for other than you. I am not adding to obligations anything, but it is that sixth; if you both gather, it is between you, and whichever of you is alone with it, it is hers. Do you not see Abu Bakr was not sufficed with al-Mughirah's testimony with him of what he testified despite his justice with him, until seeking another's testimony with him thereupon, seeking precaution in what is narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), fearing entering therein what is not of it? Like that is what Umar did in what we mentioned from him. It is possible that those he detained were for exceeding what befits their likes, until he feared they would cut people thereby and distract them from Allah's Book; its reflection and deriving things therefrom by which derivors' rank rises over mere reciters, by His saying: Those who derive it among them would know it. And mentioning others reciting it otherwise by His saying: They know the Book but wishes, that is: but recitation. So he did not praise that from them as he praised derivors upon derivation. Evidence is what Qarazah ibn Kab narrated from him in this meaning."
"7102","As Yunus and Ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, they said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Bayan, from Amir al-Shabi, from Qarazah ibn Kab, he said: We set out intending Iraq, and Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, walked with us to Sirar. He performed ablution washing twice and said: Do you know why I walked with you? They said: Yes, we are Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), you walked with us. He said: You will come to villagers with humming of the Quran like bees' humming; do not repel them with hadiths and distract them. Isolate the Quran, minimize narration from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Go, and I am your partner. When Qarazah arrived they said: Narrate to us. He said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, forbade us. The wording is Yunus's."
"7103","And as al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us. And as Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yunus al-Basri narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, both saying: from Bayan, he said: I heard al-Shabi narrating from Qarazah ibn Kab, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab bid us farewell, performed ablution, then said: Do you know why I bid you farewell? They said: We are the Ansar. He said: You will come to peoples whose tongues quiver with the Quran like bees' quivering; do not repel them with hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I am your partner. I narrated nothing from him, hearing as my companions heard. The wording is al-Kaysani's."
"7104","And as Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Amr ibn al-Haytham al-Qutai narrated to us, al-Masudi narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, from al-Shabi, from Qarazah, he said: Umar bid people farewell and said: Do you know why I came out with you? They said: To honor us. He said: I came out with you only that you minimize narration from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I am your partner in that."
"7105","And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Amir narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Sad ibn Ibrahim, from al-Shabi, from Qarazah ibn Kab, he said: I intended Iraq among some of my people, so Umar said: You will find people humming the Quran like bees' humming; do not divert them. Minimize hadith, and I am your partner."
"7106","As al-Kaysani narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Abu Yusuf narrated to us, Ash'ath ibn Sawwar and Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, from Qarazah ibn Ka'b al-Ansari that he said: I set out with a group of the Ansar toward Kufa, and Umar — may Allah be pleased with him — accompanied us walking, until we reached a place he had named, then he said: Do you know why I walked with you, O company of Ansar? They said: Yes, due to our right. He said: You do have a right, and you are going to a people who have a humming with the Quran like the humming of bees, so narrate little from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I am your partner. So Qarazah said: I will never narrate a hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ever. Abu Ja'far said: This hadith indicates that Umar only intended by what he intended in the earlier hadiths that they should not cut people off from the Book of Allah Almighty by what they narrate to them from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and in that is evidence that he only disliked this meaning from them, not anything else by which they combine preoccupation with the Book of Allah Almighty and hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by which they infer the meanings of the Book of Allah, not that by which they cut them off from the Book of Allah Almighty."
"7107","979 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about love of richness which some people imagine to be richness in wealth, and what was narrated from him about asking Allah Almighty for richness. 6050 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us, Bukayr ibn Mismar narrated to us, he said: I heard Amir ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas — and Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas was among camels and sheep of his — then his son Umar came to him, and when he saw him he said: I seek refuge with Allah from the evil of this rider, and when he reached him he said: O father, are you content to be among your camels and sheep while people in Medina dispute over kingship? So Sa'd struck the chest of Umar with his hand, then said: Be quiet, my son, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Indeed Allah Almighty loves the pious, rich, hidden servant."
"7108","6051 - And Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah who said: Among the supplication of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was: O Allah, I ask You for guidance, piety, chastity and richness. Abu Ja'far said: So an objector said: In the first of these two hadiths is that Allah the Exalted loves the rich among His servants, and in the second of them is his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asking his Lord Almighty for richness, so in that is evidence of preferring the rich over the poor. So our answer to him about that is: The richness mentioned in these two hadiths is not richness in wealth, and how could that be thought of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when Abu Dharr narrated from him what we already mentioned earlier in this book of ours, that he said: I would not love to have Uhud as gold with a night passing over me while I have a dinar of it with me, except a dinar I set aside for a debt, or I say with it among the servants of Allah: thus, and thus, and thus; rather the richness mentioned in these two hadiths — and Allah knows best — is richness of the soul that is detached from wealth which cuts off from obedience to Allah Almighty, preoccupies hearts with other than Him, and cuts them off from Him."
"7109","6052 - As Yunus narrated to us, Anas ibn Iyad informed me, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqamah, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Richness is not from abundance of goods; richness is only richness of the soul. So the praiseworthy richness in the first two hadiths is this richness by which hearts are freed from the world and from concern for it, and turn with it to the opposite of that among what Allah Almighty praises from its people. And how could the opposite of this be thought of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), or that anyone be with Allah in a station better than the station he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is upon, among the states that are the opposite of what this objector thought he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended in the two hadiths we mentioned in this chapter. And success is from Allah."
"7110","980 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about one whom poverty befalls, so he entrusts it to Allah the Exalted, or entrusts it to people. 6053 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Umar al-Wasiti narrated to us. 6054 - And Fahd and Isma'il ibn Ishaq narrated to us, they said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us. 6055 - And Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, al-Firyabi narrated to us, all of them said: Bashir ibn Salman narrated to us, he said: I heard Sayyar Abu al-Hakam mention from Tariq, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud — may Allah be pleased with him — from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever is afflicted by poverty and entrusts it to people, his poverty will not be closed, and if he entrusts it to Allah Almighty, Allah Almighty will soon grant him richness: either deferred richness or immediate richness. Abu Ja'far, may Allah have mercy on him, said: So in the hadith is that the deferred richness which makes free of need of the world, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made it richness in the meaning of richness in wealth, and his saying: or immediate richness, is that which does not distract from remembrance of Allah Almighty, nor from performing His obligations and fulfilling His right therein, and is with that a support for the one given it in his worldly life, so that he is free for those other things. And success is from Allah."
"7111","981 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying to Amr ibn al-As: How excellent is righteous wealth for a righteous man. 6056 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, Musa ibn Ulayy narrated to us, from his father who said: I heard Amr ibn al-As say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to me and said: Take your clothes and your weapon, then come to me, so I did, then I came to him while he was performing ablution, and he looked me up and down, then said: I want to send you at the head of an army, so Allah will keep you safe and grant you spoils, and I will allot to you a good allotment of wealth. I said: O Messenger of Allah, I did not emigrate for wealth, but I emigrated out of desire for Islam and to be with the Messenger of Allah. So he said: O Amr, how excellent is righteous wealth for a righteous man."
"7112","6057 - And Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, from Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth, from Musa ibn Ulayy, then he mentioned the same with its chain. Abu Ja'far said: So an objector said: In this hadith is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioning what he mentioned to Amr to make him desire what he sends him upon, and this is the opposite of what is in the reports. So our answer to him about that is: This hadith is not contrary to what is in the earlier reports, and it is like what is in the hadith of Ibn Mas'ud: or immediate richness, and this is about wealth that is a support for him in what he is engaged in, and he confirmed that by his saying: How excellent is righteous wealth for a righteous man. And wealth is not righteous except when done with it what Allah Almighty commanded to be done with it, and whoever does that with it in truth, having been given ownership of it, is righteous. So it is clear, by praise of Allah and His favor, that there is no contradiction in any of that, nor disagreement."
"7113","982 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what he answered Zayd ibn Arqam and al-Bara ibn Azib al-Ansariyyayn about what they asked him regarding their buying something on credit and something for cash, both of which being among what deferment is not proper in, and his saying to them: Whatever was hand to hand, take it, and whatever was on credit, return it. 6058 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us, Sadaqah ibn Khalid narrated to us, Uthman ibn al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: I heard Sulayman ibn Abi Muslim al-Ahwal say: I asked Abu al-Minhal about currency exchange, so he said: I and a partner of mine bought something hand to hand and something on credit, and we mentioned that to al-Bara ibn Azib who said: I and my partner Zayd ibn Arqam did it, and we mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: Whatever was hand to hand, take it, and whatever was on credit, return it. This hadith is used as proof in a fiqh question its people dispute: whether a single transaction combining what may be sold alone and what may not be sold alone, the part that may be sold alone is valid and the part that may not is void, or both are void together. In this hadith is that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not ask his two questioners about those two things they asked him about — of which sale of one alone is permissible and of the other alone is not — whether they bought them in one transaction or in two different transactions. So we understood thereby that the ruling in them was one; for had they differed he would have asked them about the reality of their purchase, whether it required purchase in one transaction or in two transactions, then answered them with what was obligatory upon what he learned from them. And since he did not ask them about that, we understood the ruling is the same in those two meanings, and that purchase is valid in what was hand to hand and void in what was on credit, and the ruling of each of those two things is its own ruling, not the ruling of the other thing joined with it in the transaction combining them. Among those who held this view: Abu Hanifah and his companions, and Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim in what he answered Asad about the view of Malik therein. Others opposed them, among them al-Shafi'i, who voided the sale in both things due to its voidness in one of them. Then we sought this hadith from other than the narration of Sulayman ibn Abi Muslim from Abu al-Minhal, whether another who narrated it from him opposed him or not."
"7114","6059 - So we found Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hasan ibn al-Haytham al-Massisi narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj said: Amr ibn Dinar and Amir ibn Mus'ab informed me that they heard Abu al-Minhal say: I asked al-Bara ibn Azib and Zayd ibn Arqam, and they said: We were two traders in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and we asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about currency exchange, so he said: If it is hand to hand there is no harm, and if it is on credit it is not proper. So in this hadith from the two narrations of Amr ibn Dinar and Amir ibn Mus'ab from Abu al-Minhal about exchange, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answered the one who asked him about cash that it is permissible, and about credit that it is not proper. The first hadith contains this meaning plus what is not in this hadith, so it is worthier than it, and we understood thereby that Amr and Amir asked Abu al-Minhal about one thing among what he has along with another thing combined in one hadith, so he answered them with the answer to what they asked him and withheld the rest of what he has in that hadith from al-Bara and Zayd ibn Arqam from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So the hadith of Sulayman ibn Abi Muslim is worthier than theirs."
"7115","6060 - And al-Qasim ibn Abdullah ibn Mahdi narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, Amr ibn Dinar narrated to us that he heard Abu al-Minhal say: A partner of mine sold dirhams for dirhams with excess between them, so I said: This is not proper. He said: I sold them in the market and no one objected to me. So I came to al-Bara and asked him, and he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came while our trade was like this, and he said: Whatever was hand to hand there is no harm, and whatever was on credit there is no good in it. And go to Zayd ibn Arqam, for he was greater in trade than me. So I came to him and mentioned that to him, and he said: Al-Bara spoke the truth. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is abridgment of what is in the hadith of Sulayman, so the hadith of Sulayman from Abu al-Minhal is worthier than it. Then we considered whether another besides those we mentioned narrated it from Abu al-Minhal."
"7116","6061 - So we found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, Habib ibn Abi Thabit narrated to us, Abu al-Minhal narrated to me, he said: I asked al-Bara ibn Azib and Zayd ibn Arqam about currency exchange, and they both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us gold for silver on credit."
"7117","6062 - And we found Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, Habib ibn Abi Thabit informed us, he said: I heard Abu al-Minhal say: I asked al-Bara about exchange, and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade selling silver for gold on credit. So in this hadith also is a portion of what is in the hadith of Sulayman, and it is established that the hadith of Sulayman from Abu al-Minhal is worthier than the hadiths of the others from Abu al-Minhal, due to his preserving what they fell short of. Then we sought that by reasoning to determine how it is therein. We saw that a sale may fall upon a share of a house to which preemption is due for the partner in the house, and upon other goods like a slave, or a slave-girl, or other goods, so preemption is due in that share for its portion of the price, not due in the rest in which there is no preemption, then the rest remains sold for its portion of the price, and that is among what it is not permissible to initiate sale upon likewise. So we understood thereby that each of the two goods combined by the transaction is charged with its own ruling, not the ruling of its companion. Likewise we saw them agree about two goods sold in one transaction for one price — and both goods being among what is agreed that if they perish in the seller's hand before the buyer takes possession of any of the sold goods, the sale is rescinded over them — like two heaps, one wheat and the other barley, sold together with a guarantee stipulated in each of them, and one of them is lost in its seller's hand before its buyer takes it from him, that it is lost for its portion of the price and the other remains sold for its portion of the price, and that is among what it is not permissible to initiate sale upon likewise alone without its companion that was included with it therein. And in that is evidence for what Abu Hanifah and his companions used to say about that."
"7118","983 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in the authentic reports over which scholars differed about a woman gifting herself to a man by way of marriage: is that a marriage or not, and the reports narrated therein. 6063 - Al-Husayn ibn Nasr ibn al-Mubarak al-Baghdadi and al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb ibn Sa'd al-Azdi narrated to us, they said: Yusuf ibn Adi al-Kufi narrated to us, Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father who said: It was said that Khawlah bint Hakim gifted herself to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and she was among the first emigrant women. Aishah — may Allah be pleased with her — said: When I mentioned it I would say: I am shy of a woman who gifts herself to a man without dowry, and she was among the most jealous people, and about her was revealed this verse: You defer whom you will of them and take to yourself whom you will."
"7119","6064 - And Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, Hannad ibn al-Sari narrated to us, Abdah ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Hisham, from his father, from Aishah that she used to say: Is a woman not shy to gift herself to a man, until Allah the Exalted revealed: You defer whom you will of them — the three verses. I said: Indeed your Lord hastens for you in your desire."
"7120","6065 - And Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Abu Usamah informed us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah who said: I was jealous of those who gifted themselves to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I would say: Does a woman gift herself to a man? So Allah the Exalted revealed His saying: You defer whom you will of them and take to yourself whom you will, and whomever you seek of those you set aside, there is no blame upon you. I said: By Allah, I see your Lord only hastening for you in your desire. This is a fiqh question its people dispute. One group says: If a woman gifts herself to a man by giving him ownership of her private part, and he accepts that from her in the presence of witnesses, that is a marriage; if he named a dowry it is hers, if not she has the dowry of her peers, and if he divorces her before consummation she has mut'ah upon him. Among those who said that: Abu Hanifah, Sufyan ibn Sa'id al-Thawri, and the companions of Abu Hanifah. Another group says: If a man gifts his minor daughter to a man to guard her and care for her by way of looking after her, that is permissible, and if he gifted her with a dowry mentioned, that is a marriage provided he intended marriage by the gift; among those who said that was Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim upon the meanings of the saying of Malik. Another group says: Marriage is not contracted by a gift; among those who said that was al-Shafi'i. Abu Ja'far said: We examined their disagreement and found Allah the Exalted said in His Book: And a believing woman if she gifts herself to the Prophet if the Prophet wishes to marry her, so Allah Almighty made that gift a permissible marriage without dowry, then followed it saying: Exclusively for you apart from the believers. So it may be that what He made exclusive and gave him — the gift — is a marriage without dowry due upon him therein, while the like of it for others is a marriage requiring dowry. If so, what we mentioned from al-Shafi'i is confirmed. In the verse we recited: If the Prophet wishes to marry her — i.e., by the gift she made to him — is evidence that her gift to him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was a marriage for him. Specification is only by a written verse, a transmitted sunnah, or consensus of scholars; if absent, it remains general except what is agreed as specific. We considered al-Shafi'i's saying that Allah named marriage in His Book by two names — nikah and tazwij — so marriage is only by them. His opponents answered: They found divorce mentioned by Allah as talaq, firaq and sarah, yet scholars agree divorce is not restricted to these three names, but included khul', khaliyyah, bariyyah, ba'in and haram. Since divorce was not made specific by the three names Allah mentioned, and they joined to them others of like meaning, likewise marriage is not restricted by the two names Allah mentioned, but what has their meaning joins them. Since a husband's gift of the wife's private part, like divorce, stands in place of divorce when intended, likewise her gifting her private part to him is like the marriage he contracts upon it, and the gift from each to the other is in the ruling of ownership, as is the gift from the other to him. And it was narrated from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib in this chapter."
"7121","What Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Buraydah, freedman of al-Aswad, narrated to me, he said: I asked Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib about a man given glad tidings of a slave-girl, and a man among the people said to him: Gift her to me, so he gifted her to him. Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib said: Gift was not lawful for anyone after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); had he given her even a whip as dowry, she would have been lawful to him. That indicates that the gift exclusive to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was, in Sa'id's view, the gift with no dowry due therein, while others besides him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) marry by gift with a dowry due as in a marriage contracted without dowry mentioned. In Aishah's hadith is a meaning to reflect upon: her saying, I am shy of a woman who gifts herself to a man without dowry — she did not mean by that man the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), but generalized men, if it came out as indefinite, and the indefinite includes all people. So her saying indicates she is shy for a woman who gifts herself to a man without dowry, entering all people therein, showing that a woman who gifts herself to a man becomes a wife thereby, and that exclusivity was only in her being a wife to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) without dowry, so it is not a marriage for others as it was for the Prophet without dowry, but for others with a dowry due with it. And success is from Allah."
"7122","984 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about those women who gifted themselves to him: was there any acceptance and keeping of any of them as wife or not? 6066 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr al-Hamdani narrated to us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Anbasah ibn al-Azhar narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had no woman who gifted herself. Abu Ja'far said: This Anbasah is Abu Yahya al-Nasa'i, called the judge of Jurjan, as al-Bukhari mentioned. An objector said: You narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the report of the woman seeking refuge, narrated by Abu Humayd al-Sa'idi, which you mentioned earlier in your book, that when al-Jawniyyah was brought, he said to her: Gift yourself to me. She said: Does a queen gift herself to a commoner? He reached his hand to her, she said: I seek refuge with Allah from you. He said: You have sought refuge with a Refuge, then he went out and said: O Abu Usayd, clothe her with two garments of provision and join her to her family. Our answer is: His entering upon that woman was only while she was already his wife before that, and upon that basis Abu Usayd brought her. His saying after that, Gift yourself to me, meant: Enable me of yourself, not initiating a marriage he contracts upon herself. How could it be thought of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — while of our law is that a man not be alone with a woman who is not his mahram? What confirms what we said is that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left her upon divorcing her and separating from her, and that is only after his prior marrying her. And success is from Allah."
"7123","985 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: When you hear from me a hadith your hearts recognize and your hair and skin soften to, and you see it close to you, then I am most worthy of it; and when you hear from me a hadith your hearts deny, and your hair and skin flee from it, and you see it rejected, then I am furthest from it. 6067 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Rabi'ah ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, from Abd al-Malik ibn Sa'id ibn Suwayd al-Ansari, from Abu Humayd and Abu Usayd that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you hear a hadith from me your hearts recognize and your hair and skin soften to, and you see it close to you, then I am most worthy of it; and when you hear a hadith from me your hearts deny and your hair and skin flee from it, and you see it rejected, then I am furthest from it. Thus Rabi'ah narrated this hadith from Abd al-Malik ibn Sa'id. And Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj narrated it from this Abd al-Malik ibn Sa'id, differing in its chain and text."
"7124","As Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to us, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj: that Abd al-Malik ibn Sa'id narrated to him from Abbas ibn Sahl that Ubayy ibn Ka'b was in a gathering, and they began narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the lenient and the strict, while Ubayy ibn Ka'b was silent, until he said: O these people, whatever hadith reaches you from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that hearts recognize and skin softens to and you hope for good therein, then believe in the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) says only good. Abu Ja'far said: We examined this hadith and found Allah Almighty said in His Book: The believers are only those who, when Allah is mentioned, their hearts tremble, and when His verses are recited to them, they increase them in faith. And He said: Allah sent down the best of speech, a Book consistent, oft-repeated, at which shiver the skins of those who fear their Lord, then their skins and hearts soften to remembrance of Allah. And He said about the companions of the Negus: And when they hear what was sent down to the Messenger, you see their eyes overflow with tears from what they recognized of truth, saying: Our Lord, we believed. So Allah informed about the people of faith in these states upon hearing what was sent down to their Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and what they narrate in his presence of what is in reality as they narrate from him is of that kind, for all of it is from Allah Almighty, the proof of the truth of what he narrates to them from Him standing with them, so they must accept his saying therein, distinguishing between it and other than it we mentioned before."
"7125","986 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: When you are narrated from me a hadith you recognize and do not deny, then believe in it whether I said it or not, for I say what is recognized and not denied; and when you are narrated from me a hadith you deny and do not recognize, then belie it, for I do not say what is denied. 6068 - Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Hulwani narrated to us, Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us, Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you are narrated from me a hadith you recognize and do not deny, then believe in it, whether I said it or not, for I say what you recognize and do not deny; and when you are narrated from me a hadith you deny and do not recognize, then belie it, for I do not say what you deny, I say what you recognize. This hadith of Ibn Abi Dhi'b revolves upon Yahya ibn Adam, and it is said he heard it at Kufa when it was carried to him. We examined this hadith to determine its meaning, Allah willing. The aspect of his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), you recognize, may be upon recognition by their natural disposition, as they recognize by their hearts things that harm and benefit them, and know by their hearts their recurrence, and that some contradict others, a natural knowledge not acquired. They knew their Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was given by Allah a law, the greatest and best of laws, so its bearers whom they taught knew thereby that the good things agreeable to his character and law include what they are narrated thereof, and since so, they must accept and believe it from him even if he did not say it to them with his tongue, for it is of what proof stands upon them for him; and when they hear a hadith from him and deny it from that angle, they must hold back from it and avoid accepting it."
"7126","987 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) over which scholars differed: is he, after raising his head from the last prostration of the rak'ah that is the even part of his prayer, to sit a sitting then stand for the second, or stand to the second without sitting? 6069 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Abu al-Rabi' al-Zahrani narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, Ayyub narrated to us, from Abu Qilabah, from Malik ibn al-Huwayrith that he used to say to his companions: Shall I show you how the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was, though not at prayer time. So he stood and made standing firm, then bowed and made bowing firm, then raised his head and stood upright a while, then prostrated, then raised his head and settled in sitting, then waited a while then prostrated. Abu Qilabah said: He prayed like the prayer of this shaykh of ours — meaning Amr ibn Salamah — prostrating a while. He said: I saw Amr ibn Salamah do something I do not see you do; when he raised his head from the first and second prostration in which there is no sitting, he sat upright then stood."
"7127","6070 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, Khalid — meaning al-Hadhdha — informed us, from Abu Qilabah: Malik ibn al-Huwayrith informed us that he saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when in an odd part of his prayer, not rise until sitting upright. This is a fiqh question its people dispute. One group acts upon this hadith and commands the praying one with this sitting; among them was al-Shafi'i. Others among Hijazi and Kufan jurists do not know this sitting at all, nor command the praying one with it. We examined whether anything contradicting it was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) or not."
"7128","6071 - So we found Ali ibn Sa'id ibn Bashir al-Razi narrated to us, he said: Abu Hammam al-Walid ibn Shuja' ibn al-Walid al-Sakuni narrated to us, my father narrated to us. 6072 - And we found Nasr ibn Ammar al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ishkab narrated to us, Shuja' narrated to us, then they agreed, saying: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn al-Hurr narrated to us, Isa ibn Abdullah ibn Malik narrated to me, from Ayyash — or Abbas ibn Sahl al-Sa'idi — who was in a gathering with his father and the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), among them Abu Hurayrah, Abu Usayd, Abu Humayd al-Sa'idi and the Ansar, that they recalled prayer, so Abu Humayd said: I am most knowledgeable of you of the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); I followed that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). They said: Show us. So he stood praying while they looked, he said takbir and raised his hands at the first takbir, then he mentioned a long hadith therein: that when he raised his head from the second prostration of the first rak'ah, he stood and did not sit with legs folded. In the hadith is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) leaving sitting after raising his head from the last prostration of the first rak'ah. This hadith was narrated by a group mentioned therein, some named, some unnamed. And Rifa'ah ibn Rafi' narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what also indicates that."
"7129","6073 - As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Abi Kathir — meaning Isma'il ibn Ja'far — narrated to us. 6074 - And as Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated to us, then they agreed, saying: from Yahya ibn Ali ibn Yahya ibn Khallad ibn Rafi' al-Zuraqi, from his father, from his grandfather, from Rifa'ah ibn Rafi': that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while sitting in the mosque one day — Rifa'ah said: and we were with him — a man like a Bedouin entered and prayed, making his prayer brief, then left and greeted the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And upon you, go back and pray, for you did not pray. He did that two or three times, then the man said at the end: Show me and teach me, for I am but a human who errs and is right. He said: Yes; when you stand for prayer, perform ablution as Allah Almighty commanded you, then testify, then say takbir; if you have Quran, recite it, otherwise praise Allah, magnify Him and declare His oneness, then bow until tranquil bowing, then rise and stand upright, then prostrate and be steady prostrating, then sit until tranquil sitting, then prostrate and be steady prostrating, then stand. If you do that, your prayer is complete. In this is his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanding the man after finishing this prostration to stand with no sitting commanded before it. The hadith of this Isma'il from Yahya ibn Ali opposed the hadith of Ibn Ajlan narrated by Hajjaj ibn Rushdin from Haywah from Ibn Ajlan from Ali ibn Yahya ibn Khallad from his father from his uncle who said: We were sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned this hadith. Some people impugned this hadith, arguing."
"7130","6075 - With what Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'ah and al-Layth informed us, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from one who informed him, from Ali ibn Yahya ibn Khallad, from his father, from his uncle Rifa'ah ibn Rafi', then he mentioned this hadith. So what this man who claimed the corruption of this hadith mentioned was as he mentioned, due to the entry of this unknown man — who claimed the corruption of this hadith — between Ibn Ajlan and Ali ibn Yahya ibn Khallad. The hadith of Isma'il is worthier than it, for the hadith of Isma'il is from Yahya ibn Ali ibn Yahya, who is the son of the man between whom and Ibn Ajlan the unnamed man entered in this hadith. The hadith of Malik ibn al-Huwayrith may be that what is mentioned therein of what he saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do — of the sitting mentioned from him — was due to an ailment he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had at that time, so he did what he did because of that ailment, not because it is of the sunnah of his prayer. The proof is that Malik ibn al-Huwayrith only stayed with him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) days then returned to his family."
"7131","6076 - As al-Muzani narrated to us, al-Shafi'i narrated to us, al-Thaqafi narrated to us, from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani who said: Abu Qilabah said: Malik ibn al-Huwayrith narrated to us, he said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among people, and we were young men close in age, and we stayed with him twenty nights. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was merciful and gentle, and when he thought we desired our families and longed, he asked us about those we left behind, so we informed him, and he said: Return to your families and stay among them, teach them and command them, and he mentioned things I remember or do not remember. Among those who narrated the hadith we mentioned from the hadith of Abbas ibn Sahl from Abu Humayd al-Sa'idi: that he followed the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and mentioned that he used to rise from the first rak'ah without sitting with legs folded, and his companions confirmed him in that and agreed with him, contrary to what was narrated of his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) teaching the Bedouin the prayer and commanding him to stand after raising his head from the second prostration of the first rak'ah. Then we returned to what reasoning requires: we saw that when a man wants to bow he says takbir and falls bowing, when he raises his head from bowing he says: Allah hears him who praises Him, when he falls to prostration from standing he says: Allah is Greatest, when he raises his head from prostration he says: Allah is Greatest, when he returns to prostration he does likewise, and when he raises his head there is from after raising his head until standing upright but one takbirah. That indicates there is no sitting between his prostration and standing; for had there been a sitting between them he would need to say takbir when rising from sitting, as he says takbir when rising from sitting in his prayer when standing to the rak'ah after that sitting. When the sitting takbirah is negated, it is established there is no sitting between rising and standing. This is the analogy in this chapter, along with the narrated reports supporting it and the number of their narrators which those who narrated the contrary lack. And success is from Allah."
"7132","988 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the mu'adhdhin's saying in the dawn adhan: Prayer is better than sleep — is that in what he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught Abu Mahdhurah, or is it of the sunnah of adhan, or not of its sunnah? 6077 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj: Uthman ibn al-Sa'ib informed us, from Umm Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Mahdhurah, from Abu Mahdhurah: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught him at the beginning of dawn: Prayer is better than sleep."
"7133","6078 - And Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, al-Haytham ibn Khalid ibn Yazid narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay' who said: I heard Abu Mahdhurah say: I was a young boy, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Say: Prayer is better than sleep."
"7134","6079 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Qays ibn Hafs al-Darimi narrated to us, al-Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Abu al-Jarrah al-Mahri narrated to me, from al-Nu'man ibn Rashid, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Mahdhurah, from Abdullah ibn Muhayriz, from Abu Mahdhurah who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) conquered Mecca and wanted to march to Hunayn, he camped at al-Batha. He said: We came and gave adhan. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent cavalry that surrounded us, and we were taken to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Give adhan, so I gave adhan, and I heard the mountain echo my voice. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Indeed Allah Almighty has willed good for you, so be with Attab ibn Asid and give adhan for him; when you reach in adhan: Come to prayer, come to success, say: Prayer is better than sleep, Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, there is no god but Allah. This hadith is among the best narrated in this chapter, and Abu al-Jarrah who narrated it is named al-Nu'man ibn Abi Shaybah."
"7135","6080 - And Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, Suwayd ibn Nasr narrated to us, Abdullah — meaning Ibn al-Mubarak — informed us, from Sufyan, from Abu Ja'far, from Abu Salman, from Abu Mahdhurah who said: I used to give adhan for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I would say in the first dawn adhan: Come to prayer, come to prayer, come to success, come to success, prayer is better than sleep, prayer is better than sleep, Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, there is no god but Allah."
"7136","6081 - And Ahmad narrated to us, Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, Yahya and Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, they said: Sufyan narrated to us with this chain the like of it. Abd al-Rahman said: And he is not Abu Ja'far al-Farra. So in what we mentioned from Abu Mahdhurah is the confirmation of Prayer is better than sleep in the adhan for the people."
"7137","6082 - And Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar who said: In the first adhan after success was: Prayer is better than sleep."
"7138","6083 - And Ali also narrated to us, Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us. 6084 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Amr ibn Awn narrated to us, they said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, from Muhammad, from Anas who said: Tathwib was only in the morning prayer: when the mu'adhdhin said: Come to success, he said: Prayer is better than sleep — twice."
"7139","6085 - And Harun ibn Kamil narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, from Yunus ibn Yazid, from Ibn Shihab who said: Hafs ibn Umar ibn Sa'd the mu'adhdhin informed me: that Sa'd used to give adhan in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for the people of Quba, until Umar ibn al-Khattab — may Allah be pleased with him — moved him in his caliphate, so he gave adhan for him in Medina in the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Hafs claimed he heard from his family that Bilal came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to announce the dawn prayer after having given adhan, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asleep, so Bilal called at the top of his voice: Prayer is better than sleep, prayer is better than sleep, and it was confirmed in the dawn adhan, and the matter remained so. The authentication of these reports may be that what was from Bilal preceded what is in the hadiths of Abu Mahdhurah, so it became of the sunnah of adhan, then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught Abu Mahdhurah the adhan, including his doing it therein, then confirmed by what we mentioned from Ibn Umar from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). This is a fiqh question its people dispute: one group upon what is in these reports — the jurists of Hijaz and Iraq; another group contrary, leaving Prayer is better than sleep. Al-Shafi'i left it in one of his views and commanded it in another; his proof for leaving it was that it is not in what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught Abu Mahdhurah, though we narrated that in this chapter from Abu Mahdhurah, except we did not find it in al-Shafi'i's narration from the companions of Ibn Jurayj. So by what we said is established the obligation of using Prayer is better than sleep as in these reports in the dawn adhan. And success is from Allah."
"7140","989 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what is said about rain: Prayer in the dwellings. Abu Ja'far said: About this are many reports whose fame dispenses with mentioning them in this book, except we wanted to know the places the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded it be done in. 6086 - So we found Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Abd al-Hamid, companion of al-Ziyadi, narrated to me, Abdullah ibn al-Harith narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abbas gave a sermon on a Friday; when the mu'adhdhin gave adhan and reached Come to success, he said: Announce: Prayer in the dwellings. Some looked at others, so he said: One better than me did it — meaning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — and I disliked to cause you hardship. This Abd al-Hamid is an eminent man; Shu'bah and Hammad ibn Zayd narrated from him. This is a sunnah we learned by this hadith that must be included among reports when needed, and it was narrated from Ibn Umar what also indicates that."
"7141","6087 - As Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us, from Nafi': that Ibn Umar experienced severe cold while traveling, so he commanded the mu'adhdhin to give adhan with him that they pray in their dwellings, for I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) command that when like this. And success is from Allah."
"7142","990 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the liability period of slaves. 6088 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: al-Mu'alla ibn Mansur al-Razi narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah informed us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Uqbah ibn Amir who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made the liability period of slaves three days."
"7143","6089 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, al-Mu'alla narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, from Uqbah ibn Amir who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no liability after four."
"7144","6090 - And Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh al-Basri narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Uqbah ibn Amir that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The liability period of slaves is three days."
"7145","6091 - And Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Uqbah ibn Amir: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no liability after four."
"7146","6092 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, from Sa'id, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Samurah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The liability period of slaves is three. This hadith came with this confusion: sometimes said from al-Hasan from Uqbah, sometimes from al-Hasan from Samurah from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). As for who said from Uqbah, that is far-fetched, for all hadith scholars do not affirm al-Hasan meeting Uqbah. As for who said from al-Hasan from Samurah, al-Hasan meeting Samurah and taking from him is imagined therein — rather it is authentic and established. As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Quraysh ibn Anas narrated to us, from Habib ibn al-Shahid who said: Muhammad ibn Sirin said to me: Ask al-Hasan from whom he heard his hadith on aqeeqah. So I asked him, and he said: I heard it from Samurah. When we examined this hadith we found it mentions liability, and liability in Arab speech is taken from covenant — matters required of the one charged with fulfilling them — such as Allah's saying: And We had covenanted to Adam, and: Did I not covenant to you, O children of Adam, and: And the covenant of Allah is questioned, and like examples in the Quran. So the best of what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter is to place it upon the stipulated contract in sales of stipulated options therein: is its period three days or more, as Abu Hanifah, Zufar and al-Shafi'i say. As for what the Medinans say about the liability of slaves involving death of the sold or what appears on his body in three days or six, we found no meaning therein that strengthens in our hearts. Ata and Tawus used to deny that and see it as nothing."
"7147","As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, al-Mu'alla narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj: Ibn Tawus informed me, from his father, that he did not see liability as anything, neither three nor more."
"7148","And as Abu Umayyah narrated to us, al-Mu'alla narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Ata said: There was no liability in the past; I said: What about three days? He said: Nothing."
"7149","And as Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Shafi'i narrated to us, al-Harith ibn Umayr narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh who said: The liability of a Muslim is that there be no disease, no deceit, no defect. In this saying of Shurayh is also denial of the liability we mentioned, agreeing with Ata and Tawus. Since we found in the mentioned liability nothing but what we stated, we sought its ruling by reasoning: we saw that when a man sells a slave or slave-girl and delivers to another, and one wants the withholder to withhold its price, he has no right to that; for had anything required by sale remained — option or otherwise — he could withhold until the sale is confirmed between them. Their consensus that he may not withhold indicates no right remains upon him by the sale they contracted, neither liability nor otherwise. And Allah is the Granter of success."
"7150","991 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from Fadalah ibn Ubayd about the necklace of gold and beads sold for gold, and what some narrated thereof raised to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that it not be sold until separated, and what some narrated stopped at Fadalah. 6093 - al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us. 6094 - And Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, Qutaybah ibn Sa'id narrated to us, then they agreed, each saying: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, Abu Shuja' Sa'id ibn Yazid al-Himyari narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Abi Imran, from Hanash — and it is established in Ahmad's hadith — from Fadalah ibn Ubayd, Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: I bought on the day of Khaybar a necklace with gold and beads for twelve dinars, then I separated it, and the gold was more than twelve dinars, so I mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: It is not to be sold until separated. In this hadith is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding the necklace with beads and gold to be sold for gold until separated. If so, therein is proof that if its measure is known he is sufficed thereby from separating it. In the hadith is what indicates permissibility of selling it before separation, for it was of spoils then sold after, and spoils are divided among their people upon what sales are valid upon."
"7151","6095 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Amr ibn Awn al-Wasiti narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from Layth ibn Sa'd, from Khalid ibn Abi Imran — without mentioning Abu Shuja' between them — from Hanash al-San'ani, from Fadalah ibn Ubayd who said: I acquired on the day of Khaybar a necklace with gold and beads, and wanted to sell it, so I came to the Prophet — upon him be peace — and mentioned that to him, and he said: Separate part from part, then sell as you wish. The hadith of al-Layth with which we began is the sound one in this chapter of his hadith, for so it is with his townspeople from him."
"7152","6096 - And Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Yazid who said: I heard Khalid ibn Abi Imran narrate from Hanash, from Fadalah who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought on the day of Khaybar a necklace with beads overlaid with gold, which a man bought for seven or nine, so he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that, and he said: No, until you distinguish between them. He said: I only wanted the stones. He said: No, until you distinguish between them. So he rejected it. In this hadith is evidence of the prior division between the man who sold it and the other spoil people, showing it may be divided thus without separation. What is permissible in spoil is permissible in sale. The Prophet's saying, No until you distinguish, about the gold and jewels therein, is when he knew of the excess in its gold over what it was sold for in al-Layth's hadith."
"7153","6097 - And Yunus narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, Qurrah ibn Abd al-Rahman and Amr ibn al-Harith informed me that Amir ibn Yahya al-Ma'afiri informed them, from Hanash who said: We were with Fadalah ibn Ubayd in a raid, and a necklace with gold, silver and jewels fell to me and my companions, and I wanted to buy it, so I asked Fadalah, who said: Remove its gold, put it in one pan and put gold in the other pan, then take only like for like, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him take only like for like. What is in this hadith of separating the necklace in the first hadith is mentioned here from Fadalah, not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), apart from what he mentioned from the Prophet of a different meaning."
"7154","6098 - And Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Abu Hani' narrated to me that he heard Ulayy ibn Rabah al-Lakhmi say: I heard Fadalah ibn Ubayd al-Ansari say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while at Khaybar, was brought a necklace with gold and beads, of spoils being sold, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered the gold in the necklace be removed alone, then the Messenger of Allah said: Gold for gold, weight for weight."
"7155","6099 - And Bakr ibn Idris al-Azdi narrated to us, Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated to us, from Abu Hani', and he mentioned with its chain the like of it. What is in this hadith is not of the hadiths we mentioned from Hanash from Fadalah in this chapter at all; for what is in Hanash's hadiths of the Messenger of Allah's command (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is that it not be sold until separated, and in some is that he rejected that sale, while what is in Ulayy ibn Rabah's hadith is the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) separating it without a prior sale, and informing people that gold for gold is weight for weight. Since the confusion we mentioned occurred in this hadith, the meaning intended thereby is what scholars dispute about selling gold and other with it in one transaction for gold. One group says: If the gold paid for them is more than the gold bought with it, the remainder is payment for what was sold with the gold in that transaction; if the sold gold's weight is unknown, or equals or is less than the gold paid for both things, the sale is corrupt; among them Abu Hanifah and his companions. Another group says: That sale is impermissible at all, for the gold paid is divided over their values, so the sold gold is sold for its share of the divided price, voiding the sale; among them al-Shafi'i. The latter make gold and the thing sold with it like two non-gold goods sold for gold in one transaction, each sold for its share of the divided price. The others hold division over values is not used here, but only in other than gold sold for gold, silver for silver, measured things sold for their kinds, and weighed things sold for their likes; therein likes are used, not values. They argued with what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) indicating that."
"7156","6100 - As Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Malik informed me, that Humayd ibn Qays narrated to him from Mujahid al-Makki: that a goldsmith asked Abdullah ibn Umar: I fashion then sell the thing for more than its weight, taking excess for my work. Abdullah ibn Umar forbade him that, until he reached his mount or the mosque door, then Abdullah ibn Umar said to him: Dinar for dinar, dirham for dirham, no excess between them; this is the covenant of our Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and our covenant to you."
"7157","6101 - And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Dirham for dirham, no increase, dinar for dinar; do not make some excel others, and do not sell absent for present."
"7158","6102 - And as Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, men of knowledge informed me, among them Malik ibn Anas, that Nafi', freedman of Ibn Umar, narrated to them, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it, without mentioning Ibn Umar between him and Abu Sa'id."
"7159","6103 - And as Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: I heard Malik say: Musa ibn Abi Tamim narrated to me, from Sa'id ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Dinar for dinar, dirham for dirham, no excess between them."
"7160","6104 - And as Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, Qatadah narrated to us, from Abu al-Khalil, from Muslim, from Abu al-Ash'ath al-San'ani: that he attended Ubadah's sermon, that he narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Gold for gold weight for weight, silver for silver weight for weight, wheat for wheat measure for measure, barley for barley measure for measure; there is no harm in selling barley for dates, the dates being more, hand to hand; dates for dates, salt for salt; whoever increases or seeks increase has committed usury."
"7161","6105 - And as Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, al-Husayn ibn Hafs al-Isbahani narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Ash'ath, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Gold for gold weight for weight, silver for silver weight for weight, wheat for wheat like for like, barley for barley like for like, salt for salt like for like; whoever increases or takes increase has committed usury."
"7162","6106 - And as Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid informed us, from Hakim ibn Jabir, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Gold for gold like for like, pan for pan, silver for silver like for like, pan for pan, wheat for wheat like for like hand to hand, until he mentioned salt."
"7163","6107 - And as Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Ya'qub ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us that Suhayl ibn Abi Salih informed him, from his father, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not sell gold for gold nor silver for silver except weight for weight, like for like, equal for equal."
"7164","6108 - And as Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Ibn Abi Dhi'b informed me, from al-Harith ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Dinar for dinar, dirham for dirham, a sa' of dates for a sa' of dates, a sa' of wheat for a sa' of wheat, a sa' of barley for a sa' of barley, no excess between anything thereof."
"7165","6109 - And as Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, al-Mu'alla ibn Mansur narrated to us, Abbad — meaning Ibn al-Awwam — and Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar informed us, from Yahya ibn Abi Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us to sell silver for silver and gold for gold except like for like, and commanded us to sell gold for silver and silver for gold as we wish. In this chapter are many reports of which we sufficed with what we brought. In these reports is the Messenger of Allah's permitting sale of gold for gold like for like, though gold may differ — one higher than the other — sold for two equal dinars; the apparent reports permit that, for had it differed by the two dinars mentioned, he would have clarified so people know he intended other than them. No one may come to what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made general with one ruling and apply differentiated rulings and examples. Likewise dates: he permitted some for some like for like hand to hand, without distinguishing between two differing dates sold for equal dates. We found dates themselves differ until a high date and a deficient date exist; when dates are sold for like dates this exists therein, yet purchase is not prevented due to internal disparity or value difference. Since that is not considered by dividing price upon it when sold for its kind, and sale therein is permissible, that indicates a distinction between weighed things and measured things sold for their likes: values are not used therein, but equality in measure or weight is used, so sale is permitted, and voided if contrary. And it was narrated from Abdullah ibn Abbas what indicates this meaning."
"7166","As Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Uthman ibn Hakim narrated to us, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas who said: Selling dates on palm-tops when in exchange there are dirhams or dinars is no harm. The point is he made the dates sold on tops sold for their like of the dates bought with them; had he applied dividing dates over values he would not have permitted that sale. His permitting indicates division over values was not used therein, as used in selling two goods of other kinds. Since so, likewise two differing golds sold for equal gold: division of price over values is not considered, but equality in weight only. An objector said: What you mentioned from Abdullah ibn Abbas is impossible, for Ibn Abbas's view was permitting silver for silver with excess in one over the other hand to hand, narrated from Usamah ibn Zayd from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein."
"7167","6110 - So he mentioned what Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Abbas, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Usury is only in deferment."
"7168","6111 - And what Fahd narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, Sufyan informed us, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid, from Ibn Abbas, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"7169","6112 - And what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Amr ibn Awn narrated to us, Khalid — meaning al-Wasiti — narrated to us, from Khalid — meaning al-Hadhdha — from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, from Usamah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. This objector said: Since this was Ibn Abbas's view, it is impossible he needed what you narrated from him. Our answer: Abdullah ibn Abbas held this view, then withdrew from it afterward to others' view therein."
"7170","6113 - As Yunus narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Nafi' al-Madini narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Qays, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri who said: I said to Ibn Abbas: What of what you say, two dinars for a dinar and two dirhams for a dirham; I bear witness I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Dinar for dinar, dirham for dirham, no excess between them. Ibn Abbas said: Did you hear this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? I said: Yes. He said: I did not hear this; Usamah ibn Zayd informed me of it. Abu Sa'id said: And Ibn Abbas withdrew from it. An objector said: How did Ibn Abbas withdraw from what he held, having taken it from Usamah ibn Zayd — whose place in Islam is his place — to what another narrated, when what Usamah narrated might be abrogating it? Our answer: The usury the Quran forbade with threat is usury in deferment — their trading terms in wealth for wealth — which the Quran forbade with threat. So deferment usury was excess in measured and weighed things. Ibn Abbas realized what Abu Sa'id narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was about usury other than deferment usury, so he adopted it and left what he held before, since it was about other usury."
"7171","992 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from Abdullah ibn Abbas about the cause for which His saying was revealed: There is no compulsion in religion. 6114 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Abu Bishr, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas about His saying: There is no compulsion in religion. He said: An Ansari woman whose child seldom lived would vow: if a child of hers lives, she will place him in Judaism. When Banu al-Nadir were expelled, among them were people of Ansari sons, so the Ansar said: O Messenger of Allah, our sons! So Allah the Exalted revealed: There is no compulsion in religion. Sa'id said: Whoever wished joined them, and whoever wished entered Islam."
"7172","6115 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr who said: I asked Sa'id ibn Jubayr about His saying: There is no compulsion in religion. He said: This verse was revealed about the Ansar. I said: Specifically? He said: Specifically; a woman in ignorance, if she seldom bore or lost children, would vow: if she bears a child she will place him among the Jews, seeking thereby long life. Islam came while among them were some of them. When Banu al-Nadir were expelled, they said: O Messenger of Allah, our sons and brothers are among them. He was silent about them, so Allah revealed: There is no compulsion in religion; right guidance is distinct from error. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Give your companions choice; if they choose you, they are of you, and if they choose them, they are of them. He said: So he expelled them with them. Ibn Khuzaymah did not mention Ibn Abbas in his hadith. This is a fiqh question its people dispute. One group said: Whoever of the Arabs adopts Judaism or Christianity becomes of them, with their ruling in slaughter permissibility and marriage permissibility if a woman; and that was narrated from Abdullah ibn Abbas."
"7173","As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas who said: Eat of the slaughter of Banu Taghlib and marry their women, for Allah Almighty said: O you who believed, do not take Jews and Christians as allies, some of them are allies of others, and whoever allies with them of you is of them. Among regional jurists holding this: Abu Hanifah and his companions, with no distinction when they entered, in ignorance or Islam. Others opposed them, saying: Their slaughter and women are not lawful to us; and that was narrated from Ali ibn Abi Talib — may Allah be pleased with him — and Abdullah ibn Mas'ud — may Allah be pleased with him."
"7174","As Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Abidah who said: I asked Ali — may Allah be pleased with him — about the slaughter of Arab Christians. He said: Their slaughter is not lawful, for they held of their religion only wine-drinking."
"7175","And as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abidah, from Ali — may Allah be pleased with him — the like of it."
"7176","And as Abu Qurrah Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ru'ayni narrated to us, Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, Musa ibn A'yan narrated to us, from Muslim — meaning al-Mula'i — from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i, from Alqamah, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud who said: He used to forbid the slaughter of Magians and Arab Christians, even if they mentioned Allah's name over it. In Ali's hadith is a phrase needing attention: his saying about forbidding their slaughter, that they held of their religion only wine-drinking — indicating had they held to their law's rites they would have differed, but since they held part and left part, they held nothing. That indicates his saying and Ibn Abbas's were alike therein. It was narrated from Ibn Abbas about the cause of There is no compulsion in religion what we mentioned in this chapter, with a meaning needing attention: Muslims do not differ that whoever of the disbelievers' men embraces Islam, his minor child is Muslim by his Islam — all scholars' view — but differ about the mother's Islam apart from the father's: some make it like the father's, among them Abu Hanifah, his companions, al-Shafi'i and most other scholars; some refuse, among them Malik ibn Anas. So we understood that those permitted to remain upon Judaism of Ansari sons and brothers were then disbelievers not ruled as their fathers, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left them to their Judaism. Scholars differ about an Arab who Judaizes: some say he enters that religion whenever it was, among them Abu Hanifah and companions; some say: if before the Furqan's revelation he is left, if after, prevented — among them al-Shafi'i. In Ibn Abbas's hadith the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not inquire when their entry into Judaism was — after or before the Furqan — though the Furqan was partly revealed at Mecca where he stayed ten years after, some say more, and seven years in Medina after emigrating before expelling Banu al-Nadir. Leaving the question indicates no difference whether after or before. That shows no distinction; for had they differed he would have inquired to know the reality and return each to its due. How is a disbeliever who entered disbelief taken back to another disbelief; people are only taken back to Islam from what they were upon, not from one disbelief to another. If an objector says: I do not take him thus, but say: either return to what you were upon or be notified of war — our answer: that is meaningless too, for I do not return him to what Allah called him to; since Allah did not call him to it, he must be left to what he adopted. And success is from Allah the Exalted."
"7177","993 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Go forth a scholar, or a learner, or a lover, or a listener, and do not be the fifth lest you perish, and what was narrated from Ibn Mas'ud indicating therein of his saying: And do not go forth an imma'ah in between. 6116 - al-Hasan ibn Abdullah ibn Mansur al-Balisi and Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Ja'far al-Waki'i narrated to us, Ubayd ibn Jannad al-Halabi narrated to us, Ata ibn Muslim al-Khaffaf narrated to us, Mis'ar ibn Kidam narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Go forth a scholar, or a learner, or a lover, or a listener, and do not be the fifth lest you perish. Muhammad ibn Ahmad said in his hadith: Ata said: Mis'ar ibn Kidam said: This is a fifth Allah added for us not in our hands; in our hands was only: Go forth a scholar, or a learner, or a listener, and do not be the fourth lest you perish. O Ata, woe to one in whom is none of these. It was narrated from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud of what enters this chapter of what is known he did not say by opinion or derivation, but took from one like him from whom that is taken — the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), none else."
"7178","As Yunus narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud — may Allah be pleased with him — that he used to say: Go forth a scholar, or a learner, and do not go forth an imma'ah in between. Abu Ja'far said: The imma'ah was other than the four categories in Abu Bakrah's hadith, for they are praiseworthy categories while the imma'ah is blameworthy; so it was the fifth the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spoke of in that hadith — meaning Ibn Mas'ud's hadith. Then we examined what the imma'ah is."
"7179","So we found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, Abu al-Za'ra' — meaning Amr ibn Amr, nephew of Abu al-Ahwas — narrated to me, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Mas'ud that he said: We used to call imma'ah in ignorance one invited to food who brings another with him; and among you it is one who loads his religion upon men — who lends his religion to another in what benefits that other in his world, while its sin remains upon him. We found nothing higher in explaining imma'ah than what we narrated from Ibn Mas'ud. Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz mentioned to us from Abu Ubayd about Abdullah's hadith, he said: The imma'ah is one who says: I am with people — meaning follows everyone in his opinion without firmness — so this was what we described of one who is so, with no description of what leads him to that, and the people curse them. And Allah is the Granter of success."
"7180","994 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated about the cause in which His saying was revealed: Your women are a tillage for you, so come to your tillage as you will, and what was from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at its revelation informing people of its intent. 6117 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Uways narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to me, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Abdullah ibn Umar: that a man came to his wife in her rear, and felt intense distress about that, so Allah the Exalted revealed: Your women are a tillage for you, so come to your tillage as you will."
"7181","6118 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd ibn Kasib narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Nafi' narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Sa'd, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Sa'id: that a man had intercourse with his wife in her rear, so people condemned him saying: He deflowered her unnaturally. So Allah the Exalted revealed: Your women are a tillage for you, so come to your tillage as you will. In these two hadiths is what some said they inferred permissibility of this mentioned meaning thereby, so we examined what was narrated therein other than these two hadiths."
"7182","6119 - So we found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn Abdullah: that the Jews said: Whoever comes to his wife in her front from her rear, his child comes out cross-eyed. So Allah the Exalted revealed: Your women are a tillage for you, so come to your tillage as you will."
"7183","6120 - And we found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us: that Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir narrated to him from Jabir ibn Abdullah the like of it."
"7184","6121 - And we found Abu Shurayh Muhammad ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn Abdullah the like of it."
"7185","6122 - And we found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir who said: The Jews said: When a man comes to his wife kneeling, his child comes squint-eyed. So that was mentioned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Allah Almighty revealed: Your women are a tillage for you, so come to your tillage as you will."
"7186","6123 - And we found Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: Ashhab ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, from Malik ibn Anas: Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir informed us, from Jabir ibn Abdullah that he said: The Jews said: When a man comes to his wife turned away, his child comes squint-eyed. So Allah the Exalted revealed: Your women are a tillage for you, so come to your tillage as you will."
"7187","6124 - And we found Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, he said: Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Abu Hazim, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn Abdullah that he used to say: The Jews used to say: When a woman is approached in her front from her rear then conceives, her child is cross-eyed. So Allah Almighty revealed this verse: Your women are a tillage for you, so come to your tillage as you will. What is in these reports refutes that."
"7188","6125 - And we found Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, my father narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Nu'man ibn Rashid narrate from al-Zuhri, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn Abdullah: that a Jew said: When a man marries his wife bending her forward, her child comes out cross-eyed. So Allah the Exalted revealed this verse — meaning: if you wish bending forward and if you wish otherwise, so long as in one passage."
"7189","6126 - And Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj informed me that Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir narrated to him, from Jabir ibn Abdullah: that the Jews said to the Muslims: Whoever comes to a woman turned away, her child comes squint-eyed. So Allah the Exalted revealed this verse, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Front or rear, so long as in the front passage, not to other than it. So these reports return to prohibiting intercourse with women in their rears, not permitting it. Some mentioned the verse was revealed about another meaning, and therein was mentioned."
"7190","6127 - With what al-Hasan ibn Musa al-Ashyab narrated to us, Ya'qub ibn Abdullah al-Qummi narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Abi al-Mughirah, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas that he said: Umar — may Allah be pleased with him — came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I am ruined! He said: What ruined you? He said: I turned my mount last night. He did not answer him, so Allah revealed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) this verse: Your women are a tillage for you; come front or rear, but beware the rear passage and menstruation. In this hadith the cause of revelation differs from that mentioned earlier in this chapter, with prohibition of intercourse in rears like prohibition during menstruation. This hadith revolves upon Ibn Abbas. We considered whether anything contradicting it was narrated from Ibn Abbas or not."
"7191","6128 - So we found al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'ah informed us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib: that Amir ibn Yahya al-Ma'afiri narrated to him that Hanash ibn Abdullah al-Saba'i narrated to him that he heard Ibn Abbas say: People of Himyar came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asking about women, so Allah revealed: Your women are a tillage for you, so come to your tillage as you will. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Come to her front or rear so long as in the front passage. In this hadith the cause of revelation differs from the cause previously narrated from Ibn Abbas in this chapter, with prohibition of approaching women other than in their front passages."
"7192","And we found Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Abu Ishaq, from Za'idah ibn Umayr al-Ta'i who said: I asked Ibn Abbas about coitus interruptus, and he said: You have asked much; if the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said anything therein, it is as he said, and if he did not say, then I say therein: Your women are a tillage for you, so come to your tillage as you will; if you wish practice withdrawal, if you wish do not — whichever you do, no harm. So this Ibn Abbas interpreted the verse contrary to what was narrated from him of its revelation cause. Then we considered: was anything narrated about its revelation from other than Ibn Abbas and those we mentioned besides him?"
"7193","So we found Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Zakariyya ibn Yahya, scribe of al-Umari, narrated to us, al-Mufaddal ibn Fadalah narrated to us, from Abdullah, from Ka'b ibn Alqamah, from Abu al-Nadr that he informed him: that he said to Nafi', freedman of Abdullah ibn Umar: Much has been said against you that you say from Ibn Umar that he ruled women may be approached in their rears. Nafi' said: They lied against me; I will tell you how it was. Ibn Umar once recited the mushaf while I was with him until he reached His saying: Your women are a tillage. He said: O Nafi', do you know about this verse? I said: No. He said: We Quraysh used to bend women forward; when we entered Medina and married Ansari women, we wanted of them what we want, but they disliked and deemed it grave. Ansari women had adopted the Jews' way — only approached lying on sides. So Allah revealed: Your women are a tillage for you, so come to your tillage as you will. In this hadith from Ibn Umar is that the verse was revealed for the mentioned meaning, not for permitting intercourse in rears. An objector said: It was narrated from Ibn Umar permitting it, and mentioned."
"7194","What Abu Qurrah Muhammad ibn Humayd al-Ru'ayni narrated to us, Asbagh ibn al-Faraj and Abu Zayd ibn Abi al-Ghamr narrated to us, they said: Ibn al-Qasim said: And Malik narrated to me, he said: Rabi'ah ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from Abu al-Hubab Sa'id ibn Yasar: that he asked Ibn Umar about it — meaning intercourse in rears — and he said: No harm. Our answer: Something contradicting this was narrated from Ibn Umar via Sa'id ibn Yasar."
"7195","As al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from al-Harith ibn Ya'qub, from Sa'id ibn Yasar Abu al-Hubab who said: I said to Ibn Umar: What do you say about slave-girls, may I prepare them? He said: What is preparing? I mentioned the rear. He said: Does any Muslim do that?! So this Ibn Umar has the opposite narrated from him to what you mentioned; since so, it is as if nothing was narrated from him therein. And Maymun ibn Mihran said therein."
"7196","What Fahd ibn Sulayman and Ishaq ibn Muhammad ibn Ma'mar narrated to us, they said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Maymun ibn Mihran — mentioned to him what was told from Nafi' of permitting intercourse in rears — and he said: Nafi' said that only after he aged and lost his mind. And it was narrated from Salim denying that of Ibn Umar."
"7197","As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Attaf ibn Khalid informed us, from Musa ibn Abdullah ibn al-Hasan: that his father asked Salim ibn Abdullah to narrate Nafi's hadith from Ibn Umar that he saw no harm in approaching women in their rears. Salim said: The slave lied — or said: erred. He only said: No harm to approach them in their front passages from their rears. Then we examined the revelation cause whether anything was narrated therein from other than those we mentioned or not."
"7198","6129 - So we found Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Salamah Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us, Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Sabit who said: I came to Hafsah bint Abd al-Rahman and said: I want to ask you something, though shy of you. She said: Ask, O son of my brother, whatever occurs. I said: About approaching women in their rears. She said: Umm Salamah narrated to me that the Ansar did not bend forward, while the Emigrants did; the Jews said: Whoever bends, his child comes out cross-eyed. When the Emigrants came to Medina and married Ansari women, a man of the Emigrants married an Ansari woman and bent her, but she refused. She came to Umm Salamah and mentioned that; when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered, Umm Salamah mentioned it to him, and the Ansari woman was shy and went out. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Call her. She called her, and he said: Your women are a tillage for you, so come to your tillage as you will — in one passage. What is in this hadith: what was permitted them by this verse returns to that passage, not to other than it. Then we considered: was anything else narrated in this chapter?"
"7199","6130 - So we found Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hakim al-Athram, from Abu Tamimah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever comes to a menstruating woman, or a woman in her rear, or comes to a soothsayer, has disbelieved in what was sent down to Muhammad."
"7200","6131 - And as Yunus narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn al-Had, from Umarah ibn Khuzaymah ibn Thabit, from his father: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Indeed Allah is not shy of truth; do not come to women in their rears."
